window.LEARN_DATA = {"config":{"label":"Exam 5","key":"e5-learn-v1","map":"Exam 5 Map.html","test":"Exam 5 Map.html#test","mhPrefix":"e5-","scope":"Weeks 13–15: psychosis, somatic/dissociative disorders, children, eating, substances, neurocognitive and personality disorders. Every currently authored topic and condition is included.","weightNote":"No professor question-count blueprint is encoded here; recommendations balance chapter coverage.","page":"Exam 5 Learn.html","chapters":["3","11","12","16","17","18","22","23","24"]},"weights":{"3":1,"11":1,"12":1,"16":1,"17":1,"18":1,"22":1,"23":1,"24":1},"lessons":[{"id":"t-spectrum","ch":"12","chapters":["12"],"title":"The schizophrenia spectrum","notes":["t-spectrum"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 12; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Psychosis means altered perception or thinking and an impaired ability to tell what is real (reality testing). Schizophrenia affects about 1% of people, usually starting 15–25 in men and 25–35 in women. Its symptoms fall into four groups: positive, negative, cognitive and affective. Positive symptoms respond best to medication; negative and cognitive symptoms are more disabling.","Types of delusions: Persecutory: “They’re poisoning my food.”; Referential (ideas of reference): “The radio is sending me messages.”; Grandiose: “I’m a famous playwright.”; Erotomanic: “He secretly loves me.”; Nihilistic: “A comet will end everything; I’m giving my things away.”; Somatic: “My heart is rotting away.”; Control: “Aliens control my thoughts.” (Thought insertion and thought broadcasting are related.); Beliefs that come from one’s culture or religion are not delusions.","Perception words: Hallucinations: auditory (most common, about 70%), visual, olfactory, gustatory, tactile (the last three suggest a medical or substance cause).; Illusion: misreading something real (a coat looks like a bear).; Depersonalization (detached from self) and derealization (the world seems unreal).","Assess the individual’s actual symptoms, intent, access to means, substance use and protective factors. Most people with schizophrenia are not violent; a diagnosis alone is not a violence prediction."],"checks":["Two required elements for a schizophrenia diagnosis besides duration?","Psychosis for 3 weeks, then full recovery?","“The TV anchor is talking about me.” Which delusion?","Inability to recognize one’s own illness, caused by the illness?"],"recallAnswers":["At least 2 symptoms, one of which must be delusions, hallucinations or disorganized speech; functioning below baseline; 6 months.","Brief psychotic disorder (more than 1 day, less than 1 month).","Referential.","Anosognosia."],"requires":[],"terms":["Positive symptoms","Negative symptoms","Cognitive symptoms","Affective symptoms","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Positive symptoms":"Hallucinations, delusions, paranoia, disorganized speech and behavior. Things added that shouldn’t be there; linked to excess dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway. Usually what brings the person to care; respond best to antipsychotics.","Negative symptoms":"Anhedonia, avolition, asociality, affective blunting, apathy, alogia (the six A’s). Normal functions taken away; linked to low frontal-lobe activity. Harder to treat; they cause poor hygiene and isolation (not laziness).","Cognitive symptoms":"Poor memory and attention, concrete thinking, poor judgment and planning (executive function), anosognosia. Frontal cortex and hippocampus changes. Use repetition, visual cues and concrete examples; break tasks into steps.","Affective symptoms":"Labile, incongruent or inappropriate mood; depression. Emotional regulation is affected, plus the stress of a chronic illness. Depression raises suicide risk (5–10% die by suicide)."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-sz-9","sourceId":"q5-sz-9","q":"Asked what brought him to the hospital, a patient answers, “An ambulance.” This is an example of:","o":["Clang association","Concrete thinking","Neologism","Echolalia"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Concrete thinking: a literal interpretation instead of an abstract one.","Supported. Concrete thinking: a literal interpretation instead of an abstract one.","Not supported for this question. Concrete thinking: a literal interpretation instead of an abstract one.","Not supported for this question. Concrete thinking: a literal interpretation instead of an abstract one."],"phys":"Concrete thinking: a literal interpretation instead of an abstract one.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-sz-10","sourceId":"q5-sz-10","q":"A patient stands by the window “reading messages in the clouds” and shouts “Stay out of my head!” Which delusions are likely? Select all that apply.","o":["Persecutory","Referential","Somatic","Erotomanic","Control"],"a":[0,1,4],"r":["Supported. Messages in the clouds = referential; “stay out of my head” = control and persecutory fear.","Supported. Messages in the clouds = referential; “stay out of my head” = control and persecutory fear.","Not supported for this question. Messages in the clouds = referential; “stay out of my head” = control and persecutory fear.","Not supported for this question. Messages in the clouds = referential; “stay out of my head” = control and persecutory fear.","Supported. Messages in the clouds = referential; “stay out of my head” = control and persecutory fear."],"phys":"Messages in the clouds = referential; “stay out of my head” = control and persecutory fear.","ex":[]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Assess the individual’s actual symptoms, intent, access to means, substance use and protective factors. Most people with schizophrenia are not violent; a diagnosis alone is not a violence prediction.","studio":"t-spectrum"},{"id":"t-antipsychotics","ch":"3","chapters":["3","12"],"title":"Antipsychotic medications","notes":["t-antipsychotics"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapters 3 and 12; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["All antipsychotics block dopamine D2 receptors (second-generation drugs also block serotonin 5-HT2A). Blocking D2 in the mesolimbic pathway treats psychosis; blocking it in the nigrostriatal pathway causes EPS, and in the tuberoinfundibular pathway raises prolactin. Blocking H1, M1 and α1 adds sedation and weight gain, anticholinergic effects and orthostasis.","First vs second generation: First (typical): haloperidol, fluphenazine, chlorpromazine, perphenazine. Positive symptoms; high EPS and TD; cheap.; Second (atypical): risperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, ziprasidone, aripiprazole, lurasidone, clozapine. Positive + some negative symptoms; less EPS; metabolic syndrome.; Partial D2 agonists (aripiprazole, brexpiprazole, cariprazine): low prolactin.; Onset 2–6 weeks; taper off; not addictive; rarely lethal in overdose.","EPS by timing: Acute dystonia (hours to days): muscle spasms of the neck, eyes or tongue → benztropine or diphenhydramine IM; watch the airway.; Akathisia (days to weeks): restlessness → propranolol or a benzodiazepine; don’t mistake it for anxiety.; Pseudoparkinsonism (weeks): tremor, shuffling, mask face → benztropine or trihexyphenidyl.; Tardive dyskinesia (months to years): lip smacking, tongue writhing; often permanent → AIMS every 3 months; valbenazine or deutetrabenazine.","FDA removed the clozapine REMS effective June 13, 2025. Severe neutropenia remains a risk: continue ANC monitoring per the current prescribing information and promptly assess signs of infection. REMS removal did not remove the monitoring need."],"checks":["Which pathway causes EPS when D2 is blocked?","Tongue writhing after 3 years on haloperidol?","Rigidity, temperature 104 °F, confusion on day 5 of haloperidol: first action?","ANC schedule for clozapine?"],"recallAnswers":["Nigrostriatal.","Tardive dyskinesia: AIMS, valbenazine or deutetrabenazine; often permanent.","Hold the antipsychotic and call the prescriber stat (NMS).","Weekly × 6 months, every 2 weeks × 6 months, then monthly."],"requires":[],"terms":["clozapine","olanzapine","quetiapine","aripiprazole","benztropine","dantrolene","haloperidol","risperidone","paliperidone","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{},"questions":[{"id":"q5-ap-3","sourceId":"q5-ap-3","q":"Which second-generation antipsychotic requires routine absolute neutrophil count monitoring?","o":["Brexpiprazole","Clozapine","Risperidone","Ziprasidone"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Clozapine can cause severe neutropenia: ANC weekly × 6 months, every 2 weeks × 6 months, then monthly.","Supported. Clozapine can cause severe neutropenia: ANC weekly × 6 months, every 2 weeks × 6 months, then monthly.","Not supported for this question. Clozapine can cause severe neutropenia: ANC weekly × 6 months, every 2 weeks × 6 months, then monthly.","Not supported for this question. Clozapine can cause severe neutropenia: ANC weekly × 6 months, every 2 weeks × 6 months, then monthly."],"phys":"Clozapine can cause severe neutropenia: ANC weekly × 6 months, every 2 weeks × 6 months, then monthly.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ap-4","sourceId":"q5-ap-4","q":"A patient on long-term fluphenazine has lip smacking and tongue protrusion. The nurse recognizes:","o":["Acute dystonia","Akathisia","Tardive dyskinesia","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Tardive dyskinesia: late, often permanent; screen with the AIMS; treat with valbenazine or deutetrabenazine.","Not supported for this question. Tardive dyskinesia: late, often permanent; screen with the AIMS; treat with valbenazine or deutetrabenazine.","Supported. Tardive dyskinesia: late, often permanent; screen with the AIMS; treat with valbenazine or deutetrabenazine.","Not supported for this question. Tardive dyskinesia: late, often permanent; screen with the AIMS; treat with valbenazine or deutetrabenazine."],"phys":"Tardive dyskinesia: late, often permanent; screen with the AIMS; treat with valbenazine or deutetrabenazine.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ap-5","sourceId":"q5-ap-5","q":"A patient on chlorpromazine and benztropine has hot dry skin, dilated pupils, urinary retention and new delirium. The nurse suspects:","o":["Serotonin syndrome","Anticholinergic toxicity","Lithium toxicity","Hypoglycemia"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Stacked anticholinergic drugs → anticholinergic toxicity; hold medications, cool; physostigmine.","Supported. Stacked anticholinergic drugs → anticholinergic toxicity; hold medications, cool; physostigmine.","Not supported for this question. Stacked anticholinergic drugs → anticholinergic toxicity; hold medications, cool; physostigmine.","Not supported for this question. Stacked anticholinergic drugs → anticholinergic toxicity; hold medications, cool; physostigmine."],"phys":"Stacked anticholinergic drugs → anticholinergic toxicity; hold medications, cool; physostigmine.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ap-6","sourceId":"q5-ap-6","q":"A patient taking clozapine reports a sore throat and fever. What is the nurse’s priority action?","o":["Give acetaminophen and recheck tomorrow","Hold the clozapine and notify the prescriber; obtain an ANC","Encourage fluids and rest","Increase the dose to prevent relapse"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Signs of infection on clozapine may mean neutropenia.","Supported. Signs of infection on clozapine may mean neutropenia.","Not supported for this question. Signs of infection on clozapine may mean neutropenia.","Not supported for this question. Signs of infection on clozapine may mean neutropenia."],"phys":"Signs of infection on clozapine may mean neutropenia.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ap-7","sourceId":"q5-ap-7","q":"A patient taking clozapine tells the nurse he quit smoking last week. What should the nurse anticipate?","o":["His clozapine level may rise, so watch for toxicity","His clozapine level will fall","No change is expected","He will need nicotine replacement for the clozapine to work"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Smoking speeds clozapine metabolism (CYP1A2); quitting raises the level, and the dose may need lowering.","Not supported for this question. Smoking speeds clozapine metabolism (CYP1A2); quitting raises the level, and the dose may need lowering.","Not supported for this question. Smoking speeds clozapine metabolism (CYP1A2); quitting raises the level, and the dose may need lowering.","Not supported for this question. Smoking speeds clozapine metabolism (CYP1A2); quitting raises the level, and the dose may need lowering."],"phys":"Smoking speeds clozapine metabolism (CYP1A2); quitting raises the level, and the dose may need lowering.","ex":[]}],"clinicalUpdate":"FDA removed the clozapine REMS effective June 13, 2025. Severe neutropenia remains a risk: continue ANC monitoring per the current prescribing information and promptly assess signs of infection. REMS removal did not remove the monitoring need.","studio":"t-antipsychotics"},{"id":"t-kids","ch":"11","chapters":["11"],"title":"Children and neurodevelopmental disorders","notes":["t-kids"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 11; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["About 1 in 5 children has a mental disorder in a given year, and half of all lifetime cases start by age 14. Young children are harder to diagnose (limited language, fast development), so the nurse gathers data from many sources (parents, teachers, the child) and uses play to assess and treat. Behavior is managed with the least restrictive intervention first.","Assessing a child: History of the present illness, developmental history (milestones, play, attachment), medical and family history.; Mental status adapted to age, including characteristics of play; developmental screening (Denver II: social/personal, fine motor, language, gross motor).; Use therapeutic games, drawing and puppets for young children; a genogram for the family.; Screen for abuse and neglect: nurses are mandated reporters (neglect is the most common abuse).","Interventions: Behavioral point systems; play therapy (feelings and trauma acted out with toys); bibliotherapy (stories); expressive arts, journaling, music; family therapy; CBT; groups.; Least → most restrictive: talk about it → offer PRN medication → time-out (the child can leave) → quiet or sensory room → seclusion → restraint.; Seclusion and restraint: constant monitoring, notify the parents, release as soon as safe, debrief with the child and the staff."],"checks":["Best-structured limit-setting statement?","Best activity for a child with ADHD?","Least restrictive intervention?","Tourette criteria?"],"recallAnswers":["“Hitting me when you are angry is unacceptable.” (Specific, calm, not judgmental.)","A physical one with quick feedback, like tennis.","Talking about the behavior and its consequences, then PRN medication, then time-out, then seclusion, then restraint.","Multiple motor and at least one vocal tic for more than 1 year, before age 18."],"requires":[],"terms":["Resilience","Temperament","aripiprazole","haloperidol"],"termDefinitions":{"Resilience":"A neglected child who bonds with a kind neighbor, loves school and earns good grades. Adaptability, a nurturing adult outside the family, distance from family chaos, social skills, problem-solving and a sense of the future protect the child. Ask about supports, coping and future plans; build these strengths.","Temperament":"An easygoing vs an intense, shy or irritable baby. An inborn style of reacting; a poor fit with a parent’s temperament risks insecure attachment. Help parents understand and adapt to the child’s style."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-ch-3","sourceId":"q5-ch-3","q":"Which questions assess a teenager’s resilience? Select all that apply.","o":["“How did you cope when your father was deployed for a year?”","“Who did you go to for advice while he was away?”","“How do you feel about talking to a counselor?”","“Where do you see yourself in 10 years?”","“Do you like your school?”"],"a":[0,1,3],"r":["Supported. Resilience includes coping, supportive adults and a sense of the future.","Supported. Resilience includes coping, supportive adults and a sense of the future.","Not supported for this question. Resilience includes coping, supportive adults and a sense of the future.","Supported. Resilience includes coping, supportive adults and a sense of the future.","Not supported for this question. Resilience includes coping, supportive adults and a sense of the future."],"phys":"Resilience includes coping, supportive adults and a sense of the future.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ch-4","sourceId":"q5-ch-4","q":"Which factors make it harder to diagnose mental illness in young children? Select all that apply.","o":["Limited language skills","Level of cognitive development","Level of emotional development","Parental denial","The severity of the illnesses"],"a":[0,1,2],"r":["Supported. Young children can’t yet describe their experiences, and they develop rapidly.","Supported. Young children can’t yet describe their experiences, and they develop rapidly.","Supported. Young children can’t yet describe their experiences, and they develop rapidly.","Not supported for this question. Young children can’t yet describe their experiences, and they develop rapidly.","Not supported for this question. Young children can’t yet describe their experiences, and they develop rapidly."],"phys":"Young children can’t yet describe their experiences, and they develop rapidly.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ch-5","sourceId":"q5-ch-5","q":"A 10-year-old removed from her home for neglect calls the neighbor her “nice mom,” loves school and earns good grades. This best reflects:","o":["Temperament","Genetic factors","Resilience","Paradoxical effects of neglect"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. A nurturing adult outside the family plus school success = resilience.","Not supported for this question. A nurturing adult outside the family plus school success = resilience.","Supported. A nurturing adult outside the family plus school success = resilience.","Not supported for this question. A nurturing adult outside the family plus school success = resilience."],"phys":"A nurturing adult outside the family plus school success = resilience.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ch-6","sourceId":"q5-ch-6","q":"A mother reports she uses time-out up to 20 times a day, and her daughter is losing control on the unit. The nurse recognizes that:","o":["Time-out is an important part of her discipline","Time-out is no longer an effective therapeutic measure","The child acts out to get alone time","Time-out should be replaced with seclusion"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Overused, time-out loses its effect; seclusion is far more restrictive.","Supported. Overused, time-out loses its effect; seclusion is far more restrictive.","Not supported for this question. Overused, time-out loses its effect; seclusion is far more restrictive.","Not supported for this question. Overused, time-out loses its effect; seclusion is far more restrictive."],"phys":"Overused, time-out loses its effect; seclusion is far more restrictive.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ch-10","sourceId":"q5-ch-10","q":"A child has multiple motor tics and a vocal tic that have lasted 18 months. The nurse recognizes:","o":["Provisional tic disorder","Persistent motor tic disorder","Tourette disorder","Stereotypic movement disorder"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Tourette: multiple motor plus at least one vocal tic for more than 1 year.","Not supported for this question. Tourette: multiple motor plus at least one vocal tic for more than 1 year.","Supported. Tourette: multiple motor plus at least one vocal tic for more than 1 year.","Not supported for this question. Tourette: multiple motor plus at least one vocal tic for more than 1 year."],"phys":"Tourette: multiple motor plus at least one vocal tic for more than 1 year.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-kids"},{"id":"t-attach","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Childhood trauma and attachment","notes":["t-attach"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 16; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Children depend on caregivers, and the developing brain changes with repeated stress (the HPA axis, amygdala and hippocampus), so early trauma and neglect can shape lifelong health. Treatment is phased: safety and stabilization, then calming arousal and working with memories, then catching up on skills. Relationships heal.","Attachment disorders (severe early neglect): Reactive attachment disorder: inhibited and withdrawn; rarely seeks or accepts comfort.; Disinhibited social engagement disorder: no fear of strangers; overly friendly; would go off with a stranger.; Risks: institutions, frequent foster-home changes, impaired parents, long separations.; Treat the child and the caregiver; a safe, stable home.","PTSD in children: Repetitive play reenacting the trauma; nightmares; regression; irritability, aggression; self-blame; withdrawal.; Imaginary friends can be a coping mechanism after trauma.; Interventions: trust and safety, developmentally appropriate words, relaxation before exploring the trauma, art and play, involve parents (unless they caused the trauma), mandatory reporting.; Treatments: CBT and EMDR (first line); medications only for specific symptoms."],"checks":["A child at the mall asks a stranger, “Maybe you’ll take me home?”","An 8-year-old repeatedly “arrests” and scares classmates in play.","Widow at 15 months with yearning, emptiness and anger?"],"recallAnswers":["Possible disinhibited social engagement disorder (a lack of early bonding).","A possible sign of traumatization (reenactment).","Adjustment disorder (complicated grief)."],"requires":[],"terms":["sertraline","paroxetine"],"termDefinitions":{},"questions":[{"id":"q5-at-2","sourceId":"q5-at-2","q":"A foster child has an imaginary friend. Which statement by the foster mother shows effective teaching?","o":["“Imaginary friends are abnormal.”","“Imaginary friends are maladaptive.”","“Her imaginary friend is a coping mechanism.”","“We should tell her imaginary friends aren’t allowed.”"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. After trauma, an imaginary friend can be an adaptive coping mechanism.","Not supported for this question. After trauma, an imaginary friend can be an adaptive coping mechanism.","Supported. After trauma, an imaginary friend can be an adaptive coping mechanism.","Not supported for this question. After trauma, an imaginary friend can be an adaptive coping mechanism."],"phys":"After trauma, an imaginary friend can be an adaptive coping mechanism.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-at-3","sourceId":"q5-at-3","q":"Which outcome is most appropriate for an 8-year-old with reactive attachment disorder?","o":["Increases self-control and decreases impulsivity","Avoids situations that trigger conflict","Expresses complex thoughts","Writes or draws feelings in a journal"],"a":[3],"r":["Not supported for this question. Expressing feelings through drawing or writing fits his age and builds emotional connection.","Not supported for this question. Expressing feelings through drawing or writing fits his age and builds emotional connection.","Not supported for this question. Expressing feelings through drawing or writing fits his age and builds emotional connection.","Supported. Expressing feelings through drawing or writing fits his age and builds emotional connection."],"phys":"Expressing feelings through drawing or writing fits his age and builds emotional connection.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-attach"},{"id":"t-somafact","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Somatic, factitious and malingering","notes":["t-somafact"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 17; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Somatization is distress expressed as physical symptoms. In somatic symptom, illness anxiety and conversion disorders the symptoms are not under conscious control and are real to the patient. In factitious disorder and malingering they are consciously produced, for the sick role or for an outside reward.","The spectrum: Somatic symptom disorder: real symptoms + excessive worry, more than 6 months; may be “with predominant pain.”; Illness anxiety disorder: fear of illness; few symptoms; care-seeking or care-avoidant.; Conversion: neurological loss; la belle indifférence; treat with reassurance, PT, CBT, hypnosis; don’t confront.; Psychological factors affecting a medical condition: stress, depression and loneliness worsening a real disease.; Factitious: consciously fakes or induces illness for the sick role; travels between hospitals; scars from many surgeries.; Factitious imposed on another: a caregiver induces illness in a dependent → report and protect the child.; Malingering: consciously fakes for external gain (disability money, drugs, avoiding duty).","Risk and protective factors: ACEs and childhood abuse; stress; anxiety and depression; loneliness (as harmful as smoking 15 cigarettes a day).; Protective: resilient coping, self-compassion, an internal locus of control, social connection."],"checks":["Conscious faking to be a patient, with no outside reward?","Faking back pain to get disability money?","Sudden paralysis with calm unconcern?","Best outcome for impaired coping in somatic disorder?"],"recallAnswers":["Factitious disorder.","Malingering.","Conversion disorder (la belle indifférence).","“Calls a friend to talk when feeling lonely.”"],"requires":[],"terms":[],"termDefinitions":{},"questions":[{"id":"q5-so-5","sourceId":"q5-so-5","q":"A patient with negative test results is reluctant to be discharged and now reports new back and abdominal pain. The nurse suspects factitious disorder, meaning the patient is:","o":["Consciously trying to keep the sick role","Unaware of unmet needs","Protecting a child from illness","Recognizing symptoms as a coping mechanism"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Factitious disorder = conscious production of symptoms in order to be a patient.","Not supported for this question. Factitious disorder = conscious production of symptoms in order to be a patient.","Not supported for this question. Factitious disorder = conscious production of symptoms in order to be a patient.","Not supported for this question. Factitious disorder = conscious production of symptoms in order to be a patient."],"phys":"Factitious disorder = conscious production of symptoms in order to be a patient.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-so-6","sourceId":"q5-so-6","q":"Which situation describes malingering?","o":["A patient worried for months about having cancer despite normal tests","A man exaggerating back pain only while his disability claim is pending","A woman injecting herself with bacteria to be admitted","Sudden paralysis after a traumatic event"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Malingering = conscious faking for an external reward.","Supported. Malingering = conscious faking for an external reward.","Not supported for this question. Malingering = conscious faking for an external reward.","Not supported for this question. Malingering = conscious faking for an external reward."],"phys":"Malingering = conscious faking for an external reward.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-somafact"},{"id":"t-feeding","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Eating and feeding disorders","notes":["t-feeding"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 18; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Eating disorders have the highest death rate of the psychiatric disorders. They lie on a spectrum and can shift (restriction → binge → binge-purge). They are about 50–60% heritable, involve changes in the brain’s reward and body-signal circuits, and are fed by thin-ideal culture and trauma. Medical stability comes first.","Compare the three: Anorexia: low weight, fear of gain, distorted image; restricting or binge/purge type; often denies a problem (ego-syntonic).; Bulimia: binge + compensating at least weekly for 3 months; normal weight; ashamed and wants help.; Binge-eating disorder: binges with distress and no compensating; often obese; the most common eating disorder.","Hospitalize when…: Anorexia: under 75% of ideal weight, under 10% body fat, HR under 50, systolic under 90, temperature under 96 °F, arrhythmias, severe electrolyte imbalance, suicide risk.; Bulimia: syncope, K⁺ under 3.2, Cl⁻ under 88, esophageal tears, arrhythmias, intractable vomiting, hematemesis, suicide risk."],"checks":["“I’m ashamed, but I eat my hair.”","Lab danger in bulimia?","HR 46 in anorexia: what does it mean?","Refeeding syndrome lab to watch?"],"recallAnswers":["Pica.","Hypokalemia (and arrhythmias).","Meets the hospitalization criterion (HR under 50).","Phosphate (also K⁺ and Mg²⁺)."],"requires":[],"terms":["bupropion","fluoxetine"],"termDefinitions":{},"questions":[{"id":"q5-ea-9","sourceId":"q5-ea-9","q":"Which is the best treatment option for a person with binge-eating disorder?","o":["Bariatric surgery","Coping strategies","Avoiding eating in public","Appetite suppressants"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Binge eating is about coping with emotion; therapy and coping skills come first.","Supported. Binge eating is about coping with emotion; therapy and coping skills come first.","Not supported for this question. Binge eating is about coping with emotion; therapy and coping skills come first.","Not supported for this question. Binge eating is about coping with emotion; therapy and coping skills come first."],"phys":"Binge eating is about coping with emotion; therapy and coping skills come first.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ea-10","sourceId":"q5-ea-10","q":"Which drug is FDA approved for moderate to severe binge-eating disorder in adults?","o":["Fluoxetine","Lisdexamfetamine","Topiramate","Naltrexone"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Lisdexamfetamine (Vyvanse); watch for misuse, insomnia and ↑ HR.","Supported. Lisdexamfetamine (Vyvanse); watch for misuse, insomnia and ↑ HR.","Not supported for this question. Lisdexamfetamine (Vyvanse); watch for misuse, insomnia and ↑ HR.","Not supported for this question. Lisdexamfetamine (Vyvanse); watch for misuse, insomnia and ↑ HR."],"phys":"Lisdexamfetamine (Vyvanse); watch for misuse, insomnia and ↑ HR.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-feeding"},{"id":"t-substances","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Substance use and addiction","notes":["t-substances"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 22; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Addiction is a chronic, relapsing brain disease: craving, seeking and using despite harm. Drugs hijack the dopamine reward pathway (ventral tegmental area), plus the opioid and GABA systems. Know four words: addiction, intoxication (being under the influence), tolerance (needing more for the same effect) and withdrawal (the substance-specific syndrome when stopping). Process addictions like gambling use the same reward circuits.","Intoxication vs withdrawal, by substance: Alcohol: slurred speech, poor coordination → withdrawal: tremor (6–8 h), seizures (12–24 h), DTs (within 72 h). Dangerous.; Sedatives and benzodiazepines: slurred speech, ataxia, respiratory depression with alcohol or opioids → withdrawal: anxiety, tremor, insomnia, seizures. Dangerous: taper.; Opioids: pinpoint pupils, sedation, low RR → withdrawal: dilated pupils, yawning, tearing, runny nose, gooseflesh, cramps, diarrhea. Miserable but rarely deadly.; Stimulants (cocaine, amphetamines): euphoria, ↑ HR and BP, dilated pupils, chest pain, paranoia, “cocaine bugs” → withdrawal (“crash”): fatigue, hypersomnia, hunger, depression and suicidal thoughts.; Cannabis: red eyes, hunger, dry mouth, tachycardia, slowed time → withdrawal (after about a week): irritability, insomnia, poor appetite.; Hallucinogens (LSD): perceptual changes, synesthesia, dilated pupils → “talk them down”; flashbacks (hallucinogen persisting perception disorder).; PCP: violence, nystagmus, high BP, numbness to pain, hyperthermia → can’t be talked down; low stimulation, benzodiazepine, restraint if needed.; Inhalants (glue, aerosols, gasoline): disinhibition, nystagmus → “sudden sniffing death” (arrhythmia); mostly teens.; Tobacco: withdrawal: irritability, anxiety, poor concentration, insomnia, hunger. Treat with nicotine replacement, bupropion, varenicline.; Caffeine: over 250 mg → restlessness, tachycardia, GI upset; overdose (energy drinks) → arrhythmia, seizures; withdrawal headache within 12–24 h.","Screening and assessment: SBIRT: screening, brief intervention, referral to treatment.; CAGE (2 or more = significant), CAGE-AID (adds drugs), AUDIT, T-ACE, CIWA-Ar for alcohol withdrawal, urine drug screens.; Assess co-occurring mental illness, trauma history, family history, strengths and readiness to change.; Look at your own attitudes: addiction is a disease, not a choice."],"checks":["Pinpoint pupils + coma + RR 6?","Which two withdrawals can be fatal?","When do alcohol withdrawal seizures occur?","Disulfiram + alcohol causes?"],"recallAnswers":["Opioid overdose: airway, naloxone, watch for re-sedation.","Alcohol and benzodiazepine/sedative withdrawal (seizures, DTs).","12–24 hours after the last drink.","Nausea and vomiting, flushing, sweating, headache, dyspnea, confusion."],"requires":[],"terms":["bupropion","naloxone","naltrexone","buprenorphine","disulfiram"],"termDefinitions":{},"questions":[{"id":"q5-su-3","sourceId":"q5-su-3","q":"Which characteristics describe the nursing diagnosis of denial in substance use? Select all that apply.","o":["Reports inability to cope","Does not perceive the danger of use","Minimizes symptoms","Refuses healthcare attention","Unable to admit the impact on life patterns"],"a":[1,2,3,4],"r":["Not supported for this question. Denial = not seeing or admitting the problem. “Reports inability to cope” fits impaired coping.","Supported. Denial = not seeing or admitting the problem. “Reports inability to cope” fits impaired coping.","Supported. Denial = not seeing or admitting the problem. “Reports inability to cope” fits impaired coping.","Supported. Denial = not seeing or admitting the problem. “Reports inability to cope” fits impaired coping.","Supported. Denial = not seeing or admitting the problem. “Reports inability to cope” fits impaired coping."],"phys":"Denial = not seeing or admitting the problem. “Reports inability to cope” fits impaired coping.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-su-6","sourceId":"q5-su-6","q":"Which statement shows progress for a patient in treatment for gambling disorder?","o":["“I’m a bad person for draining our savings.”","“Gambling activates the reward pathways in my brain.”","“Gambling is the only thing that makes me feel alive.”","“We’ve always enjoyed it; I don’t know why she’s upset.”"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Understanding the disease model without shame shows insight.","Supported. Understanding the disease model without shame shows insight.","Not supported for this question. Understanding the disease model without shame shows insight.","Not supported for this question. Understanding the disease model without shame shows insight."],"phys":"Understanding the disease model without shame shows insight.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-su-7","sourceId":"q5-su-7","q":"Opioid use disorder is characterized by:","o":["No withdrawal symptoms","Dilated pupils and agitation during intoxication","Tolerance","Needing less drug for the same high over time"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Tolerance: more drug is needed for the same effect. Intoxication causes pinpoint pupils.","Not supported for this question. Tolerance: more drug is needed for the same effect. Intoxication causes pinpoint pupils.","Supported. Tolerance: more drug is needed for the same effect. Intoxication causes pinpoint pupils.","Not supported for this question. Tolerance: more drug is needed for the same effect. Intoxication causes pinpoint pupils."],"phys":"Tolerance: more drug is needed for the same effect. Intoxication causes pinpoint pupils.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-su-8","sourceId":"q5-su-8","q":"A man who worked 36 hours straight drank 8 double espressos and 12 caffeinated sodas and says he feels like he’s having a stroke. The nurse suspects:","o":["Fluid overload","Dehydration and caffeine overdose","Benzodiazepine overdose","Sleep deprivation syndrome"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Massive caffeine (a diuretic) without food → caffeine overdose and dehydration.","Supported. Massive caffeine (a diuretic) without food → caffeine overdose and dehydration.","Not supported for this question. Massive caffeine (a diuretic) without food → caffeine overdose and dehydration.","Not supported for this question. Massive caffeine (a diuretic) without food → caffeine overdose and dehydration."],"phys":"Massive caffeine (a diuretic) without food → caffeine overdose and dehydration.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-substances"},{"id":"t-neurocog","ch":"23","chapters":["23"],"title":"Neurocognitive disorders","notes":["t-neurocog"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 23; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Delirium affects lower-level cognition (attention, orientation) and is acute and usually reversible. Mild and major neurocognitive disorders (major = dementia) affect higher cognition (memory, language, executive function, social cognition); dementia is progressive and irreversible. Alzheimer’s disease causes 60–80% of dementias.","Compare: Onset: delirium sudden (hours to days) · dementia slow (months to years) · depression gradual, worse with crisis.; Course: delirium fluctuates, worse at night · dementia progressive (sundowning) · depression worse in the morning.; Attention: impaired in delirium · intact early in dementia · poor concentration in depression.; Cause: delirium = medical illness, drugs, withdrawal · dementia = Alzheimer’s, vascular, Lewy body, etc. · depression = losses, illness.; Reversible? Delirium yes · dementia no · depression yes.; An older adult with new confusion → think delirium first; a grieving older adult with poor concentration → depression (pseudodementia).","Types of dementia: Alzheimer’s (60–80%): memory first.; Vascular (5–10%): stepwise after strokes; impaired judgment, slow gait.; Frontotemporal: onset 45–60; personality change and disinhibition first.; Lewy body: Alzheimer’s-like plus visual hallucinations, movement problems, sleep disorders (very sensitive to antipsychotics).; Parkinson’s disease dementia; mixed causes are common."],"checks":["“She was fine last night, but this morning she’s confused.”","An 85-year-old suddenly disheveled, incoherent, smelling of urine?","Can’t recognize faces of old coworkers?","Side effects to watch with donepezil?"],"recallAnswers":["Suggests delirium (acute).","Likely UTI → delirium.","A deficit in social cognition.","Insomnia, bradycardia, dizziness, headache, GI upset."],"requires":[],"terms":["donepezil"],"termDefinitions":{},"questions":[{"id":"q5-nc-8","sourceId":"q5-nc-8","q":"Two months after his wife died, a healthy 90-year-old is neglecting hygiene, has poor concentration, poor sleep and low energy, but does tasks when reminded. The most appropriate response is to:","o":["Reorient him to the date each time","Prepare the family for stage 2 dementia","Avoid touch and closeness","Arrange an evaluation for major depressive disorder"],"a":[3],"r":["Not supported for this question. Grief-triggered symptoms with intact ability when prompted suggest depression (pseudodementia), which is treatable.","Not supported for this question. Grief-triggered symptoms with intact ability when prompted suggest depression (pseudodementia), which is treatable.","Not supported for this question. Grief-triggered symptoms with intact ability when prompted suggest depression (pseudodementia), which is treatable.","Supported. Grief-triggered symptoms with intact ability when prompted suggest depression (pseudodementia), which is treatable."],"phys":"Grief-triggered symptoms with intact ability when prompted suggest depression (pseudodementia), which is treatable.","ex":[]},{"id":"learn|neurocog-change","q":"A patient with established dementia becomes inattentive and markedly more confused over a few hours. Which interpretation guides care?","o":["Possible delirium superimposed on dementia; assess for acute causes","Proof that dementia always progresses suddenly","A reason to skip the physical assessment"],"a":[0],"r":["An acute fluctuating change suggests delirium, including when dementia is already present.","Dementia usually follows a longer course; a rapid change needs investigation.","A physical cause such as infection, hypoxia or a medication effect may be treatable."],"phys":"","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-neurocog"},{"id":"t-personality","ch":"24","chapters":["24"],"title":"Personality disorders","notes":["t-personality"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 24; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Personality is a person’s lasting pattern of thinking, feeling and behaving. A personality disorder is an inflexible, maladaptive pattern that impairs relationships and functioning; people often believe the problem lies outside themselves. They usually enter care for a comorbid problem (depression, anxiety, substance use, self-harm). Personality still changes slowly across life, so treatment can help. Judge personality within the person’s culture.","Cluster A: odd or eccentric: Paranoid: distrust, suspicion, holds grudges, projection → keep every promise, be clear and neutral (not overly friendly), explain procedures, set limits on threats.; Schizoid: detached loner, restricted emotion, doesn’t want relationships → respect privacy, don’t push socialization, protect from ridicule.; Schizotypal: magical thinking, odd beliefs and speech, ideas of reference, social anxiety; can be made aware of distortions (unlike schizophrenia) → respect isolation, assess for suicide, low-dose antipsychotics.","Cluster B: dramatic, emotional, erratic: Borderline: unstable relationships, identity and mood; splitting; self-harm; fear of abandonment → consistent limits, DBT.; Antisocial: violates others’ rights, deceit, no remorse; conduct disorder before 15 → safety, consistent consequences.; Histrionic: attention-seeking, dramatic, seductive, vague speech → keep it professional, encourage concrete language, assess suicide gestures.; Narcissistic: grandiose, entitled, no empathy, fragile self-esteem underneath → stay neutral, avoid power struggles, don’t challenge grandiosity directly, model empathy."],"checks":["Which personality disorders are “odd or eccentric”?","“I’d really like to hurt her for hurting me.” Next step?","Schizotypal vs schizophrenia?","Bupropion for a schizoid patient aims to?"],"recallAnswers":["Paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal (Cluster A).","Assess immediately for violence risk.","Schizotypal patients can be made aware of their odd beliefs; delusions are not fixed.","Increase the pleasure of living."],"requires":[],"terms":["bupropion","psychotherapy"],"termDefinitions":{},"questions":[{"id":"q5-pd-2","sourceId":"q5-pd-2","q":"Which personality disorders are described as “odd or eccentric”? Select all that apply.","o":["Paranoid","Schizoid","Histrionic","Obsessive-compulsive","Avoidant"],"a":[0,1],"r":["Supported. Cluster A: paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal.","Supported. Cluster A: paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal.","Not supported for this question. Cluster A: paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal.","Not supported for this question. Cluster A: paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal.","Not supported for this question. Cluster A: paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal."],"phys":"Cluster A: paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-pd-3","sourceId":"q5-pd-3","q":"Which behaviors are primitive defense mechanisms often seen in personality disorders? Select all that apply.","o":["Regularly trying to split staff","Doing favors to undo feelings of anger","Rocking and humming when fearful","Lashing out verbally when criticized","Destroying belongings when angry"],"a":[0,1,2],"r":["Supported. Splitting, undoing, regression. Lashing out and destroying property are behaviors (acting out), not the defenses listed.","Supported. Splitting, undoing, regression. Lashing out and destroying property are behaviors (acting out), not the defenses listed.","Supported. Splitting, undoing, regression. Lashing out and destroying property are behaviors (acting out), not the defenses listed.","Not supported for this question. Splitting, undoing, regression. Lashing out and destroying property are behaviors (acting out), not the defenses listed.","Not supported for this question. Splitting, undoing, regression. Lashing out and destroying property are behaviors (acting out), not the defenses listed."],"phys":"Splitting, undoing, regression. Lashing out and destroying property are behaviors (acting out), not the defenses listed.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-pd-9","sourceId":"q5-pd-9","q":"A man with lifelong detachment (“I’ve always been a loner”) is started on bupropion to:","o":["Improve flat emotions","Help him sleep","Increase the pleasure of living","Prepare him for group therapy"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. In schizoid personality disorder, bupropion may increase pleasure in life.","Not supported for this question. In schizoid personality disorder, bupropion may increase pleasure in life.","Supported. In schizoid personality disorder, bupropion may increase pleasure in life.","Not supported for this question. In schizoid personality disorder, bupropion may increase pleasure in life."],"phys":"In schizoid personality disorder, bupropion may increase pleasure in life.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-pd-11","sourceId":"q5-pd-11","q":"Which nursing approach fits a patient with paranoid personality disorder?","o":["Be very warm and friendly","Keep every promised appointment and explain procedures clearly and neutrally","Push the patient into group activities","Challenge suspicious beliefs firmly"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Reliability and clear, neutral explanations build trust; too much friendliness raises suspicion.","Supported. Reliability and clear, neutral explanations build trust; too much friendliness raises suspicion.","Not supported for this question. Reliability and clear, neutral explanations build trust; too much friendliness raises suspicion.","Not supported for this question. Reliability and clear, neutral explanations build trust; too much friendliness raises suspicion."],"phys":"Reliability and clear, neutral explanations build trust; too much friendliness raises suspicion.","ex":[]}],"studio":"t-personality"},{"id":"c-adhd","ch":"11","chapters":["11"],"title":"ADHD","patient":"7-year-old boy brought to the clinic by his mother. His teacher reports he leaves his seat, interrupts, can’t wait his turn, and starts work but never finishes it. At home he loses shoes and homework, and “runs like a motor.” Classmates no longer invite him to play. Methylphenidate is being started.","notes":["c-adhd"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 11; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: The symptoms are in both settings, began early and impair learning and friendships, so this fits ADHD, combined type. Note his low self-esteem and peer rejection: they drive later problems if not addressed.","Priority: Priorities: safety (impulsive injury), then school performance and social relationships, then family coping and medication safety (appetite, growth, sleep, heart).","Act: Combine behavior therapy (parent training first under age 6; parent training plus medication at 6 and older) with school supports (IEP or 504 plan) and stimulant medication. Use structure, consistent limits, rewards and the disruptive-behavior techniques (limit setting, redirection, planned ignoring, signals, time-out).","Reassess: Use teacher and parent rating scales to measure attention and behavior; track height, weight, BP, HR, sleep and appetite on medication; and look at friendships, grades and family stress."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Look for inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity that is out of line with age, present before 12, in two or more settings (home and school), for 6 months, and that impairs school or social functioning. Gather data from parents, teachers and the child, and look for learning problems, anxiety and depression too. The symptoms are in both settings, began early and impair learning and friendships, so this fits ADHD, combined type. Note his low self-esteem and peer rejection: they drive later problems if not addressed.","Priorities: safety (impulsive injury), then school performance and social relationships, then family coping and medication safety (appetite, growth, sleep, heart). Combine behavior therapy (parent training first under age 6; parent training plus medication at 6 and older) with school supports (IEP or 504 plan) and stimulant medication. Use structure, consistent limits, rewards and the disruptive-behavior techniques (limit setting, redirection, planned ignoring, signals, time-out).","Use teacher and parent rating scales to measure attention and behavior; track height, weight, BP, HR, sleep and appetite on medication; and look at friendships, grades and family stress."],"requires":[],"terms":["Inattention","Hyperactivity","Impulsivity","methylphenidate","atomoxetine"],"termDefinitions":{"Inattention":"Careless mistakes, loses things, doesn’t seem to listen, doesn’t finish work, easily distracted, forgetful. The brain’s attention and executive-function networks (frontal lobe, low dopamine and norepinephrine signaling) don’t sustain focus. Use short, clear instructions, one step at a time; cut down distractions; check that he understood.","Hyperactivity":"Fidgets, leaves his seat, runs and climbs, can’t play quietly, talks constantly, “driven by a motor.” Poor regulation of motor activity and arousal. Give safe physical outlets (sports, movement breaks) rather than demanding stillness.","Impulsivity":"Blurts out answers, can’t wait his turn, interrupts and grabs. Weak inhibition (“braking”) by the prefrontal cortex. Safety risk (darting into traffic, climbing) and peer rejection. Clear rules, consistent consequences, praise for waiting."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-ch-2","sourceId":"q5-ch-2","q":"Which activity is most appropriate for a child with ADHD?","o":["Reading an adventure novel","Monopoly","Checkers","Tennis"],"a":[3],"r":["Not supported for this question. Physical activity with quick feedback suits a short attention span and high energy.","Not supported for this question. Physical activity with quick feedback suits a short attention span and high energy.","Not supported for this question. Physical activity with quick feedback suits a short attention span and high energy.","Supported. Physical activity with quick feedback suits a short attention span and high energy."],"phys":"Physical activity with quick feedback suits a short attention span and high energy.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ch-7","sourceId":"q5-ch-7","q":"Which teaching points apply to a child starting methylphenidate? Select all that apply.","o":["Give the last dose by about 4 p.m.","Track height and weight","Give it at bedtime to help sleep","Report a racing heart or new tics","Keep the medication secured"],"a":[0,1,3,4],"r":["Supported. Stimulants cause insomnia, appetite and growth suppression, ↑ HR and BP, can worsen tics, and are controlled substances.","Supported. Stimulants cause insomnia, appetite and growth suppression, ↑ HR and BP, can worsen tics, and are controlled substances.","Not supported for this question. Stimulants cause insomnia, appetite and growth suppression, ↑ HR and BP, can worsen tics, and are controlled substances.","Supported. Stimulants cause insomnia, appetite and growth suppression, ↑ HR and BP, can worsen tics, and are controlled substances.","Supported. Stimulants cause insomnia, appetite and growth suppression, ↑ HR and BP, can worsen tics, and are controlled substances."],"phys":"Stimulants cause insomnia, appetite and growth suppression, ↑ HR and BP, can worsen tics, and are controlled substances.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-adhd"},{"id":"c-autism","ch":"11","chapters":["11"],"title":"Autism spectrum disorder","patient":"12-year-old boy with autism, nonverbal, needs help with toileting and dressing. He spins a ball for long periods, can’t stand the sound of a flushing toilet, and only eats bananas when they are perfectly ripe. Since starting a new school 3 weeks ago, he bites his hand and hits his head when upset. His parents say, “We’re overwhelmed.”","notes":["c-autism"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 11; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: A major change in routine (new school) is driving escalating self-injury in a nonverbal child who can’t say he is distressed: this is level 3 autism. The family is under heavy stress.","Priority: Priorities: risk for injury (self-injury), then self-care deficit, impaired communication and socialization, and caregiver stress and family coping.","Act: Keep him safe; keep routines predictable (visual schedules); introduce change gradually in small steps; reduce sensory triggers; use rewards for adaptive behavior; refer early to intensive behavioral programs (ABA / early intensive behavioral intervention), speech, occupational and physical therapy; support the parents.","Reassess: Count self-injury episodes, measure the time tolerated in new activities, track progress toward skills (communication, self-care), and ask about parental stress and use of resources."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Look for the two core areas: social communication deficits (reciprocity, nonverbal communication, relationships) and restricted, repetitive behavior (stereotyped movements or speech, insistence on sameness, fixated interests, over- or under-reaction to sensory input). Note the support level, any self-injury, the developmental history and family stress. A major change in routine (new school) is driving escalating self-injury in a nonverbal child who can’t say he is distressed: this is level 3 autism. The family is under heavy stress.","Priorities: risk for injury (self-injury), then self-care deficit, impaired communication and socialization, and caregiver stress and family coping. Keep him safe; keep routines predictable (visual schedules); introduce change gradually in small steps; reduce sensory triggers; use rewards for adaptive behavior; refer early to intensive behavioral programs (ABA / early intensive behavioral intervention), speech, occupational and physical therapy; support the parents.","Count self-injury episodes, measure the time tolerated in new activities, track progress toward skills (communication, self-care), and ask about parental stress and use of resources."],"requires":[],"terms":["Insistence on sameness","Sensory hyper- or hyporeactivity","Stereotyped behavior and echolalia","Self-injury","aripiprazole","naltrexone","risperidone"],"termDefinitions":{"Insistence on sameness":"Extreme distress at small changes: a new route, an unripe banana, a new school. Predictability lowers overwhelming anxiety for a brain that processes change and sensory input differently. Keep routines and structure; prepare for changes with visual schedules and warnings; change one thing at a time.","Sensory hyper- or hyporeactivity":"Covers ears at a toilet flush; doesn’t react to pain; loves the sound of Velcro. Sensory input is processed atypically: too intense or too weak. Reduce triggers (don’t flush near him), use preferred sensations calmingly, and check carefully for injury or illness, since pain may not show.","Stereotyped behavior and echolalia":"Spinning objects, hand flapping, rocking, lining up toys, repeating words. Self-soothing and self-regulating behaviors. Allow safe ones; redirect only when unsafe or when they block learning.","Self-injury":"Hand biting, head banging, face slapping when frustrated. A way to communicate distress or regulate overwhelming input when he has no words. Risk for injury is the priority: protect (a helmet if needed), find the trigger, and teach other ways to signal distress."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-ch-8","sourceId":"q5-ch-8","q":"For a nonverbal 12-year-old with autism who bites himself when routines change, which nursing action is indicated?","o":["Write a behavioral contract with him","Have him role-play a script","Use a reward system for adapting to new situations","Firmly insist on immediate changes to his routine"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Rewards and gradual change work; contracts and role-play need language skills he lacks.","Not supported for this question. Rewards and gradual change work; contracts and role-play need language skills he lacks.","Supported. Rewards and gradual change work; contracts and role-play need language skills he lacks.","Not supported for this question. Rewards and gradual change work; contracts and role-play need language skills he lacks."],"phys":"Rewards and gradual change work; contracts and role-play need language skills he lacks.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ch-9","sourceId":"q5-ch-9","q":"Which medications are FDA approved for irritability associated with autism? Select all that apply.","o":["Risperidone","Aripiprazole","Lithium","Methylphenidate"],"a":[0,1],"r":["Supported. Risperidone and aripiprazole; watch for EPS, sleepiness and weight gain.","Supported. Risperidone and aripiprazole; watch for EPS, sleepiness and weight gain.","Not supported for this question. Risperidone and aripiprazole; watch for EPS, sleepiness and weight gain.","Not supported for this question. Risperidone and aripiprazole; watch for EPS, sleepiness and weight gain."],"phys":"Risperidone and aripiprazole; watch for EPS, sleepiness and weight gain.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-autism"},{"id":"c-anorexia","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Anorexia nervosa","patient":"21-year-old college track athlete brought in by his brothers after fainting at practice. 5 ft 2 in and 58 lb (about 50% of ideal body weight). BP 74/50, HR 54, temperature 96 °F, hemoglobin 9, fine downy hair on his back, cold mottled hands. He says, “I check my legs every night. I’m so afraid of getting fat or slowing down.”","notes":["c-anorexia"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 18; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Severe malnutrition (about 50% of ideal weight) with cardiovascular compromise (bradycardia, hypotension, syncope) = medical admission. Once feeding starts, the big hidden danger is refeeding syndrome.","Priority: First: impaired nutrition, decreased cardiac output, electrolyte and fluid imbalance (life-threatening). Then body image, ineffective coping, low self-esteem and family processes.","Act: Medically stabilize, then a structured weight-restoration program: set meal times and duration, a dietitian’s plan, observe meals and 1 hour after, supervise bathroom use, limit exercise, weigh at set times in a gown or underwear after voiding (backward if needed), don’t negotiate or reweigh, praise progress, and use CBT and family-based therapy.","Reassess: Track weight on schedule, vital signs, orthostatics, electrolytes (especially phosphate in the first week), intake, hiding or compensating behaviors and statements about body image."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Recognize severe underweight (BMI and % of ideal weight), an intense fear of weight gain, a distorted body image and the signs of starvation: bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, orthostasis, lanugo, cold extremities, amenorrhea, constipation, anemia, low bone density. Ask about purging and exercise. Most patients deny there is a problem. Severe malnutrition (about 50% of ideal weight) with cardiovascular compromise (bradycardia, hypotension, syncope) = medical admission. Once feeding starts, the big hidden danger is refeeding syndrome.","First: impaired nutrition, decreased cardiac output, electrolyte and fluid imbalance (life-threatening). Then body image, ineffective coping, low self-esteem and family processes. Medically stabilize, then a structured weight-restoration program: set meal times and duration, a dietitian’s plan, observe meals and 1 hour after, supervise bathroom use, limit exercise, weigh at set times in a gown or underwear after voiding (backward if needed), don’t negotiate or reweigh, praise progress, and use CBT and family-based therapy.","Track weight on schedule, vital signs, orthostatics, electrolytes (especially phosphate in the first week), intake, hiding or compensating behaviors and statements about body image."],"requires":[],"terms":["Bradycardia and hypotension","Lanugo","Body image disturbance","Ego-syntonic illness","Refeeding syndrome"],"termDefinitions":{"Bradycardia and hypotension":"HR 54, BP 74/50, dizziness, fainting. The starving body lowers its metabolic rate to save energy, and heart muscle wastes too, so the heart pumps less. HR under 50, systolic under 90, temperature under 96 °F, arrhythmias or under 75% of ideal weight → hospitalize.","Lanugo":"Fine, soft, downy hair on the face, back and arms. The body grows insulating hair when fat stores are gone. A visible sign of significant starvation.","Body image disturbance":"“I’m fat” while emaciated; checks body parts every night. Altered processing of body signals (insula) plus a self-worth tied to shape and weight. Don’t argue about size; focus on health and feelings. Weigh him backward on the scale if seeing the number raises anxiety.","Ego-syntonic illness":"He values the restriction and resists treatment. Restriction briefly relieves anxiety (lower tryptophan, then less serotonin activity), which rewards it. Expect ambivalence; the nurse does not negotiate weight, but supports him through the fear.","Refeeding syndrome":"After feeding begins: weakness, swelling, confusion, arrhythmias, seizures, heart failure. Insulin rises with carbohydrate, pushing phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells (and using up thiamine), so blood levels crash. The wasted heart can’t handle the fluid. Start feeding low and slow; check phosphate, K⁺ and Mg²⁺ daily at first; give thiamine; watch for edema and heart failure. Gain no more than 2–3 lb a week (over 5 lb a week risks pulmonary edema)."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-ea-2","sourceId":"q5-ea-2","q":"Which monitoring interventions are appropriate for a patient with anorexia? Select all that apply.","o":["Scheduled meals and snacks","Praise weight gain","Limit time in the bathroom when unsupervised","Promote exercise to boost appetite","Observe during and after meals"],"a":[0,2,4],"r":["Supported. Structure and observation prevent hiding food and purging. Exercise is limited; praise behaviors, not the number.","Not supported for this question. Structure and observation prevent hiding food and purging. Exercise is limited; praise behaviors, not the number.","Supported. Structure and observation prevent hiding food and purging. Exercise is limited; praise behaviors, not the number.","Not supported for this question. Structure and observation prevent hiding food and purging. Exercise is limited; praise behaviors, not the number.","Supported. Structure and observation prevent hiding food and purging. Exercise is limited; praise behaviors, not the number."],"phys":"Structure and observation prevent hiding food and purging. Exercise is limited; praise behaviors, not the number.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ea-3","sourceId":"q5-ea-3","q":"Which patient statement supports a diagnosis of anorexia nervosa?","o":["“I’m terrified of gaining weight.”","“I wish I had a friend to talk to.”","“I drink too much.”","“Nothing gives me pleasure.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. An intense fear of weight gain is a core criterion.","Not supported for this question. An intense fear of weight gain is a core criterion.","Not supported for this question. An intense fear of weight gain is a core criterion.","Not supported for this question. An intense fear of weight gain is a core criterion."],"phys":"An intense fear of weight gain is a core criterion.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ea-4","sourceId":"q5-ea-4","q":"On day 3 of refeeding, a patient with severe anorexia has new edema and shortness of breath. Which lab should the nurse check first?","o":["Phosphate","Amylase","TSH","Hemoglobin A1C"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Refeeding syndrome: phosphate (and K⁺, Mg²⁺) drop as insulin pushes them into cells.","Not supported for this question. Refeeding syndrome: phosphate (and K⁺, Mg²⁺) drop as insulin pushes them into cells.","Not supported for this question. Refeeding syndrome: phosphate (and K⁺, Mg²⁺) drop as insulin pushes them into cells.","Not supported for this question. Refeeding syndrome: phosphate (and K⁺, Mg²⁺) drop as insulin pushes them into cells."],"phys":"Refeeding syndrome: phosphate (and K⁺, Mg²⁺) drop as insulin pushes them into cells.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-anorexia"},{"id":"c-bulimia","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Bulimia nervosa","patient":"40-year-old teacher at 95% of ideal weight who binges every night and vomits up to 15 times. She has eroded tooth enamel, swollen parotid glands and calluses on the back of her hand. Potassium 2.7 mEq/L, and her ECG shows changes consistent with hypokalemia. She says, “I’m ashamed I can’t control it. I hate myself.”","notes":["c-bulimia"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 18; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Frequent purging has caused severe hypokalemia with ECG changes and orthostasis: a cardiac risk, even though her weight looks fine. Shame and a trauma history drive the binge-purge cycle.","Priority: First: electrolyte imbalance and decreased cardiac output (from purging). Then ineffective coping, chronic low self-esteem, body image and social isolation.","Act: Replace electrolytes and monitor; structured, regular meals; observe for 1–2 hours after meals and supervise the bathroom; teach the binge-purge cycle and planned eating; CBT (first line) to challenge distorted thoughts; journal triggers; treat comorbid depression; fluoxetine (FDA approved for bulimia, at higher doses).","Reassess: Track potassium and the ECG, the number of binge-purge episodes (journal), adherence to meal plans, use of coping skills and mood."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Recognize recurrent binges (a large amount in about 2 hours with loss of control) followed by compensating (vomiting, laxatives, diuretics, fasting, excess exercise) at least weekly for 3 months, with self-worth tied to shape and weight. Weight is often normal, so look for the signs: Russell sign, parotid swelling, dental erosion, and electrolyte losses. Frequent purging has caused severe hypokalemia with ECG changes and orthostasis: a cardiac risk, even though her weight looks fine. Shame and a trauma history drive the binge-purge cycle.","First: electrolyte imbalance and decreased cardiac output (from purging). Then ineffective coping, chronic low self-esteem, body image and social isolation. Replace electrolytes and monitor; structured, regular meals; observe for 1–2 hours after meals and supervise the bathroom; teach the binge-purge cycle and planned eating; CBT (first line) to challenge distorted thoughts; journal triggers; treat comorbid depression; fluoxetine (FDA approved for bulimia, at higher doses).","Track potassium and the ECG, the number of binge-purge episodes (journal), adherence to meal plans, use of coping skills and mood."],"requires":[],"terms":["Hypokalemia","Russell sign","Parotid swelling and dental erosion","bupropion","fluoxetine"],"termDefinitions":{"Hypokalemia":"K⁺ 2.7; weakness, palpitations, ECG changes (flat T, U waves). Vomiting loses stomach acid and potassium; the alkalosis that follows drives K⁺ into cells and the kidneys waste more. The most dangerous finding: arrhythmias and sudden death. Replace potassium and monitor the ECG. K⁺ below 3.2, chloride below 88, esophageal tears or arrhythmias → hospitalize.","Russell sign":"Calluses or scars on the knuckles and back of the hand. Teeth rub the hand during self-induced vomiting. A physical clue to purging in a normal-weight patient.","Parotid swelling and dental erosion":"“Chipmunk” cheeks, worn enamel, cavities. Repeated vomiting stimulates the salivary glands, and stomach acid dissolves enamel. Dental referral; teach rinsing with water (not brushing right after vomiting)."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-ea-6","sourceId":"q5-ea-6","q":"Which antidepressant is contraindicated in bulimia nervosa?","o":["Fluoxetine","Sertraline","Bupropion","Imipramine"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Bupropion raises seizure risk, especially with purging and electrolyte imbalance. Fluoxetine is FDA approved.","Not supported for this question. Bupropion raises seizure risk, especially with purging and electrolyte imbalance. Fluoxetine is FDA approved.","Supported. Bupropion raises seizure risk, especially with purging and electrolyte imbalance. Fluoxetine is FDA approved.","Not supported for this question. Bupropion raises seizure risk, especially with purging and electrolyte imbalance. Fluoxetine is FDA approved."],"phys":"Bupropion raises seizure risk, especially with purging and electrolyte imbalance. Fluoxetine is FDA approved.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-ea-7","sourceId":"q5-ea-7","q":"Which intervention promotes independence in a patient being treated for bulimia?","o":["The patient monitors her own calories and intake and output","Behavior modification for weight gain","A daily log of feelings and urges","Allowing limited choices about eating and exercise as progress continues"],"a":[3],"r":["Not supported for this question. Gradually returning control as goals are met builds independence.","Not supported for this question. Gradually returning control as goals are met builds independence.","Not supported for this question. Gradually returning control as goals are met builds independence.","Supported. Gradually returning control as goals are met builds independence."],"phys":"Gradually returning control as goals are met builds independence.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-bulimia"},{"id":"c-delirium","ch":"23","chapters":["23"],"title":"Delirium","patient":"82-year-old woman, day 3 after hip surgery, on opioids for pain, with a urinary catheter. Yesterday she chatted with her daughter; tonight she is picking at the sheets, says there are bugs on the blanket, tries to climb out of bed and pulls at her IV. She dozes, then is agitated again. Temperature 38.3 °C, cloudy urine.","notes":["c-delirium"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 23; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: An acute change from yesterday, fluctuating, with inattention, perceptual disturbance and fever with cloudy urine on opioids = delirium from likely UTI plus medication. It is a medical emergency: untreated, it raises mortality, length of stay and permanent cognitive decline.","Priority: Priorities: risk for injury (falls, pulling lines), acute confusion and its underlying cause, fluid balance, sleep, and fear or anxiety.","Act: Find and treat the cause (labs, cultures, medication review). Keep her safe without restraints if possible (they worsen delirium): close observation, family at the bedside, a low bed, a well-lit, quiet room, glasses and hearing aids on, clocks and calendars, consistent caregivers, short simple statements, introduce yourself every time, reorient gently (don’t quiz her). Fluids, nutrition, sleep and mobility. Antipsychotics or anxiolytics only cautiously for dangerous agitation.","Reassess: Recheck attention and orientation each shift (with a tool such as the CAM), vital signs, labs, intake, sleep and falls; she should return to baseline as the cause clears (delirium is usually reversible)."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Delirium = an acute (hours to days), fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness plus another cognitive change (disorientation, memory, perception), caused by a medical condition, substance or withdrawal. Look for inattention (can’t focus or follow), a sudden change from baseline (ask the family), visual hallucinations or illusions, a disrupted sleep–wake cycle, hyperactive (agitated) or hypoactive (quiet, easily missed) forms, and autonomic signs. An acute change from yesterday, fluctuating, with inattention, perceptual disturbance and fever with cloudy urine on opioids = delirium from likely UTI plus medication. It is a medical emergency: untreated, it raises mortality, length of stay and permanent cognitive decline.","Priorities: risk for injury (falls, pulling lines), acute confusion and its underlying cause, fluid balance, sleep, and fear or anxiety. Find and treat the cause (labs, cultures, medication review). Keep her safe without restraints if possible (they worsen delirium): close observation, family at the bedside, a low bed, a well-lit, quiet room, glasses and hearing aids on, clocks and calendars, consistent caregivers, short simple statements, introduce yourself every time, reorient gently (don’t quiz her). Fluids, nutrition, sleep and mobility. Antipsychotics or anxiolytics only cautiously for dangerous agitation.","Recheck attention and orientation each shift (with a tool such as the CAM), vital signs, labs, intake, sleep and falls; she should return to baseline as the cause clears (delirium is usually reversible)."],"requires":[],"terms":["Inattention","Illusion vs hallucination","Hypoactive delirium"],"termDefinitions":{"Inattention":"Stares past you, loses track mid-sentence, can’t follow a two-step instruction. Brain metabolism is disturbed globally (infection, hypoxia, drugs, electrolytes), so arousal and attention fail first. The cardinal sign: any acute confusion in an older adult is delirium until proven otherwise, not “just dementia.”","Illusion vs hallucination":"Folds in the blanket look like snakes (illusion) vs seeing bugs that aren’t there (hallucination). Illusions misread a real stimulus; hallucinations have no stimulus. In delirium they are usually visual (and tactile). Illusions can be explained and corrected (turn on the light, show her the blanket). Respond to the fear.","Hypoactive delirium":"A quiet, drowsy, withdrawn patient who “seems calm.” Same brain dysfunction, without agitation. Often missed: assess attention in every older inpatient."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-nc-3","sourceId":"q5-nc-3","q":"Which intervention is specific to delirium rather than dementia?","o":["Assisting with nutrition, elimination and hygiene","Monitoring neurological status on an ongoing basis","An ID bracelet","One simple direction at a time"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Delirium has a medical cause and can change quickly, so ongoing neuro monitoring is essential.","Supported. Delirium has a medical cause and can change quickly, so ongoing neuro monitoring is essential.","Not supported for this question. Delirium has a medical cause and can change quickly, so ongoing neuro monitoring is essential.","Not supported for this question. Delirium has a medical cause and can change quickly, so ongoing neuro monitoring is essential."],"phys":"Delirium has a medical cause and can change quickly, so ongoing neuro monitoring is essential.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-nc-6","sourceId":"q5-nc-6","q":"A nurse finds her 85-year-old mother disheveled, speaking nonsense and smelling of urine. Her mother’s condition is most likely due to:","o":["Early-onset dementia","A mild cognitive disorder","A urinary tract infection","Skipping breakfast"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. An acute change + urine odor → UTI-induced delirium.","Not supported for this question. An acute change + urine odor → UTI-induced delirium.","Supported. An acute change + urine odor → UTI-induced delirium.","Not supported for this question. An acute change + urine odor → UTI-induced delirium."],"phys":"An acute change + urine odor → UTI-induced delirium.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-nc-7","sourceId":"q5-nc-7","q":"On post-op day 3, a 70-year-old is affectionate with her nurse, then later asks her to leave because she doesn’t recognize her. The most likely reason is:","o":["Attention-seeking","Early dementia","Acute delirium","Splitting staff"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Fluctuating recognition after surgery = delirium.","Not supported for this question. Fluctuating recognition after surgery = delirium.","Supported. Fluctuating recognition after surgery = delirium.","Not supported for this question. Fluctuating recognition after surgery = delirium."],"phys":"Fluctuating recognition after surgery = delirium.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-delirium"},{"id":"c-alz","ch":"23","chapters":["23"],"title":"Alzheimer’s disease (major neurocognitive disorder)","patient":"67-year-old man with Alzheimer’s disease diagnosed 4 years ago. He wanders away from home about four times a week, has fallen getting out of bed at night (“I’m in a sleeping bag in the mountains”), is incontinent when he can’t find the bathroom, can’t find his words, and asked his wife, “Who is this woman?” She sobs: “I feel like I’ve betrayed him.”","notes":["c-alz"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 23; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: He is in moderate to severe Alzheimer’s disease: wandering, night-time disorientation, agnosia and aphasia make injury the top risk, and his wife shows caregiver stress and grief.","Priority: Priority: risk for injury (wandering, falls, burns, eating nonfood items). Then self-care deficits (eating, toileting, dressing), communication, sleep, and caregiver stress.","Act: Person-centered care (know his life story; relationships before tasks), with unconditional positive regard. Safety: low bed or mattress on the floor, lighting, remove hazards, locks placed high, alarms, ID bracelet. Communication: name, introduce yourself, one simple direction at a time, distraction rather than confrontation, no quizzing. Routine and structure, finger foods, scheduled toileting, labeled rooms with pictures. Support the family.","Reassess: Count falls and wandering episodes, track weight and intake, continence, sleep, the level of assistance needed, and caregiver coping. Reset outcomes as the disease progresses."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Recognize a gradual, progressive, irreversible decline in memory plus other domains (language, executive function, perception, social cognition) that interferes with daily independence (major neurocognitive disorder). Know the signs: aphasia, apraxia, agnosia, agraphia, confabulation, perseveration, hyperorality, sundowning, wandering. Identify the stage and the safety risks. He is in moderate to severe Alzheimer’s disease: wandering, night-time disorientation, agnosia and aphasia make injury the top risk, and his wife shows caregiver stress and grief.","Priority: risk for injury (wandering, falls, burns, eating nonfood items). Then self-care deficits (eating, toileting, dressing), communication, sleep, and caregiver stress. Person-centered care (know his life story; relationships before tasks), with unconditional positive regard. Safety: low bed or mattress on the floor, lighting, remove hazards, locks placed high, alarms, ID bracelet. Communication: name, introduce yourself, one simple direction at a time, distraction rather than confrontation, no quizzing. Routine and structure, finger foods, scheduled toileting, labeled rooms with pictures. Support the family.","Count falls and wandering episodes, track weight and intake, continence, sleep, the level of assistance needed, and caregiver coping. Reset outcomes as the disease progresses."],"requires":[],"terms":["Agnosia","Apraxia","Aphasia and agraphia","Confabulation","Sundowning","Perseveration and hyperorality","donepezil","rivastigmine","memantine"],"termDefinitions":{"Agnosia":"Doesn’t recognize his wife, or picks up a spoon and asks what it is. Damage to the brain areas that match sensations to stored knowledge. Introduce yourself and others every time; label objects and rooms with pictures.","Apraxia":"Can’t dress himself (arms in his trouser legs) though his muscles work. Loss of planned, purposeful movement sequences. Give one step at a time (“Here’s your shirt. Put your arm in here.”); Velcro and elastic clothing.","Aphasia and agraphia":"Searches for words, then only a few words, then babbling or mutism; can’t read or write. The language areas degenerate. Short, simple sentences; yes/no questions; gestures and pictures; patience.","Confabulation":"“I went to lunch with my family Sunday” (he was in bed all weekend). The brain fills memory gaps to protect self-esteem; it is not lying. Don’t confront it; redirect gently.","Sundowning":"More agitated, confused and wandering in the late afternoon and evening. Fatigue, fading light and a disrupted body clock. Calm evenings, good lighting, daytime activity and morning sunlight; avoid naps and caffeine late in the day.","Perseveration and hyperorality":"Repeats “hello” again and again; puts everything in his mouth. Loss of frontal control. Redirect; remove nonfood items that look like food."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-nc-4","sourceId":"q5-nc-4","q":"Which side effects should the nurse monitor for with donepezil? Select all that apply.","o":["Insomnia","Constipation","Bradycardia","Dizziness","Headache"],"a":[0,2,3,4],"r":["Supported. More acetylcholine → GI upset (often diarrhea), bradycardia, dizziness, insomnia, headache.","Not supported for this question. More acetylcholine → GI upset (often diarrhea), bradycardia, dizziness, insomnia, headache.","Supported. More acetylcholine → GI upset (often diarrhea), bradycardia, dizziness, insomnia, headache.","Supported. More acetylcholine → GI upset (often diarrhea), bradycardia, dizziness, insomnia, headache.","Supported. More acetylcholine → GI upset (often diarrhea), bradycardia, dizziness, insomnia, headache."],"phys":"More acetylcholine → GI upset (often diarrhea), bradycardia, dizziness, insomnia, headache.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-nc-5","sourceId":"q5-nc-5","q":"Why offer easily available finger foods throughout the day to a person with dementia?","o":["To increase intake throughout the day","Because the person may be anorexic","To help monitor intake","To prevent constipation"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. People who won’t sit for meals can eat on the move.","Not supported for this question. People who won’t sit for meals can eat on the move.","Not supported for this question. People who won’t sit for meals can eat on the move.","Not supported for this question. People who won’t sit for meals can eat on the move."],"phys":"People who won’t sit for meals can eat on the move.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-nc-9","sourceId":"q5-nc-9","q":"For a patient with middle-stage Alzheimer’s disease, which actions will the nurse implement? Select all that apply.","o":["Minimize sensory stimulation","Replace Velcro with buttons and zippers","Expose to morning sunlight","Provide picture magazines and children’s books","Provide a loosely structured environment"],"a":[0,2,3],"r":["Supported. Low stimulation, morning light for sleep, simple picture materials. Use Velcro and elastic, and keep a structured routine.","Not supported for this question. Low stimulation, morning light for sleep, simple picture materials. Use Velcro and elastic, and keep a structured routine.","Supported. Low stimulation, morning light for sleep, simple picture materials. Use Velcro and elastic, and keep a structured routine.","Supported. Low stimulation, morning light for sleep, simple picture materials. Use Velcro and elastic, and keep a structured routine.","Not supported for this question. Low stimulation, morning light for sleep, simple picture materials. Use Velcro and elastic, and keep a structured routine."],"phys":"Low stimulation, morning light for sleep, simple picture materials. Use Velcro and elastic, and keep a structured routine.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-nc-10","sourceId":"q5-nc-10","q":"A patient in bed all weekend says he went to lunch with his family Sunday. This is:","o":["Lying","Confabulation","Perseveration","Agnosia"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Confabulation fills memory gaps unconsciously to protect self-esteem.","Supported. Confabulation fills memory gaps unconsciously to protect self-esteem.","Not supported for this question. Confabulation fills memory gaps unconsciously to protect self-esteem.","Not supported for this question. Confabulation fills memory gaps unconsciously to protect self-esteem."],"phys":"Confabulation fills memory gaps unconsciously to protect self-esteem.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-alz"},{"id":"c-bpd","ch":"24","chapters":["24"],"title":"Borderline personality disorder","patient":"24-year-old admitted after cutting her wrists, ankles and labia with glass after her college graduation. She says, “I feel empty inside.” On day 1 she calls the nurses “angels of mercy”; by day 7 she demands a new primary nurse because “she hates me,” sneaks alcohol onto the unit, and threatens to cut again when told no.","notes":["c-bpd"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 24; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Repeated self-harm after a life transition (graduation = a separation), chronic emptiness, idealization then devaluation (splitting), impulsivity with alcohol and sex, and threats when limits are set = borderline personality disorder with high risk of self-mutilation and suicide.","Priority: Priority: risk for suicide and self-mutilation. Then risk for violence, ineffective coping, disturbed identity, and impaired social interaction.","Act: Safety first; a consistent team approach with clear, firm, consistent limits and consequences; straightforward communication; neutral wound care; encourage verbalizing feelings; teach coping and DBT skills; watch for manipulation (flattery, guilt); weekly staff meetings to manage splitting.","Reassess: Measure self-harm incidents, suicidal statements, use of skills and verbalizing instead of acting out, adherence to limits and attendance at DBT."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Look for a long-standing pattern of unstable relationships (idealize → devalue), unstable self-image, intense, rapidly shifting moods (emotional lability), impulsivity (spending, sex, substances, binge eating, reckless driving), recurrent self-harm or suicidal behavior, chronic emptiness, intense anger, frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, and brief stress-related paranoia or dissociation. Hear splitting. Repeated self-harm after a life transition (graduation = a separation), chronic emptiness, idealization then devaluation (splitting), impulsivity with alcohol and sex, and threats when limits are set = borderline personality disorder with high risk of self-mutilation and suicide.","Priority: risk for suicide and self-mutilation. Then risk for violence, ineffective coping, disturbed identity, and impaired social interaction. Safety first; a consistent team approach with clear, firm, consistent limits and consequences; straightforward communication; neutral wound care; encourage verbalizing feelings; teach coping and DBT skills; watch for manipulation (flattery, guilt); weekly staff meetings to manage splitting.","Measure self-harm incidents, suicidal statements, use of skills and verbalizing instead of acting out, adherence to limits and attendance at DBT."],"requires":[],"terms":["Splitting","Self-mutilation","Fear of abandonment","lithium","naltrexone","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Splitting":"“You’re the best nurse ever. The night nurse is cruel.” Then, a day later, you’re the cruel one. An early defense: people are seen as all good or all bad, because good and bad can’t be held in one picture; linked to disrupted early attachment (Mahler’s separation-individuation). Don’t accept the praise or defend against the attack. Staff stay consistent and united; discuss it in team meetings.","Self-mutilation":"Cutting, burning, pouring drain cleaner on her skin. Relieves unbearable tension or numbness and can summon care. Treat wounds matter-of-factly and neutrally, then ask her to write down the events before and after before discussing it, which builds thinking over acting.","Fear of abandonment":"Rage or self-harm when a nurse goes on vacation or a partner leaves the room. Hypersensitivity to rejection, with a strong limbic reaction and weak frontal control. Prepare her for staff changes and endings ahead of time."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-pd-4","sourceId":"q5-pd-4","q":"A patient with borderline personality disorder burns her underarms with a curling iron 2 hours after hearing her nurse will be on vacation, saying it “makes the numbness stop.” The nurse recognizes:","o":["Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder","Borderline personality disorder","Antisocial personality disorder","Schizotypal personality disorder"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Fear of abandonment + self-harm + emptiness = borderline.","Supported. Fear of abandonment + self-harm + emptiness = borderline.","Not supported for this question. Fear of abandonment + self-harm + emptiness = borderline.","Not supported for this question. Fear of abandonment + self-harm + emptiness = borderline."],"phys":"Fear of abandonment + self-harm + emptiness = borderline.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-pd-5","sourceId":"q5-pd-5","q":"A patient with borderline personality disorder has cut her arm superficially. After neutral wound care, the nurse’s best action is to:","o":["Give extra attention to show she is cared for","Ask her to write out the events before and after the cutting, then discuss them","Place her in restraints","Tell her the behavior is manipulative"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Matter-of-fact care plus a cognitive exercise encourages thinking over acting, without rewarding self-harm.","Supported. Matter-of-fact care plus a cognitive exercise encourages thinking over acting, without rewarding self-harm.","Not supported for this question. Matter-of-fact care plus a cognitive exercise encourages thinking over acting, without rewarding self-harm.","Not supported for this question. Matter-of-fact care plus a cognitive exercise encourages thinking over acting, without rewarding self-harm."],"phys":"Matter-of-fact care plus a cognitive exercise encourages thinking over acting, without rewarding self-harm.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-pd-6","sourceId":"q5-pd-6","q":"Which therapy has the strongest evidence for chronically suicidal patients with borderline personality disorder?","o":["Psychoanalysis","Dialectical behavior therapy","Aversion therapy","Electroconvulsive therapy"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. DBT: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness.","Supported. DBT: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness.","Not supported for this question. DBT: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness.","Not supported for this question. DBT: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness."],"phys":"DBT: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-bpd"},{"id":"c-aspd","ch":"24","chapters":["24"],"title":"Antisocial personality disorder","patient":"25-year-old divorced cab driver referred by the court after an assault charge. He has a history of multiple arrests, substance use and dropping out of three clinics because “the staff don’t understand me.” He is polite and flirtatious with the female nurse, says he isn’t responsible because he is “manic,” and says he can’t tolerate any medication.","notes":["c-aspd"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 24; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Repeated assaults and arrests, deceit, blaming others, charm used to manipulate and conduct problems since adolescence = antisocial personality disorder, with risk for violence and substance use.","Priority: Priority: risk for violence (to others) and safety of patients and staff. Then impaired impulse control and impaired social interaction.","Act: Keep everyone safe; give clear, consistent limits and consequences enforced by the whole team; address manipulation directly; document behavior; help him recognize anger early and offer physical outlets (walking); listen (being heard defuses tension); use behavioral rewards for positive behavior.","Reassess: Count violent or threatening incidents and rule violations, his response to limits and consequences, and any signs of responsibility-taking."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Look for a pervasive pattern since age 15 of disregard for and violation of others’ rights: illegal acts, deceit and conning, impulsivity, irritability and fights, recklessness, irresponsibility (work, debts), and lack of remorse. Must be 18 or older with a history of conduct disorder before 15. Notice superficial charm and manipulation. Repeated assaults and arrests, deceit, blaming others, charm used to manipulate and conduct problems since adolescence = antisocial personality disorder, with risk for violence and substance use.","Priority: risk for violence (to others) and safety of patients and staff. Then impaired impulse control and impaired social interaction. Keep everyone safe; give clear, consistent limits and consequences enforced by the whole team; address manipulation directly; document behavior; help him recognize anger early and offer physical outlets (walking); listen (being heard defuses tension); use behavioral rewards for positive behavior.","Count violent or threatening incidents and rule violations, his response to limits and consequences, and any signs of responsibility-taking."],"requires":[],"terms":["Callousness","Manipulation","lithium","valproate","methylphenidate"],"termDefinitions":{"Callousness":"No guilt about hurting others; rationalizes (“he deserved it”). A profound lack of empathy; genetic risk (aggressive disregard, disinhibition), low serotonin with high dopamine, and childhood abuse or neglect. Don’t expect guilt to motivate change; use clear consequences.","Manipulation":"Flattery, flirting, playing staff against each other, making staff feel guilty. People are used as tools to get what he wants. Name the behavior directly and set limits: “Comments like that are not part of our professional relationship.” Document it; the whole team enforces the same limits."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-pd-7","sourceId":"q5-pd-7","q":"A prison nurse practitioner treating a 40-year-old serving a life sentence for murder recognizes that treatment will most likely:","o":["Transform him into a model prisoner","Not improve his coping","Reinforce high-risk behavior","Show small, incremental changes"],"a":[3],"r":["Not supported for this question. Expect small, incremental progress with antisocial personality disorder.","Not supported for this question. Expect small, incremental progress with antisocial personality disorder.","Not supported for this question. Expect small, incremental progress with antisocial personality disorder.","Supported. Expect small, incremental progress with antisocial personality disorder."],"phys":"Expect small, incremental progress with antisocial personality disorder.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-pd-10","sourceId":"q5-pd-10","q":"Which nursing approaches are appropriate for a patient with antisocial personality disorder? Select all that apply.","o":["Clear, consistent limits enforced by all staff","Directly address manipulation","Document behaviors carefully","Allow special privileges to build rapport","Offer physical outlets like walking to release anger"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. Special privileges invite manipulation; consistency is key.","Supported. Special privileges invite manipulation; consistency is key.","Supported. Special privileges invite manipulation; consistency is key.","Not supported for this question. Special privileges invite manipulation; consistency is key.","Supported. Special privileges invite manipulation; consistency is key."],"phys":"Special privileges invite manipulation; consistency is key.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-aspd"},{"id":"c-schizo","ch":"12","chapters":["12"],"title":"Schizophrenia — acute psychosis","patient":"24-year-old brought in by her mother after 3 weeks without sleep, mumbling to herself, and covering her windows with foil “so they can’t read my thoughts.” She stopped her antipsychotic 2 months ago because “it made me gain weight.” She drinks large amounts of water and smokes 2 packs a day. She looks to the corner of the room and whispers between answers.","notes":["c-schizo"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 12; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: This is an acute relapse of schizophrenia after stopping medication (because of weight gain, a side effect nobody addressed). Two safety risks stand out: command hallucinations telling her to die and paranoia. There is also a medical problem: hyponatremia from polydipsia may be adding confusion. Suicide risk in schizophrenia is high (5–10% die by suicide).","Priority: Priority one: safety: risk of suicide (command hallucinations) and violence (paranoia). Then the physical: hyponatremia, sleep, nutrition, hygiene. Then disturbed thought and perception, nonadherence and family support.","Act: Keep her safe and calm (low stimulation, a room near the station, closer observation). Build trust with short, frequent, honest contacts. With hallucinations: ask what she hears, don’t argue, state your reality, respond to the feeling and teach competing stimuli. With delusions: never debate; focus on the feeling and present reality. Manage fluids and sodium. Restart an antipsychotic she can tolerate, watch for cheeking, and teach the patient and family.","Reassess: Reassess safety every shift (commands, paranoia, suicide). Track sodium, intake and weight, sleep, hygiene and meals; whether she can say the voices are a symptom; whether she takes medication without cheeking; and side effects (AIMS for movements, weight, glucose).","Assess command content, intent, ability to resist, access to means and other actual risk factors. Most people with schizophrenia are not violent; respond to this patient’s findings and needs."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Recognize positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions, disorganized speech and behavior), negative symptoms (the six A’s), cognitive problems and affective changes. Hear command hallucinations and paranoia: these are the safety cues. Then check the body: sleep, food, hygiene, water intake (polydipsia) and substance use. Rule out medical causes first. This is an acute relapse of schizophrenia after stopping medication (because of weight gain, a side effect nobody addressed). Two safety risks stand out: command hallucinations telling her to die and paranoia. There is also a medical problem: hyponatremia from polydipsia may be adding confusion. Suicide risk in schizophrenia is high (5–10% die by suicide).","Priority one: safety: risk of suicide (command hallucinations) and violence (paranoia). Then the physical: hyponatremia, sleep, nutrition, hygiene. Then disturbed thought and perception, nonadherence and family support. Keep her safe and calm (low stimulation, a room near the station, closer observation). Build trust with short, frequent, honest contacts. With hallucinations: ask what she hears, don’t argue, state your reality, respond to the feeling and teach competing stimuli. With delusions: never debate; focus on the feeling and present reality. Manage fluids and sodium. Restart an antipsychotic she can tolerate, watch for cheeking, and teach the patient and family.","Reassess safety every shift (commands, paranoia, suicide). Track sodium, intake and weight, sleep, hygiene and meals; whether she can say the voices are a symptom; whether she takes medication without cheeking; and side effects (AIMS for movements, weight, glucose)."],"requires":[],"terms":["Auditory hallucinations","Command hallucination","Persecutory delusion","Paranoia","Negative symptoms (the A’s)","Polydipsia and water intoxication","Anosognosia","clozapine","olanzapine","quetiapine","aripiprazole","haloperidol","risperidone","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Auditory hallucinations":"She turns her head toward the corner, whispers between answers and laughs at nothing. The brain’s hearing areas fire with no outside sound; dopamine overactivity in the mesolimbic pathway is the main theory. The voice feels as real as yours. Ask “What are you hearing?” (not “What are the voices saying?”). Ask whether they tell her to do anything (command hallucinations) and whether she believes them.","Command hallucination":"“The voice says I don’t deserve to live and should jump.” A voice directing an action. Risk is highest when the patient believes the voice is real and feels unable to resist. A psychiatric emergency cue: assess suicide and violence risk, increase observation, and tell the provider.","Persecutory delusion":"“They’re following me. They put a chip in my head.” A fixed false belief held despite evidence, kept in place by impaired reality testing. Never argue with the delusion and never agree. Respond to the feeling (fear) and help her feel safe.","Paranoia":"Suspicious, guarded, won’t eat food from the tray, watches staff. An irrational fear that others intend harm. The symptom most linked to violence toward others (she may act in “self-defense”). Keep a calm distance, explain everything, and offer sealed or packaged food.","Negative symptoms (the A’s)":"Flat face, says little, stays in bed, no interest in anything or anyone. Anhedonia, avolition, asociality, affective blunting, apathy, alogia: the loss of normal functions, linked to low frontal-lobe activity. Harder to treat than positive symptoms and more disabling. They are not laziness; break tasks into small steps.","Polydipsia and water intoxication":"Carrying water bottles everywhere, dilute urine, and new confusion or worse psychosis. Compulsive drinking (and antipsychotic dry mouth) dilutes the blood sodium (hyponatremia). A sudden worsening of psychosis or confusion → check sodium. Track weights and intake, and restrict fluids as ordered.","Anosognosia":"“I’m not sick. Why would I take medicine for nothing?” The illness itself damages the brain’s ability to recognize the illness; it isn’t denial. The leading cause of stopping medication. Don’t argue; tie treatment to her goals (“to quiet the voices,” “to keep your job”)."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-sz-1","sourceId":"q5-sz-1","q":"Which findings suggest a young man is in the prodromal phase of schizophrenia? Select all that apply.","o":["Increasingly worried that others will steal his things","Unusual preoccupation with numbers and certain topics","More unusual thoughts and odd use of words","Growing difficulty concentrating","Sustained full remission of symptoms"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Supported. The prodrome brings mild, early suspiciousness, odd thinking and speech, and falling concentration and functioning, months before full psychosis.","Supported. The prodrome brings mild, early suspiciousness, odd thinking and speech, and falling concentration and functioning, months before full psychosis.","Supported. The prodrome brings mild, early suspiciousness, odd thinking and speech, and falling concentration and functioning, months before full psychosis.","Supported. The prodrome brings mild, early suspiciousness, odd thinking and speech, and falling concentration and functioning, months before full psychosis.","Not supported for this question. The prodrome brings mild, early suspiciousness, odd thinking and speech, and falling concentration and functioning, months before full psychosis."],"phys":"The prodrome brings mild, early suspiciousness, odd thinking and speech, and falling concentration and functioning, months before full psychosis.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-sz-4","sourceId":"q5-sz-4","q":"Which statements are therapeutic for a patient experiencing hallucinations? Select all that apply.","o":["“I know you say you hear voices, but I can’t hear them.”","“Stop listening to the voices; they are not real.”","“Tell me more about what you hear.”","“Please tell the voices to leave you alone for now.”"],"a":[0,2],"r":["Supported. State your reality and explore what she hears. Don’t dismiss the experience or talk to the voices as if they were real.","Not supported for this question. State your reality and explore what she hears. Don’t dismiss the experience or talk to the voices as if they were real.","Supported. State your reality and explore what she hears. Don’t dismiss the experience or talk to the voices as if they were real.","Not supported for this question. State your reality and explore what she hears. Don’t dismiss the experience or talk to the voices as if they were real."],"phys":"State your reality and explore what she hears. Don’t dismiss the experience or talk to the voices as if they were real.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-sz-5","sourceId":"q5-sz-5","q":"A patient with schizophrenia refuses medication. The nurse recognizes anosognosia when the patient believes:","o":["The medications don’t work","The nurses are trying to control his mind","The medications will make him sick","He is not actually ill"],"a":[3],"r":["Not supported for this question. Anosognosia is an inability, caused by the illness itself, to recognize that one is ill.","Not supported for this question. Anosognosia is an inability, caused by the illness itself, to recognize that one is ill.","Not supported for this question. Anosognosia is an inability, caused by the illness itself, to recognize that one is ill.","Supported. Anosognosia is an inability, caused by the illness itself, to recognize that one is ill."],"phys":"Anosognosia is an inability, caused by the illness itself, to recognize that one is ill.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-sz-6","sourceId":"q5-sz-6","q":"A patient says, “The voices are really bad today.” The most helpful nursing response is to:","o":["Give an extra PRN dose of the antipsychotic","Tell him the voices aren’t real and to ignore them","Direct him to his room to stop thinking about them","Encourage humming or listening to music"],"a":[3],"r":["Not supported for this question. Competing auditory stimuli reduce hallucinations; isolation tends to worsen them.","Not supported for this question. Competing auditory stimuli reduce hallucinations; isolation tends to worsen them.","Not supported for this question. Competing auditory stimuli reduce hallucinations; isolation tends to worsen them.","Supported. Competing auditory stimuli reduce hallucinations; isolation tends to worsen them."],"phys":"Competing auditory stimuli reduce hallucinations; isolation tends to worsen them.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-sz-7","sourceId":"q5-sz-7","q":"A patient with schizophrenia who drinks water constantly becomes newly confused and more psychotic. Which lab result should the nurse check first?","o":["Serum sodium","Hemoglobin A1C","Serum amylase","Lithium level"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Polydipsia → hyponatremia (water intoxication) causes confusion and worsening psychosis.","Not supported for this question. Polydipsia → hyponatremia (water intoxication) causes confusion and worsening psychosis.","Not supported for this question. Polydipsia → hyponatremia (water intoxication) causes confusion and worsening psychosis.","Not supported for this question. Polydipsia → hyponatremia (water intoxication) causes confusion and worsening psychosis."],"phys":"Polydipsia → hyponatremia (water intoxication) causes confusion and worsening psychosis.","ex":[]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Assess command content, intent, ability to resist, access to means and other actual risk factors. Most people with schizophrenia are not violent; respond to this patient’s findings and needs.","studio":"c-schizo"},{"id":"c-nms","ch":"12","chapters":["12"],"title":"Antipsychotic side-effect emergencies","patient":"30-year-old man on day 5 of haloperidol for acute psychosis. This morning he is stiff “like a board,” sweating, barely responds, and is incontinent. BP 172/104, then 98/60 an hour later; HR 124; temperature 40.1 °C (104.2 °F). Two days ago his neck twisted to one side and his eyes rolled up; benztropine fixed it within 20 minutes.","notes":["c-nms"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 12; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Rigidity + fever + altered consciousness + unstable vital signs on a first-generation antipsychotic early in treatment = NMS until proven otherwise. The earlier dystonia shows he is sensitive to D2 blockade. Check CK (very high), WBC, kidney function and electrolytes.","Priority: NMS is the emergency: hyperthermia, risk of airway or respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and kidney injury. Everything else waits.","Act: Hold all antipsychotics and call the prescriber stat; transfer to critical care. Cool (cooling blanket, ice packs or bath as ordered), give IV fluids and correct electrolytes, monitor the airway, rhythm and urine output, and give dantrolene (muscle relaxant) or bromocriptine (dopamine agonist) as ordered. For the milder EPS, treat as below.","Reassess: Monitor temperature, vital signs, mental status, rigidity, CK, kidney function and urine output until normal. Document the reaction as an allergy or adverse event, and make sure any future antipsychotic is restarted cautiously, after full recovery and at a low dose."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Know the side effects by timing and look. Acute dystonia (hours to days: sudden muscle spasm of the neck, eyes or tongue). Akathisia (days to weeks: can’t sit still, inner restlessness). Pseudoparkinsonism (weeks: tremor, shuffling, mask face). Tardive dyskinesia (months to years: lip smacking, tongue writhing). The emergencies: NMS (rigidity + high fever + altered mental status + unstable vitals), anticholinergic toxicity and severe neutropenia. Rigidity + fever + altered consciousness + unstable vital signs on a first-generation antipsychotic early in treatment = NMS until proven otherwise. The earlier dystonia shows he is sensitive to D2 blockade. Check CK (very high), WBC, kidney function and electrolytes.","NMS is the emergency: hyperthermia, risk of airway or respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and kidney injury. Everything else waits. Hold all antipsychotics and call the prescriber stat; transfer to critical care. Cool (cooling blanket, ice packs or bath as ordered), give IV fluids and correct electrolytes, monitor the airway, rhythm and urine output, and give dantrolene (muscle relaxant) or bromocriptine (dopamine agonist) as ordered. For the milder EPS, treat as below.","Monitor temperature, vital signs, mental status, rigidity, CK, kidney function and urine output until normal. Document the reaction as an allergy or adverse event, and make sure any future antipsychotic is restarted cautiously, after full recovery and at a low dose."],"requires":[],"terms":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)","Acute dystonia","Akathisia","Pseudoparkinsonism","Tardive dyskinesia","Anticholinergic toxicity","clozapine","olanzapine","quetiapine","benztropine","dantrolene","diazepam","lorazepam","haloperidol","risperidone","paliperidone","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)":"“Lead-pipe” rigidity, temperature above 103 °F (39.4 °C), confusion or stupor, sweating, labile BP, tachycardia, incontinence. Sudden, excessive dopamine blockade in the hypothalamus (temperature control) and the muscles; the rigid muscles generate heat and break down (rhabdomyolysis, high CK) → kidney failure, respiratory failure. Life-threatening emergency (about 6% die). Hold all antipsychotics, call the prescriber stat, cool, hydrate, transfer to critical care; dantrolene or bromocriptine.","Acute dystonia":"Sudden painful spasm: neck twisted (torticollis), eyes rolled up (oculogyric crisis), tongue protruding, back arched. D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway upsets the dopamine–acetylcholine balance that controls movement. Frightening, rarely dangerous (unless laryngeal: watch the airway). Give benztropine or diphenhydramine IM; relief in 5–15 minutes. Stay with him.","Akathisia":"Paces, shifts from foot to foot, rocks, says “I can’t sit still.” Motor restlessness from D2 blockade. Often mistaken for anxiety or agitation, which leads to giving more of the same drug. Report it; propranolol, a benzodiazepine or a medication change. Severe akathisia raises suicide risk.","Pseudoparkinsonism":"Mask-like face, shuffling gait, pill-rolling tremor, stiffness, drooling, slow movement. Too little dopamine effect in the movement circuits, as in Parkinson disease. Antiparkinsonian drug (benztropine, trihexyphenidyl), dose reduction; fall and choking precautions.","Tardive dyskinesia":"Lip smacking, tongue writhing or protruding, chewing, grimacing, finger and trunk movements. Long-term D2 blockade causes the receptors to become oversensitive; it develops after months to years. Often permanent. Screen with the AIMS at least every 3 months; valbenazine or deutetrabenazine; consider a second-generation drug.","Anticholinergic toxicity":"Hot, dry, flushed skin with fever, dilated pupils, urinary retention, no bowel sounds, tachycardia, delirium, worsening “psychosis.” Too much muscarinic blockade from several anticholinergic drugs (antipsychotic + benztropine + diphenhydramine). Emergency: hold medications, cool, catheterize as needed; specialist-directed physostigmine (after contraindication review, with cardiac monitoring). Suspect it whenever psychosis suddenly worsens."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-ap-1","sourceId":"q5-ap-1","q":"One week after starting haloperidol, a patient is rigid, diaphoretic and unable to speak. BP 170/100, P 110, T 104.2 °F. Which actions are the priority? Select all that apply.","o":["Hold the medication and contact the prescriber stat","Begin cooling measures","Give IM benztropine for a dystonic reaction","Reassure him that this will pass without treatment","Hold the medication and consult the prescriber when he rounds later"],"a":[0,1],"r":["Supported. This is NMS, a life-threatening emergency: hold antipsychotics, call stat and cool. It is not dystonia and it can’t wait.","Supported. This is NMS, a life-threatening emergency: hold antipsychotics, call stat and cool. It is not dystonia and it can’t wait.","Not supported for this question. This is NMS, a life-threatening emergency: hold antipsychotics, call stat and cool. It is not dystonia and it can’t wait.","Not supported for this question. This is NMS, a life-threatening emergency: hold antipsychotics, call stat and cool. It is not dystonia and it can’t wait.","Not supported for this question. This is NMS, a life-threatening emergency: hold antipsychotics, call stat and cool. It is not dystonia and it can’t wait."],"phys":"This is NMS, a life-threatening emergency: hold antipsychotics, call stat and cool. It is not dystonia and it can’t wait.","ex":[]},{"id":"learn|nms-response","q":"A patient receiving an antipsychotic develops fever, severe muscle rigidity, confusion and unstable blood pressure. Which response is appropriate?","o":["Hold the suspected drug and urgently activate medical evaluation and supportive care for possible NMS","Give the next antipsychotic dose because this is an expected therapeutic effect","Encourage walking alone until the rigidity resolves"],"a":[0],"r":["This cluster suggests neuroleptic malignant syndrome, a medical emergency.","These findings are not an expected therapeutic response.","The patient is unstable and needs monitored emergency care."],"phys":"","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-nms"},{"id":"c-somatic","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Somatic symptom and conversion disorders","patient":"49-year-old recently divorced mother in the ED for the fourth time this year with palpitations, shortness of breath and fatigue. Cardiac work-ups are normal. She says, “My mood is fine, but my body is a major problem,” leaves home only for work and groceries, and says, “I don’t have any friends.” (A second patient: a woman who suddenly can’t see after witnessing her husband’s affair, and calmly discusses it.)","notes":["c-somatic"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 17; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Normal work-ups, repeated visits, excessive health worry, a recent loss (divorce, children leaving) and isolation and loneliness point to somatic symptom disorder, with unresolved grief and few coping resources.","Priority: Priorities: ineffective coping, anxiety, pain, social isolation (loneliness) and low self-esteem; for factitious disorder imposed on another, the child’s safety comes first.","Act: Build a trusting relationship; acknowledge the symptoms as real; after a medical work-up, avoid more tests and don’t reinforce complaints; shift the focus to feelings and coping; teach relaxation and assertiveness; keep one consistent provider with brief, regular visits; refer for CBT.","Reassess: Evaluate the number of ED visits, use of coping skills, social contacts and her statements about her symptoms. Symptoms may persist, but concern and impairment should drop."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Recognize real, distressing physical symptoms (pain, fatigue, palpitations, GI symptoms) with excessive thoughts, worry or time spent on them for more than 6 months (somatic symptom disorder); worry about having an illness with few or no symptoms (illness anxiety); or a neurological loss (paralysis, blindness, seizures) with no neurological cause (conversion), often with la belle indifférence. Always rule out a medical cause first. Normal work-ups, repeated visits, excessive health worry, a recent loss (divorce, children leaving) and isolation and loneliness point to somatic symptom disorder, with unresolved grief and few coping resources.","Priorities: ineffective coping, anxiety, pain, social isolation (loneliness) and low self-esteem; for factitious disorder imposed on another, the child’s safety comes first. Build a trusting relationship; acknowledge the symptoms as real; after a medical work-up, avoid more tests and don’t reinforce complaints; shift the focus to feelings and coping; teach relaxation and assertiveness; keep one consistent provider with brief, regular visits; refer for CBT.","Evaluate the number of ED visits, use of coping skills, social contacts and her statements about her symptoms. Symptoms may persist, but concern and impairment should drop."],"requires":[],"terms":["Somatization","La belle indifférence","Secondary gain"],"termDefinitions":{"Somatization":"Distress shows up as back pain, palpitations, headaches or stomachaches, not as sadness or worry. Stress activates the body (HPA axis, sympathetic system), and some people and cultures experience distress physically. The symptoms are real, not faked or under conscious control. Never say “it’s all in your head.”","La belle indifférence":"Calmly discusses sudden blindness or paralysis. In conversion, the symptom may “solve” an unbearable conflict, so the anxiety is relieved. A clue to conversion, but assume an organic cause until it is ruled out.","Secondary gain":"Extra attention, being excused from duties, others taking over care. The benefits of the sick role reinforce the symptoms (not consciously). Reduce it: attend to the person at times other than complaints; encourage self-care."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-so-2","sourceId":"q5-so-2","q":"A patient with somatic symptom disorder keeps talking about severe headaches. Which intervention is appropriate?","o":["Hold a family meeting to confront him about the diagnosis","Teach alternative therapies for pain only","Tell him the headaches aren’t real","After a brief discussion of physical concerns, shift the focus to feelings and coping"],"a":[3],"r":["Not supported for this question. Acknowledge, then redirect to feelings and coping; never confront or say it isn’t real.","Not supported for this question. Acknowledge, then redirect to feelings and coping; never confront or say it isn’t real.","Not supported for this question. Acknowledge, then redirect to feelings and coping; never confront or say it isn’t real.","Supported. Acknowledge, then redirect to feelings and coping; never confront or say it isn’t real."],"phys":"Acknowledge, then redirect to feelings and coping; never confront or say it isn’t real.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-so-3","sourceId":"q5-so-3","q":"After a bitter argument with her son, a woman develops severe neck pain with no physical cause. The nurse understands that she:","o":["Will become more obsessed if the nurse shows concern","Is consciously manipulating","Believes the cause is physical and will resist a psychological explanation","Is trying to make her son feel guilty"],"a":[2],"r":["Not supported for this question. Somatic symptoms are not conscious; patients believe they are physical.","Not supported for this question. Somatic symptoms are not conscious; patients believe they are physical.","Supported. Somatic symptoms are not conscious; patients believe they are physical.","Not supported for this question. Somatic symptoms are not conscious; patients believe they are physical."],"phys":"Somatic symptoms are not conscious; patients believe they are physical.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-somatic"},{"id":"c-did","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Dissociative identity disorder and amnesia","patient":"29-year-old admitted after arriving at the ED with deep cuts on her arms and no memory of how they happened. She describes “losing time,” finding clothes she doesn’t remember buying, and people calling her by another name. History of severe childhood abuse. She refers to herself as “we” at times.","notes":["c-did"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 16; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Self-injury that she can’t remember, plus alter behavior, means safety can’t be reliably judged from one conversation. The underlying cause is severe early trauma.","Priority: Priority: risk for self-harm and suicide, then disturbed identity, anxiety and role performance.","Act: Ensure safety (close observation, 1:1 if needed), give a simple, predictable routine, orient her to time, place and identity, teach grounding (feet on the floor, holding ice, 5-4-3-2-1, a shower), let memories come at her own pace (don’t flood her with details), support her through painful disclosures, and refer for phased trauma therapy (safety → processing → integration).","Reassess: Evaluate for no self-harm, fewer episodes of lost time, grounding used effectively, less anxiety and engagement in therapy."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Recognize dissociation: gaps in memory (“lost time”), depersonalization (detached from self) or derealization (world unreal), amnesia for personal information (sometimes with fugue: travel with a new identity), or two or more distinct identities (alters) that take control. Screen for self-harm and suicide (very high in DID), trauma history, PTSD, depression and substance use. Rule out seizures, head injury and substances. Self-injury that she can’t remember, plus alter behavior, means safety can’t be reliably judged from one conversation. The underlying cause is severe early trauma.","Priority: risk for self-harm and suicide, then disturbed identity, anxiety and role performance. Ensure safety (close observation, 1:1 if needed), give a simple, predictable routine, orient her to time, place and identity, teach grounding (feet on the floor, holding ice, 5-4-3-2-1, a shower), let memories come at her own pace (don’t flood her with details), support her through painful disclosures, and refer for phased trauma therapy (safety → processing → integration).","Evaluate for no self-harm, fewer episodes of lost time, grounding used effectively, less anxiety and engagement in therapy."],"requires":[],"terms":["Alters","Depersonalization and derealization","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Alters":"A change in voice, posture, name or handedness; using “we”; others report behavior she can’t recall. Severe, repeated childhood trauma before a unified identity forms; parts of experience are split off to survive it. Speak to whoever is present respectfully, but keep focusing on the whole person; safety covers all alters.","Depersonalization and derealization":"“I’m watching myself in a movie.” “Everything looks foggy and unreal.” The brain dampens emotion and sensation to escape overwhelming stress. Grounding brings her back to the present. Reality testing stays intact (unlike psychosis)."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-di-2","sourceId":"q5-di-2","q":"A mother asks if her daughter with a dissociative disorder is “crazy.” The best response is:","o":["“People with dissociative disorders are out of touch with reality.”","“She will need long-term inpatient care for her delusions.”","“Many providers doubt these disorders are real.”","“She is handling overwhelming anxiety by separating herself from it. With treatment she can get back to her previous functioning.”"],"a":[3],"r":["Not supported for this question. Dissociation is a protective response to trauma, not psychosis, and it is treatable.","Not supported for this question. Dissociation is a protective response to trauma, not psychosis, and it is treatable.","Not supported for this question. Dissociation is a protective response to trauma, not psychosis, and it is treatable.","Supported. Dissociation is a protective response to trauma, not psychosis, and it is treatable."],"phys":"Dissociation is a protective response to trauma, not psychosis, and it is treatable.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-di-3","sourceId":"q5-di-3","q":"Which techniques ground a patient who is dissociating? Select all that apply.","o":["Stomping her feet on the floor","Holding an ice cube","Naming 5 things she can see","Recounting every detail of the trauma right away","Taking a shower"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Supported. Grounding brings awareness back to the present. Flooding her with trauma details increases anxiety.","Supported. Grounding brings awareness back to the present. Flooding her with trauma details increases anxiety.","Supported. Grounding brings awareness back to the present. Flooding her with trauma details increases anxiety.","Not supported for this question. Grounding brings awareness back to the present. Flooding her with trauma details increases anxiety.","Supported. Grounding brings awareness back to the present. Flooding her with trauma details increases anxiety."],"phys":"Grounding brings awareness back to the present. Flooding her with trauma details increases anxiety.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-did"},{"id":"c-alcohol","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Alcohol withdrawal and delirium tremens","patient":"61-year-old admitted for detox after a DUI (court’s choice: detox or jail). Heavy daily drinker for years. About 8 hours since her last drink: hand tremor, foot bouncing, sweating, BP 168/98, HR 112, temperature 38.1 °C, “pins and needles” in her legs. She vomits, becomes confused and grabs for her alcohol-containing cough syrup.","notes":["c-alcohol"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 22; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Rising vital signs, tremor, vomiting and new confusion about 8 hours after stopping mean moderate to severe withdrawal progressing toward DTs, made riskier by older age, neuropathy (malnutrition) and depression. She is also at risk of seizures and Wernicke.","Priority: Priority: withdrawal and its dangers (seizures, DTs, injury). Then fluids and nutrition, then suicide risk (depression plus alcohol), then denial, coping and the family.","Act: Benzodiazepines per the CIWA-Ar protocol (chlordiazepoxide, lorazepam, diazepam), thiamine before glucose, fluids and nutrition, seizure precautions, a calm low-stimulation and well-lit room, frequent vital signs, reorientation, and remove alcohol-containing products (cough syrup, mouthwash). Then rehab, relapse prevention and AA / Al-Anon.","Reassess: Trend the CIWA-Ar score and vital signs, look for seizures and confusion, check intake and electrolytes, and later assess readiness for change, AA attendance and relapse-prevention skills."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Know the timeline: tremor (\"the shakes\") 6–8 hours after the last drink; hallucinations or psychosis from 8–10 hours; withdrawal seizures at 12–24 hours; delirium tremens (DTs) any time in the first 72 hours. Watch autonomic signs (↑ HR, BP and temperature, sweating), agitation, vomiting, confusion and visual or tactile hallucinations. Use the CIWA-Ar scale. Screen with CAGE or AUDIT. Rising vital signs, tremor, vomiting and new confusion about 8 hours after stopping mean moderate to severe withdrawal progressing toward DTs, made riskier by older age, neuropathy (malnutrition) and depression. She is also at risk of seizures and Wernicke.","Priority: withdrawal and its dangers (seizures, DTs, injury). Then fluids and nutrition, then suicide risk (depression plus alcohol), then denial, coping and the family. Benzodiazepines per the CIWA-Ar protocol (chlordiazepoxide, lorazepam, diazepam), thiamine before glucose, fluids and nutrition, seizure precautions, a calm low-stimulation and well-lit room, frequent vital signs, reorientation, and remove alcohol-containing products (cough syrup, mouthwash). Then rehab, relapse prevention and AA / Al-Anon.","Trend the CIWA-Ar score and vital signs, look for seizures and confusion, check intake and electrolytes, and later assess readiness for change, AA attendance and relapse-prevention skills."],"requires":[],"terms":["Withdrawal tremor and autonomic hyperactivity","Delirium tremens (DTs)","Wernicke encephalopathy","Tolerance","naltrexone","disulfiram","acamprosate","diazepam","lorazepam","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Withdrawal tremor and autonomic hyperactivity":"Shaky hands, sweating, racing pulse, high BP, fever, anxiety, insomnia. Long-term alcohol boosts GABA (the brake) and suppresses glutamate; the brain adapts. When alcohol stops, the brake is gone and the system rebounds into overexcitation. Treat early with a benzodiazepine, which replaces the missing GABA effect. Rising vital signs mean withdrawal is getting worse.","Delirium tremens (DTs)":"Confusion, disorientation, agitation, visual and tactile hallucinations (bugs on the skin), fever, sweating, tachycardia, fluctuating consciousness. The most severe withdrawal state; more likely with medical illness (pneumonia, pancreatitis, liver disease). Medical emergency: up to 20% die untreated. IV lorazepam, fluids, a safe low-stimulation room, close observation, seizure precautions.","Wernicke encephalopathy":"Confusion, ataxic gait, nystagmus and eye-movement palsies. Thiamine (B1) deficiency from poor diet and poor absorption. Give thiamine before glucose (glucose uses up the last thiamine). Untreated, it becomes Korsakoff syndrome: permanent memory loss with confabulation.","Tolerance":"Blood alcohol 0.31 but alert and talking normally. The brain and liver adapt, so it takes more alcohol for the same effect. A sign of physical dependence, and a warning that withdrawal will be significant."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-su-1","sourceId":"q5-su-1","q":"A patient taking disulfiram is suspected of drinking. Which findings support relapse? Select all that apply.","o":["Intense nausea","Diaphoresis","Acute paranoia","Confusion","Dyspnea"],"a":[0,1,3,4],"r":["Supported. Disulfiram + alcohol → nausea and vomiting, sweating, flushing, headache, dyspnea and confusion.","Supported. Disulfiram + alcohol → nausea and vomiting, sweating, flushing, headache, dyspnea and confusion.","Not supported for this question. Disulfiram + alcohol → nausea and vomiting, sweating, flushing, headache, dyspnea and confusion.","Supported. Disulfiram + alcohol → nausea and vomiting, sweating, flushing, headache, dyspnea and confusion.","Supported. Disulfiram + alcohol → nausea and vomiting, sweating, flushing, headache, dyspnea and confusion."],"phys":"Disulfiram + alcohol → nausea and vomiting, sweating, flushing, headache, dyspnea and confusion.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-su-10","sourceId":"q5-su-10","q":"A patient admitted for heavy drinking becomes confused and ataxic with nystagmus. Before IV dextrose, the nurse should give:","o":["Thiamine","Naloxone","Folic acid","Flumazenil"],"a":[0],"r":["Supported. Wernicke encephalopathy: thiamine first, because glucose uses up the remaining thiamine.","Not supported for this question. Wernicke encephalopathy: thiamine first, because glucose uses up the remaining thiamine.","Not supported for this question. Wernicke encephalopathy: thiamine first, because glucose uses up the remaining thiamine.","Not supported for this question. Wernicke encephalopathy: thiamine first, because glucose uses up the remaining thiamine."],"phys":"Wernicke encephalopathy: thiamine first, because glucose uses up the remaining thiamine.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-su-11","sourceId":"q5-su-11","q":"Within which time frame are alcohol withdrawal seizures most likely?","o":["1–2 hours after the last drink","12–24 hours after the last drink","5–7 days after the last drink","Only after 2 weeks"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Tremor at 6–8 h, hallucinations at 8–10 h, seizures at 12–24 h, DTs within 72 h.","Supported. Tremor at 6–8 h, hallucinations at 8–10 h, seizures at 12–24 h, DTs within 72 h.","Not supported for this question. Tremor at 6–8 h, hallucinations at 8–10 h, seizures at 12–24 h, DTs within 72 h.","Not supported for this question. Tremor at 6–8 h, hallucinations at 8–10 h, seizures at 12–24 h, DTs within 72 h."],"phys":"Tremor at 6–8 h, hallucinations at 8–10 h, seizures at 12–24 h, DTs within 72 h.","ex":[]}],"studio":"c-alcohol"},{"id":"c-opioid","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Opioid overdose and withdrawal","patient":"23-year-old found unresponsive in a bathroom. RR 6 and shallow, SpO₂ 82%, pinpoint pupils, BP 88/50, HR 54, fresh injection marks. After intranasal naloxone he wakes, angry, sweating, yawning, with runny nose and eyes, gooseflesh, cramps and vomiting.","notes":["c-opioid"],"source":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 22; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Classic opioid overdose, reversed by naloxone, which pushed him into precipitated withdrawal. Naloxone is short-acting, so the opioid (especially fentanyl or methadone) can outlast it, and he can stop breathing again.","Priority: Priority: airway and breathing (overdose), then re-sedation risk, then withdrawal comfort, then engagement in treatment and harm reduction.","Act: Airway and ventilation, naloxone (IN, IM, SubQ or IV; repeat as needed, since it is short-acting), continuous monitoring. For withdrawal: clonidine or lofexidine, then buprenorphine (only once in early withdrawal, 12–24 hours after the last opioid) or methadone; naltrexone after detox. Manage withdrawal well early on (it keeps people in treatment) and refer to NA.","Reassess: Continuous RR and SpO₂ until naloxone’s effect has clearly outlasted the opioid; a withdrawal score (e.g., COWS); and engagement in treatment (accepted buprenorphine or methadone, follow-up booked, naloxone kit given).","For suspected opioid overdose, activate emergency help, support breathing and give naloxone under the product directions/protocol. Continue observation and repeat dosing as indicated: respiratory depression can return after naloxone wears off."],"checks":["Identify the relevant findings and explain their mechanism.","Explain the priority, immediate actions and important precautions.","State the findings that show improvement or deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Intoxication or overdose: pinpoint pupils (miosis), sedation and head-nodding, slurred speech, respiratory depression, low BP, HR and temperature, decreased bowel sounds, track marks. The overdose triad: coma + pinpoint pupils + respiratory depression. Withdrawal is the opposite picture: dilated pupils, yawning, tearing, runny nose, gooseflesh (piloerection), sweating, ↑ HR and BP, cramps, vomiting, diarrhea, aches, anxiety, insomnia. Classic opioid overdose, reversed by naloxone, which pushed him into precipitated withdrawal. Naloxone is short-acting, so the opioid (especially fentanyl or methadone) can outlast it, and he can stop breathing again.","Priority: airway and breathing (overdose), then re-sedation risk, then withdrawal comfort, then engagement in treatment and harm reduction. Airway and ventilation, naloxone (IN, IM, SubQ or IV; repeat as needed, since it is short-acting), continuous monitoring. For withdrawal: clonidine or lofexidine, then buprenorphine (only once in early withdrawal, 12–24 hours after the last opioid) or methadone; naltrexone after detox. Manage withdrawal well early on (it keeps people in treatment) and refer to NA.","Continuous RR and SpO₂ until naloxone’s effect has clearly outlasted the opioid; a withdrawal score (e.g., COWS); and engagement in treatment (accepted buprenorphine or methadone, follow-up booked, naloxone kit given)."],"requires":[],"terms":["Respiratory depression","Withdrawal (cold turkey)","naloxone","naltrexone","buprenorphine","methadone","methylphenidate","lorazepam","alprazolam"],"termDefinitions":{"Respiratory depression":"RR 6, shallow, SpO₂ dropping, cyanosis. Opioids act on mu receptors in the brainstem’s breathing center, making it less responsive to CO₂. The cause of death in overdose: airway, breathing (ventilate), naloxone.","Withdrawal (cold turkey)":"Gooseflesh, yawning, tears, runny nose, dilated pupils, cramps, diarrhea, sweating. Rebound of the systems that opioids suppressed (sympathetic overactivity). Very uncomfortable but rarely life-threatening (unlike alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal). Starts 6–8 hours after heroin; peaks on days 2–3."},"questions":[{"id":"q5-su-2","sourceId":"q5-su-2","q":"Which findings confirm opioid withdrawal? Select all that apply.","o":["Dilated pupils","Pulse 62","Slow movements","Extreme anxiety","Sleepiness"],"a":[0,3],"r":["Supported. Withdrawal = dilated pupils, anxiety, ↑ HR, yawning, tearing. Slow pulse, slow movement and sleepiness fit intoxication.","Not supported for this question. Withdrawal = dilated pupils, anxiety, ↑ HR, yawning, tearing. Slow pulse, slow movement and sleepiness fit intoxication.","Not supported for this question. Withdrawal = dilated pupils, anxiety, ↑ HR, yawning, tearing. Slow pulse, slow movement and sleepiness fit intoxication.","Supported. Withdrawal = dilated pupils, anxiety, ↑ HR, yawning, tearing. Slow pulse, slow movement and sleepiness fit intoxication.","Not supported for this question. Withdrawal = dilated pupils, anxiety, ↑ HR, yawning, tearing. Slow pulse, slow movement and sleepiness fit intoxication."],"phys":"Withdrawal = dilated pupils, anxiety, ↑ HR, yawning, tearing. Slow pulse, slow movement and sleepiness fit intoxication.","ex":[]},{"id":"q5-su-12","sourceId":"q5-su-12","q":"After naloxone reverses an opioid overdose, why must the nurse keep monitoring?","o":["Naloxone causes hypertension","Naloxone may wear off before the opioid, and respiratory depression can return","Naloxone is addictive","It causes delayed seizures in every patient"],"a":[1],"r":["Not supported for this question. Naloxone is short-acting; fentanyl and methadone last longer.","Supported. Naloxone is short-acting; fentanyl and methadone last longer.","Not supported for this question. Naloxone is short-acting; fentanyl and methadone last longer.","Not supported for this question. Naloxone is short-acting; fentanyl and methadone last longer."],"phys":"Naloxone is short-acting; fentanyl and methadone last longer.","ex":[]}],"clinicalUpdate":"For suspected opioid overdose, activate emergency help, support breathing and give naloxone under the product directions/protocol. Continue observation and repeat dosing as indicated: respiratory depression can return after naloxone wears off.","studio":"c-opioid"}],"studios":{"version":1,"mapPage":"Exam 5 Map.html","learnPage":"Exam 5 Learn.html","labs":[{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"The schizophrenia spectrum","cards":[{"title":"Types of delusions","text":"Persecutory: “They’re poisoning my food.” · Referential (ideas of reference): “The radio is sending me messages.” · Grandiose: “I’m a famous playwright.”"},{"title":"Perception words","text":"Hallucinations: auditory (most common, about 70%), visual, olfactory, gustatory, tactile (the last three suggest a medical or substance cause). · Illusion: misreading something real (a coat looks like a bear). · Depersonalization (detached from self) and derealization (the world seems unreal)."},{"title":"Behavior words","text":"Catatonia: little or no movement, rigidity, waxy flexibility (stays in the position you place); or purposeless excess activity. · Echopraxia: copying movements. Negativism: resisting all requests. · Stereotyped behaviors, posturing, grimacing, impaired impulse control, boundary impairment."}],"caption":"Symptom groups, speech and delusion terms, phases, and how to talk with someone in psychosis. See the highlighted clinical update below."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Two required elements for a schizophrenia diagnosis besides duration?","cue":"Symptom groups, speech and delusion terms, phases, and how to talk with someone in psychosis.","rule":"At least 2 symptoms, one of which must be delusions, hallucinations or disorganized speech; functioning below baseline; 6 months.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-spectrum","ch":"12","chapters":["12"],"title":"The schizophrenia spectrum","notes":["t-spectrum"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 12; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Psychosis means altered perception or thinking and an impaired ability to tell what is real (reality testing). Schizophrenia affects about 1% of people, usually starting 15–25 in men and 25–35 in women. Its symptoms fall into four groups: positive, negative, cognitive and affective. Positive symptoms respond best to medication; negative and cognitive symptoms are more disabling.","Types of delusions: Persecutory: “They’re poisoning my food.”; Referential (ideas of reference): “The radio is sending me messages.”; Grandiose: “I’m a famous playwright.”; Erotomanic: “He secretly loves me.”; Nihilistic: “A comet will end everything; I’m giving my things away.”; Somatic: “My heart is rotting away.”; Control: “Aliens control my thoughts.” (Thought insertion and thought broadcasting are related.); Beliefs that come from one’s culture or religion are not delusions.","Perception words: Hallucinations: auditory (most common, about 70%), visual, olfactory, gustatory, tactile (the last three suggest a medical or substance cause).; Illusion: misreading something real (a coat looks like a bear).; Depersonalization (detached from self) and derealization (the world seems unreal).","Assess the individual’s actual symptoms, intent, access to means, substance use and protective factors. Most people with schizophrenia are not violent; a diagnosis alone is not a violence prediction."],"terms":["Positive symptoms","Negative symptoms","Cognitive symptoms","Affective symptoms","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Positive symptoms":"Hallucinations, delusions, paranoia, disorganized speech and behavior. Things added that shouldn’t be there; linked to excess dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway. Usually what brings the person to care; respond best to antipsychotics.","Negative symptoms":"Anhedonia, avolition, asociality, affective blunting, apathy, alogia (the six A’s). Normal functions taken away; linked to low frontal-lobe activity. Harder to treat; they cause poor hygiene and isolation (not laziness).","Cognitive symptoms":"Poor memory and attention, concrete thinking, poor judgment and planning (executive function), anosognosia. Frontal cortex and hippocampus changes. Use repetition, visual cues and concrete examples; break tasks into steps.","Affective symptoms":"Labile, incongruent or inappropriate mood; depression. Emotional regulation is affected, plus the stress of a chronic illness. Depression raises suicide risk (5–10% die by suicide)."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-spectrum","title":"The schizophrenia spectrum","blocks":[{"title":"Types of delusions","lines":["Persecutory: “They’re poisoning my food.”","Referential (ideas of reference): “The radio is sending me messages.”","Grandiose: “I’m a famous playwright.”","Erotomanic: “He secretly loves me.”","Nihilistic: “A comet will end everything; I’m giving my things away.”","Somatic: “My heart is rotting away.”","Control: “Aliens control my thoughts.” (Thought insertion and thought broadcasting are related.)","Beliefs that come from one’s culture or religion are not delusions."]},{"title":"Perception words","lines":["Hallucinations: auditory (most common, about 70%), visual, olfactory, gustatory, tactile (the last three suggest a medical or substance cause).","Illusion: misreading something real (a coat looks like a bear).","Depersonalization (detached from self) and derealization (the world seems unreal)."]},{"title":"Behavior words","lines":["Catatonia: little or no movement, rigidity, waxy flexibility (stays in the position you place); or purposeless excess activity.","Echopraxia: copying movements. Negativism: resisting all requests.","Stereotyped behaviors, posturing, grimacing, impaired impulse control, boundary impairment."]},{"title":"Assessment guidelines","lines":["Medical work-up first (delirium, intoxication, withdrawal, anticholinergic or digitalis toxicity, fever, head injury).","Ask “What do you hear?” and ask about commands and whether she believes them.","Ask whether delusions are firmly held and involve danger.","Suicide risk, substance use, self-care, sleep, intake, medication adherence and side effects, family knowledge."]},{"title":"Care across the phases","lines":["Acute: safety and stabilization, often in hospital; a structured milieu; short, frequent contacts (under 30 minutes).","Stabilization: learn about the illness, manage side effects, cope with symptoms.","Maintenance: relapse prevention, adherence, work, housing, NAMI, clubhouses, case management, the recovery model (hope and a full life).","Therapies: CBT, cognitive remediation, social skills, family therapy, support groups."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Assess the individual’s actual symptoms, intent, access to means, substance use and protective factors. Most people with schizophrenia are not violent; a diagnosis alone is not a violence prediction.","sources":[{"title":"NIMH: schizophrenia, symptoms and individualized risk","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/schizophrenia"}],"steps":[{"id":"t-spectrum:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient had hallucinations and disorganized speech for 2 weeks after a severe stressor, then returned completely to normal. This fits:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Schizophrenia","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Brief psychotic disorder lasts more than 1 day and less than 1 month, followed by a full return to functioning."},{"id":"o1","text":"Schizophreniform disorder","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Brief psychotic disorder lasts more than 1 day and less than 1 month, followed by a full return to functioning."},{"id":"o2","text":"Brief psychotic disorder","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Brief psychotic disorder lasts more than 1 day and less than 1 month, followed by a full return to functioning."},{"id":"o3","text":"Delusional disorder","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Brief psychotic disorder lasts more than 1 day and less than 1 month, followed by a full return to functioning."}]},{"id":"t-spectrum:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Two required elements for a schizophrenia diagnosis besides duration?","answer":"At least 2 symptoms, one of which must be delusions, hallucinations or disorganized speech; functioning below baseline; 6 months.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-spectrum:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Psychosis for 3 weeks, then full recovery?","answer":"Brief psychotic disorder (more than 1 day, less than 1 month).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Antipsychotic medications","cards":[{"title":"First vs second generation","text":"First (typical): haloperidol, fluphenazine, chlorpromazine, perphenazine. Positive symptoms; high EPS and TD; cheap. · Second (atypical): risperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, ziprasidone, aripiprazole, lurasidone, clozapine. Positive + some negative symptoms; less EPS; metabolic syndrome. · Partial D2 agonists (aripiprazole, brexpiprazole, cariprazine): low prolactin."},{"title":"EPS by timing","text":"Acute dystonia (hours to days): muscle spasms of the neck, eyes or tongue → benztropine or diphenhydramine IM; watch the airway. · Akathisia (days to weeks): restlessness → propranolol or a benzodiazepine; don’t mistake it for anxiety. · Pseudoparkinsonism (weeks): tremor, shuffling, mask face → benztropine or trihexyphenidyl."},{"title":"Dangerous effects","text":"NMS: rigidity, fever, altered mental status, unstable vitals → hold, call stat, cool, fluids, ICU; dantrolene or bromocriptine. · Anticholinergic toxicity: hot dry skin, dilated pupils, urinary retention, ileus, delirium → hold, cool, specialist-directed physostigmine (after contraindication review, with cardiac monitoring). · Severe neutropenia (ANC under 500; most with clozapine): fever, sore throat → hold, notify."}],"caption":"First vs second generation, EPS, NMS, metabolic syndrome, clozapine, and long-acting injectables. See the highlighted clinical update below."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Which pathway causes EPS when D2 is blocked?","cue":"First vs second generation, EPS, NMS, metabolic syndrome, clozapine, and long-acting injectables.","rule":"Nigrostriatal.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-antipsychotics","ch":"3","chapters":["3","12"],"title":"Antipsychotic medications","notes":["t-antipsychotics"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapters 3 and 12; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["All antipsychotics block dopamine D2 receptors (second-generation drugs also block serotonin 5-HT2A). Blocking D2 in the mesolimbic pathway treats psychosis; blocking it in the nigrostriatal pathway causes EPS, and in the tuberoinfundibular pathway raises prolactin. Blocking H1, M1 and α1 adds sedation and weight gain, anticholinergic effects and orthostasis.","First vs second generation: First (typical): haloperidol, fluphenazine, chlorpromazine, perphenazine. Positive symptoms; high EPS and TD; cheap.; Second (atypical): risperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, ziprasidone, aripiprazole, lurasidone, clozapine. Positive + some negative symptoms; less EPS; metabolic syndrome.; Partial D2 agonists (aripiprazole, brexpiprazole, cariprazine): low prolactin.; Onset 2–6 weeks; taper off; not addictive; rarely lethal in overdose.","EPS by timing: Acute dystonia (hours to days): muscle spasms of the neck, eyes or tongue → benztropine or diphenhydramine IM; watch the airway.; Akathisia (days to weeks): restlessness → propranolol or a benzodiazepine; don’t mistake it for anxiety.; Pseudoparkinsonism (weeks): tremor, shuffling, mask face → benztropine or trihexyphenidyl.; Tardive dyskinesia (months to years): lip smacking, tongue writhing; often permanent → AIMS every 3 months; valbenazine or deutetrabenazine.","FDA removed the clozapine REMS effective June 13, 2025. Severe neutropenia remains a risk: continue ANC monitoring per the current prescribing information and promptly assess signs of infection. REMS removal did not remove the monitoring need."],"terms":["clozapine","olanzapine","quetiapine","aripiprazole","benztropine","dantrolene","haloperidol","risperidone","paliperidone","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-antipsychotics","title":"Antipsychotic medications","blocks":[{"title":"First vs second generation","lines":["First (typical): haloperidol, fluphenazine, chlorpromazine, perphenazine. Positive symptoms; high EPS and TD; cheap.","Second (atypical): risperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, ziprasidone, aripiprazole, lurasidone, clozapine. Positive + some negative symptoms; less EPS; metabolic syndrome.","Partial D2 agonists (aripiprazole, brexpiprazole, cariprazine): low prolactin.","Onset 2–6 weeks; taper off; not addictive; rarely lethal in overdose."]},{"title":"EPS by timing","lines":["Acute dystonia (hours to days): muscle spasms of the neck, eyes or tongue → benztropine or diphenhydramine IM; watch the airway.","Akathisia (days to weeks): restlessness → propranolol or a benzodiazepine; don’t mistake it for anxiety.","Pseudoparkinsonism (weeks): tremor, shuffling, mask face → benztropine or trihexyphenidyl.","Tardive dyskinesia (months to years): lip smacking, tongue writhing; often permanent → AIMS every 3 months; valbenazine or deutetrabenazine."]},{"title":"Dangerous effects","lines":["NMS: rigidity, fever, altered mental status, unstable vitals → hold, call stat, cool, fluids, ICU; dantrolene or bromocriptine.","Anticholinergic toxicity: hot dry skin, dilated pupils, urinary retention, ileus, delirium → hold, cool, specialist-directed physostigmine (after contraindication review, with cardiac monitoring).","Severe neutropenia (ANC under 500; most with clozapine): fever, sore throat → hold, notify.","QT prolongation (ziprasidone, haloperidol, quetiapine): get an ECG first.","Liver injury (jaundice, dark urine), seizures, choking, falls."]},{"title":"Clozapine","lines":["For treatment-resistant schizophrenia (2 failed drugs); lowers suicide; best efficacy.","Clozapine REMS was removed in 2025; ANC monitoring continues per labeling. Benign ethnic neutropenia: get a baseline ANC.","Also: seizures, myocarditis, constipation and bowel emergencies, drooling, sedation, weight gain and diabetes.","Smoking lowers its level; quitting smoking raises it."]},{"title":"Metabolic syndrome","lines":["Weight gain (especially abdominal), high glucose and insulin resistance, high lipids; highest with clozapine and olanzapine.","Monitor weight, waist, BP, glucose and lipids; teach diet and activity; metformin may be used."]},{"title":"Long-acting injectables","lines":["Haloperidol and fluphenazine decanoate (deep Z-track IM); risperidone (every 2 weeks; Perseris SubQ monthly); paliperidone (monthly, or every 3 months); aripiprazole (monthly); olanzapine pamoate (observe 3 hours afterward for sedation and delirium).","They improve adherence and reduce conflict over medication."]},{"title":"Side-effect teaching","lines":["Dry mouth: sips of water, ice chips, sugarless gum. Constipation: fluids, fiber, activity. Urinary hesitancy: report retention.","Orthostasis: rise slowly; hold if standing systolic is below 80.","Sedation: take at bedtime; no driving until the effect is known.","Photosensitivity (chlorpromazine): sunscreen.","Report fever, sore throat, stiffness, confusion or new movements right away."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"FDA removed the clozapine REMS effective June 13, 2025. Severe neutropenia remains a risk: continue ANC monitoring per the current prescribing information and promptly assess signs of infection. REMS removal did not remove the monitoring need.","sources":[{"title":"FDA: clozapine REMS removed; ANC monitoring continues","url":"https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-safety-and-availability/fda-removes-risk-evaluation-and-mitigation-strategy-rems-program-antipsychotic-drug-clozapine"},{"title":"Utah Poison Control: physostigmine precautions and monitoring","url":"https://poisoncontrol.utah.edu/news/2024/02/physostigmine-or-rivastigmine-anticholinergic-toxicity"},{"title":"DailyMed: haloperidol labeling, NMS recognition and management","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b5a6c54c-fb70-4132-90ae-7d75b5e9c9de"}],"steps":[{"id":"t-antipsychotics:case","kind":"choice","scene":"For a patient taking a second-generation antipsychotic, the nurse should frequently assess for:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Alcohol use disorder","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Second-generation antipsychotics cause weight gain, high glucose and dyslipidemia."},{"id":"o1","text":"Stomach cancer","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Second-generation antipsychotics cause weight gain, high glucose and dyslipidemia."},{"id":"o2","text":"Polydipsia","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Second-generation antipsychotics cause weight gain, high glucose and dyslipidemia."},{"id":"o3","text":"Metabolic syndrome","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Second-generation antipsychotics cause weight gain, high glucose and dyslipidemia."}]},{"id":"t-antipsychotics:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Which pathway causes EPS when D2 is blocked?","answer":"Nigrostriatal.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-antipsychotics:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Tongue writhing after 3 years on haloperidol?","answer":"Tardive dyskinesia: AIMS, valbenazine or deutetrabenazine; often permanent.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Children and neurodevelopmental disorders","cards":[{"title":"Assessing a child","text":"History of the present illness, developmental history (milestones, play, attachment), medical and family history. · Mental status adapted to age, including characteristics of play; developmental screening (Denver II: social/personal, fine motor, language, gross motor). · Use therapeutic games, drawing and puppets for young children; a genogram for the family."},{"title":"Interventions","text":"Behavioral point systems; play therapy (feelings and trauma acted out with toys); bibliotherapy (stories); expressive arts, journaling, music; family therapy; CBT; groups. · Least → most restrictive: talk about it → offer PRN medication → time-out (the child can leave) → quiet or sensory room → seclusion → restraint. · Seclusion and restraint: constant monitoring, notify the parents, release as soon as safe, debrief with the child and the staff."},{"title":"The neurodevelopmental disorders","text":"Intellectual disability: deficits in intellectual, social and daily (adaptive) functioning, starting in childhood; causes include Down, fragile X, PKU, prenatal alcohol or drugs, infection, neglect. Focus on strengths, self-care and family support. · Communication disorders: language (expressive or receptive), speech sound, stuttering (childhood-onset fluency), social (pragmatic) communication. Hearing test first, then speech therapy. · Specific learning disorder: reading (dyslexia), math (dyscalculia), writing (dysgraphia); IEP under IDEA."}],"caption":"Assessing children, play therapy, least-restrictive behavior management, ID, tics, learning and communication disorders."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Best-structured limit-setting statement?","cue":"Assessing children, play therapy, least-restrictive behavior management, ID, tics, learning and communication disorders.","rule":"“Hitting me when you are angry is unacceptable.” (Specific, calm, not judgmental.)","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-kids","ch":"11","chapters":["11"],"title":"Children and neurodevelopmental disorders","notes":["t-kids"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 11; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["About 1 in 5 children has a mental disorder in a given year, and half of all lifetime cases start by age 14. Young children are harder to diagnose (limited language, fast development), so the nurse gathers data from many sources (parents, teachers, the child) and uses play to assess and treat. Behavior is managed with the least restrictive intervention first.","Assessing a child: History of the present illness, developmental history (milestones, play, attachment), medical and family history.; Mental status adapted to age, including characteristics of play; developmental screening (Denver II: social/personal, fine motor, language, gross motor).; Use therapeutic games, drawing and puppets for young children; a genogram for the family.; Screen for abuse and neglect: nurses are mandated reporters (neglect is the most common abuse).","Interventions: Behavioral point systems; play therapy (feelings and trauma acted out with toys); bibliotherapy (stories); expressive arts, journaling, music; family therapy; CBT; groups.; Least → most restrictive: talk about it → offer PRN medication → time-out (the child can leave) → quiet or sensory room → seclusion → restraint.; Seclusion and restraint: constant monitoring, notify the parents, release as soon as safe, debrief with the child and the staff."],"terms":["Resilience","Temperament","aripiprazole","haloperidol"],"termDefinitions":{"Resilience":"A neglected child who bonds with a kind neighbor, loves school and earns good grades. Adaptability, a nurturing adult outside the family, distance from family chaos, social skills, problem-solving and a sense of the future protect the child. Ask about supports, coping and future plans; build these strengths.","Temperament":"An easygoing vs an intense, shy or irritable baby. An inborn style of reacting; a poor fit with a parent’s temperament risks insecure attachment. Help parents understand and adapt to the child’s style."},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-kids","title":"Children and neurodevelopmental disorders","blocks":[{"title":"Assessing a child","lines":["History of the present illness, developmental history (milestones, play, attachment), medical and family history.","Mental status adapted to age, including characteristics of play; developmental screening (Denver II: social/personal, fine motor, language, gross motor).","Use therapeutic games, drawing and puppets for young children; a genogram for the family.","Screen for abuse and neglect: nurses are mandated reporters (neglect is the most common abuse)."]},{"title":"Interventions","lines":["Behavioral point systems; play therapy (feelings and trauma acted out with toys); bibliotherapy (stories); expressive arts, journaling, music; family therapy; CBT; groups.","Least → most restrictive: talk about it → offer PRN medication → time-out (the child can leave) → quiet or sensory room → seclusion → restraint.","Seclusion and restraint: constant monitoring, notify the parents, release as soon as safe, debrief with the child and the staff."]},{"title":"The neurodevelopmental disorders","lines":["Intellectual disability: deficits in intellectual, social and daily (adaptive) functioning, starting in childhood; causes include Down, fragile X, PKU, prenatal alcohol or drugs, infection, neglect. Focus on strengths, self-care and family support.","Communication disorders: language (expressive or receptive), speech sound, stuttering (childhood-onset fluency), social (pragmatic) communication. Hearing test first, then speech therapy.","Specific learning disorder: reading (dyslexia), math (dyscalculia), writing (dysgraphia); IEP under IDEA.","Developmental coordination disorder: clumsy, delayed motor skills → PT and OT.","Stereotypic movement disorder: repetitive rocking, head banging → safety (helmet), habit reversal.","Tics: provisional (under 1 year), persistent motor or vocal (over 1 year), Tourette (multiple motor plus at least one vocal tic, over 1 year; coprolalia in under 10%). Habit reversal (CBIT); haloperidol, pimozide, aripiprazole, guanfacine, clonidine; stimulants can worsen tics."]},{"title":"Laws and services","lines":["IDEA: early intervention from birth to 3, then special education to age 21; IEP.","Teens’ consent rules vary by state."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-kids:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which statement is a well-structured example of limit setting?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“Hitting me when you are angry is unacceptable.”","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Limit setting is specific, calm and nonjudgmental."},{"id":"o1","text":"“I expect you to behave yourself during dinner.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Limit setting is specific, calm and nonjudgmental."},{"id":"o2","text":"“Come here, right now!”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Limit setting is specific, calm and nonjudgmental."},{"id":"o3","text":"“Good boys don’t bite.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Limit setting is specific, calm and nonjudgmental."}]},{"id":"t-kids:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Best-structured limit-setting statement?","answer":"“Hitting me when you are angry is unacceptable.” (Specific, calm, not judgmental.)","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-kids:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Best activity for a child with ADHD?","answer":"A physical one with quick feedback, like tennis.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Childhood trauma and attachment","cards":[{"title":"Attachment disorders (severe early neglect)","text":"Reactive attachment disorder: inhibited and withdrawn; rarely seeks or accepts comfort. · Disinhibited social engagement disorder: no fear of strangers; overly friendly; would go off with a stranger. · Risks: institutions, frequent foster-home changes, impaired parents, long separations."},{"title":"PTSD in children","text":"Repetitive play reenacting the trauma; nightmares; regression; irritability, aggression; self-blame; withdrawal. · Imaginary friends can be a coping mechanism after trauma. · Interventions: trust and safety, developmentally appropriate words, relaxation before exploring the trauma, art and play, involve parents (unless they caused the trauma), mandatory reporting."},{"title":"Arousal: polyvagal theory and the window of tolerance","text":"Ventral vagal = calm and socially engaged; sympathetic = fight or flight; dorsal vagal = shutdown, numbness, dissociation (“playing dead”). · Traumatized people swing between hyperarousal (anxious, aggressive) and hypoarousal (numb, withdrawn). · The goal is to keep them within the window of tolerance using breathing, mindfulness, soothing and limits without harshness."}],"caption":"Reactive attachment and disinhibited social engagement disorders, PTSD in children, polyvagal theory, the window of tolerance."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"A child at the mall asks a stranger, “Maybe you’ll take me home?”","cue":"Reactive attachment and disinhibited social engagement disorders, PTSD in children, polyvagal theory, the window of tolerance.","rule":"Possible disinhibited social engagement disorder (a lack of early bonding).","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-attach","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Childhood trauma and attachment","notes":["t-attach"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 16; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Children depend on caregivers, and the developing brain changes with repeated stress (the HPA axis, amygdala and hippocampus), so early trauma and neglect can shape lifelong health. Treatment is phased: safety and stabilization, then calming arousal and working with memories, then catching up on skills. Relationships heal.","Attachment disorders (severe early neglect): Reactive attachment disorder: inhibited and withdrawn; rarely seeks or accepts comfort.; Disinhibited social engagement disorder: no fear of strangers; overly friendly; would go off with a stranger.; Risks: institutions, frequent foster-home changes, impaired parents, long separations.; Treat the child and the caregiver; a safe, stable home.","PTSD in children: Repetitive play reenacting the trauma; nightmares; regression; irritability, aggression; self-blame; withdrawal.; Imaginary friends can be a coping mechanism after trauma.; Interventions: trust and safety, developmentally appropriate words, relaxation before exploring the trauma, art and play, involve parents (unless they caused the trauma), mandatory reporting.; Treatments: CBT and EMDR (first line); medications only for specific symptoms."],"terms":["sertraline","paroxetine"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-attach","title":"Childhood trauma and attachment","blocks":[{"title":"Attachment disorders (severe early neglect)","lines":["Reactive attachment disorder: inhibited and withdrawn; rarely seeks or accepts comfort.","Disinhibited social engagement disorder: no fear of strangers; overly friendly; would go off with a stranger.","Risks: institutions, frequent foster-home changes, impaired parents, long separations.","Treat the child and the caregiver; a safe, stable home."]},{"title":"PTSD in children","lines":["Repetitive play reenacting the trauma; nightmares; regression; irritability, aggression; self-blame; withdrawal.","Imaginary friends can be a coping mechanism after trauma.","Interventions: trust and safety, developmentally appropriate words, relaxation before exploring the trauma, art and play, involve parents (unless they caused the trauma), mandatory reporting.","Treatments: CBT and EMDR (first line); medications only for specific symptoms."]},{"title":"Arousal: polyvagal theory and the window of tolerance","lines":["Ventral vagal = calm and socially engaged; sympathetic = fight or flight; dorsal vagal = shutdown, numbness, dissociation (“playing dead”).","Traumatized people swing between hyperarousal (anxious, aggressive) and hypoarousal (numb, withdrawn).","The goal is to keep them within the window of tolerance using breathing, mindfulness, soothing and limits without harshness."]},{"title":"Other trauma-related disorders","lines":["Acute stress disorder: 3 days to 1 month after trauma; CBT reduces later PTSD.","Adjustment disorder: within 3 months of a stressor; complicated grief can be a type (more than 12 months of intense yearning).","In adults: SSRIs sertraline and paroxetine are FDA approved for PTSD; avoid antipsychotics for PTSD."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-attach:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A young child lost at a mall says to a stranger, “Maybe you’ll take me home with you?” This may be due to:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"A lack of bonding as an infant","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Indiscriminate friendliness with strangers suggests disinhibited social engagement disorder from early neglect."},{"id":"o1","text":"Healthy confidence","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Indiscriminate friendliness with strangers suggests disinhibited social engagement disorder from early neglect."},{"id":"o2","text":"Adequate parental bonding","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Indiscriminate friendliness with strangers suggests disinhibited social engagement disorder from early neglect."},{"id":"o3","text":"Normal parenting","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Indiscriminate friendliness with strangers suggests disinhibited social engagement disorder from early neglect."}]},{"id":"t-attach:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"A child at the mall asks a stranger, “Maybe you’ll take me home?”","answer":"Possible disinhibited social engagement disorder (a lack of early bonding).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-attach:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"An 8-year-old repeatedly “arrests” and scares classmates in play.","answer":"A possible sign of traumatization (reenactment).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Somatic, factitious and malingering","cards":[{"title":"The spectrum","text":"Somatic symptom disorder: real symptoms + excessive worry, more than 6 months; may be “with predominant pain.” · Illness anxiety disorder: fear of illness; few symptoms; care-seeking or care-avoidant. · Conversion: neurological loss; la belle indifférence; treat with reassurance, PT, CBT, hypnosis; don’t confront."},{"title":"Risk and protective factors","text":"ACEs and childhood abuse; stress; anxiety and depression; loneliness (as harmful as smoking 15 cigarettes a day). · Protective: resilient coping, self-compassion, an internal locus of control, social connection."},{"title":"Six keys for working with somatic patients","text":"One consistent provider (continuity). · Avoid unnecessary tests and procedures. · Brief, regular, scheduled visits."}],"caption":"Unconscious vs conscious symptoms, loneliness, self-compassion and the nurse’s approach."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Conscious faking to be a patient, with no outside reward?","cue":"Unconscious vs conscious symptoms, loneliness, self-compassion and the nurse’s approach.","rule":"Factitious disorder.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-somafact","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Somatic, factitious and malingering","notes":["t-somafact"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 17; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Somatization is distress expressed as physical symptoms. In somatic symptom, illness anxiety and conversion disorders the symptoms are not under conscious control and are real to the patient. In factitious disorder and malingering they are consciously produced, for the sick role or for an outside reward.","The spectrum: Somatic symptom disorder: real symptoms + excessive worry, more than 6 months; may be “with predominant pain.”; Illness anxiety disorder: fear of illness; few symptoms; care-seeking or care-avoidant.; Conversion: neurological loss; la belle indifférence; treat with reassurance, PT, CBT, hypnosis; don’t confront.; Psychological factors affecting a medical condition: stress, depression and loneliness worsening a real disease.; Factitious: consciously fakes or induces illness for the sick role; travels between hospitals; scars from many surgeries.; Factitious imposed on another: a caregiver induces illness in a dependent → report and protect the child.; Malingering: consciously fakes for external gain (disability money, drugs, avoiding duty).","Risk and protective factors: ACEs and childhood abuse; stress; anxiety and depression; loneliness (as harmful as smoking 15 cigarettes a day).; Protective: resilient coping, self-compassion, an internal locus of control, social connection."],"terms":[],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-somafact","title":"Somatic, factitious and malingering","blocks":[{"title":"The spectrum","lines":["Somatic symptom disorder: real symptoms + excessive worry, more than 6 months; may be “with predominant pain.”","Illness anxiety disorder: fear of illness; few symptoms; care-seeking or care-avoidant.","Conversion: neurological loss; la belle indifférence; treat with reassurance, PT, CBT, hypnosis; don’t confront.","Psychological factors affecting a medical condition: stress, depression and loneliness worsening a real disease.","Factitious: consciously fakes or induces illness for the sick role; travels between hospitals; scars from many surgeries.","Factitious imposed on another: a caregiver induces illness in a dependent → report and protect the child.","Malingering: consciously fakes for external gain (disability money, drugs, avoiding duty)."]},{"title":"Risk and protective factors","lines":["ACEs and childhood abuse; stress; anxiety and depression; loneliness (as harmful as smoking 15 cigarettes a day).","Protective: resilient coping, self-compassion, an internal locus of control, social connection."]},{"title":"Six keys for working with somatic patients","lines":["One consistent provider (continuity).","Avoid unnecessary tests and procedures.","Brief, regular, scheduled visits.","Always do a physical exam.","Never say “it’s all in your head.”","Set reasonable goals (keep functioning despite pain)."]},{"title":"Culture","lines":["Many cultures express distress physically (e.g., hwa-byung: Korean suppressed anger with epigastric pain).","Immigrants and refugees may present trauma as physical symptoms."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-somafact:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which statement about conversion disorder is true?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Patients are extremely upset about their dramatic symptoms","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Imaging shows abnormal cerebral activation; patients often show la belle indifférence; it isn’t voluntary."},{"id":"o1","text":"Abnormal patterns of brain activation have been found","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Imaging shows abnormal cerebral activation; patients often show la belle indifférence; it isn’t voluntary."},{"id":"o2","text":"An organic cause is usually found","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Imaging shows abnormal cerebral activation; patients often show la belle indifférence; it isn’t voluntary."},{"id":"o3","text":"Patients can turn symptoms on and off at will","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Imaging shows abnormal cerebral activation; patients often show la belle indifférence; it isn’t voluntary."}]},{"id":"t-somafact:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Conscious faking to be a patient, with no outside reward?","answer":"Factitious disorder.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-somafact:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Faking back pain to get disability money?","answer":"Malingering.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Eating and feeding disorders","cards":[{"title":"Compare the three","text":"Anorexia: low weight, fear of gain, distorted image; restricting or binge/purge type; often denies a problem (ego-syntonic). · Bulimia: binge + compensating at least weekly for 3 months; normal weight; ashamed and wants help. · Binge-eating disorder: binges with distress and no compensating; often obese; the most common eating disorder."},{"title":"Hospitalize when…","text":"Anorexia: under 75% of ideal weight, under 10% body fat, HR under 50, systolic under 90, temperature under 96 °F, arrhythmias, severe electrolyte imbalance, suicide risk. · Bulimia: syncope, K⁺ under 3.2, Cl⁻ under 88, esophageal tears, arrhythmias, intractable vomiting, hematemesis, suicide risk."},{"title":"Medications","text":"Anorexia: no FDA-approved drug; treat comorbid conditions. · Bulimia: fluoxetine (FDA approved, higher dose); no bupropion (seizures). · Binge eating: lisdexamfetamine (Vyvanse) is FDA approved (watch BP, HR and misuse); SNRIs, topiramate."}],"caption":"Anorexia vs bulimia vs binge eating, the labs, refeeding, and pica, rumination and ARFID."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"“I’m ashamed, but I eat my hair.”","cue":"Anorexia vs bulimia vs binge eating, the labs, refeeding, and pica, rumination and ARFID.","rule":"Pica.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-feeding","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Eating and feeding disorders","notes":["t-feeding"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 18; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Eating disorders have the highest death rate of the psychiatric disorders. They lie on a spectrum and can shift (restriction → binge → binge-purge). They are about 50–60% heritable, involve changes in the brain’s reward and body-signal circuits, and are fed by thin-ideal culture and trauma. Medical stability comes first.","Compare the three: Anorexia: low weight, fear of gain, distorted image; restricting or binge/purge type; often denies a problem (ego-syntonic).; Bulimia: binge + compensating at least weekly for 3 months; normal weight; ashamed and wants help.; Binge-eating disorder: binges with distress and no compensating; often obese; the most common eating disorder.","Hospitalize when…: Anorexia: under 75% of ideal weight, under 10% body fat, HR under 50, systolic under 90, temperature under 96 °F, arrhythmias, severe electrolyte imbalance, suicide risk.; Bulimia: syncope, K⁺ under 3.2, Cl⁻ under 88, esophageal tears, arrhythmias, intractable vomiting, hematemesis, suicide risk."],"terms":["bupropion","fluoxetine"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-feeding","title":"Eating and feeding disorders","blocks":[{"title":"Compare the three","lines":["Anorexia: low weight, fear of gain, distorted image; restricting or binge/purge type; often denies a problem (ego-syntonic).","Bulimia: binge + compensating at least weekly for 3 months; normal weight; ashamed and wants help.","Binge-eating disorder: binges with distress and no compensating; often obese; the most common eating disorder."]},{"title":"Hospitalize when…","lines":["Anorexia: under 75% of ideal weight, under 10% body fat, HR under 50, systolic under 90, temperature under 96 °F, arrhythmias, severe electrolyte imbalance, suicide risk.","Bulimia: syncope, K⁺ under 3.2, Cl⁻ under 88, esophageal tears, arrhythmias, intractable vomiting, hematemesis, suicide risk."]},{"title":"Medications","lines":["Anorexia: no FDA-approved drug; treat comorbid conditions.","Bulimia: fluoxetine (FDA approved, higher dose); no bupropion (seizures).","Binge eating: lisdexamfetamine (Vyvanse) is FDA approved (watch BP, HR and misuse); SNRIs, topiramate.","Bariatric surgery is controversial with binge eating (binges can return)."]},{"title":"Binge-eating care","lines":["It’s about coping with emotion, not food: track triggers and feelings.","Small, regular meals (restriction triggers binges); slow, steady movement.","Use non-stigmatizing words (“unhealthy weight,” not “heavy”).","CBT, DBT, interpersonal therapy; address gastrointestinal symptoms and social isolation."]},{"title":"Feeding disorders","lines":["Pica: eating nonfood items (dirt, paint, hair) past toddlerhood → lead poisoning, bowel obstruction, infection; more common with intellectual disability; monitor and reward appropriate eating.","Rumination: regurgitating, rechewing and reswallowing or spitting food, with no GI cause, for 1 month or more → malnutrition; repositioning, pleasant meals, distraction.","Avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID): avoids food (sensory dislikes, fear) → weight loss, deficiencies, tube feeding; no body-image distortion; behavior modification."]},{"title":"Helpful extras","lines":["Yoga before meals lowers premeal anxiety and negative mood.","Ask about faith and culture; personal beliefs can be strengths."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-feeding:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which statement fits a diagnosis of pica?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“Nothing could make me drink milk.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Pica = eating nonfood items. Rechewing is rumination."},{"id":"o1","text":"“I’m ashamed of it, but I eat my hair.”","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Pica = eating nonfood items. Rechewing is rumination."},{"id":"o2","text":"“I haven’t eaten a green vegetable since I was 3.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Pica = eating nonfood items. Rechewing is rumination."},{"id":"o3","text":"“I bring up and rechew my food after most meals.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Pica = eating nonfood items. Rechewing is rumination."}]},{"id":"t-feeding:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"“I’m ashamed, but I eat my hair.”","answer":"Pica.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-feeding:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Lab danger in bulimia?","answer":"Hypokalemia (and arrhythmias).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Substance use and addiction","cards":[{"title":"Intoxication vs withdrawal, by substance","text":"Alcohol: slurred speech, poor coordination → withdrawal: tremor (6–8 h), seizures (12–24 h), DTs (within 72 h). Dangerous. · Sedatives and benzodiazepines: slurred speech, ataxia, respiratory depression with alcohol or opioids → withdrawal: anxiety, tremor, insomnia, seizures. Dangerous: taper. · Opioids: pinpoint pupils, sedation, low RR → withdrawal: dilated pupils, yawning, tearing, runny nose, gooseflesh, cramps, diarrhea. Miserable but rarely deadly."},{"title":"Screening and assessment","text":"SBIRT: screening, brief intervention, referral to treatment. · CAGE (2 or more = significant), CAGE-AID (adds drugs), AUDIT, T-ACE, CIWA-Ar for alcohol withdrawal, urine drug screens. · Assess co-occurring mental illness, trauma history, family history, strengths and readiness to change."},{"title":"Alcohol facts","text":"Standard drink = 14 g alcohol (12 oz beer, 5 oz wine, 1.5 oz liquor). · Binge drinking: 4+ drinks (women) or 5+ (men) in about 2 hours. Heavy: 8+ per week (women), 15+ per week (men). · Alcohol use disorder: 2 or more of 11 criteria in 12 months (mild 2–3, moderate 4–5, severe 6+)."}],"caption":"Intoxication and withdrawal for every substance, the DEA schedules, gambling, and the care continuum."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Pinpoint pupils + coma + RR 6?","cue":"Intoxication and withdrawal for every substance, the DEA schedules, gambling, and the care continuum.","rule":"Opioid overdose: airway, naloxone, watch for re-sedation.","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-substances","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Substance use and addiction","notes":["t-substances"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 22; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Addiction is a chronic, relapsing brain disease: craving, seeking and using despite harm. Drugs hijack the dopamine reward pathway (ventral tegmental area), plus the opioid and GABA systems. Know four words: addiction, intoxication (being under the influence), tolerance (needing more for the same effect) and withdrawal (the substance-specific syndrome when stopping). Process addictions like gambling use the same reward circuits.","Intoxication vs withdrawal, by substance: Alcohol: slurred speech, poor coordination → withdrawal: tremor (6–8 h), seizures (12–24 h), DTs (within 72 h). Dangerous.; Sedatives and benzodiazepines: slurred speech, ataxia, respiratory depression with alcohol or opioids → withdrawal: anxiety, tremor, insomnia, seizures. Dangerous: taper.; Opioids: pinpoint pupils, sedation, low RR → withdrawal: dilated pupils, yawning, tearing, runny nose, gooseflesh, cramps, diarrhea. Miserable but rarely deadly.; Stimulants (cocaine, amphetamines): euphoria, ↑ HR and BP, dilated pupils, chest pain, paranoia, “cocaine bugs” → withdrawal (“crash”): fatigue, hypersomnia, hunger, depression and suicidal thoughts.; Cannabis: red eyes, hunger, dry mouth, tachycardia, slowed time → withdrawal (after about a week): irritability, insomnia, poor appetite.; Hallucinogens (LSD): perceptual changes, synesthesia, dilated pupils → “talk them down”; flashbacks (hallucinogen persisting perception disorder).; PCP: violence, nystagmus, high BP, numbness to pain, hyperthermia → can’t be talked down; low stimulation, benzodiazepine, restraint if needed.; Inhalants (glue, aerosols, gasoline): disinhibition, nystagmus → “sudden sniffing death” (arrhythmia); mostly teens.; Tobacco: withdrawal: irritability, anxiety, poor concentration, insomnia, hunger. Treat with nicotine replacement, bupropion, varenicline.; Caffeine: over 250 mg → restlessness, tachycardia, GI upset; overdose (energy drinks) → arrhythmia, seizures; withdrawal headache within 12–24 h.","Screening and assessment: SBIRT: screening, brief intervention, referral to treatment.; CAGE (2 or more = significant), CAGE-AID (adds drugs), AUDIT, T-ACE, CIWA-Ar for alcohol withdrawal, urine drug screens.; Assess co-occurring mental illness, trauma history, family history, strengths and readiness to change.; Look at your own attitudes: addiction is a disease, not a choice."],"terms":["bupropion","naloxone","naltrexone","buprenorphine","disulfiram"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-substances","title":"Substance use and addiction","blocks":[{"title":"Intoxication vs withdrawal, by substance","lines":["Alcohol: slurred speech, poor coordination → withdrawal: tremor (6–8 h), seizures (12–24 h), DTs (within 72 h). Dangerous.","Sedatives and benzodiazepines: slurred speech, ataxia, respiratory depression with alcohol or opioids → withdrawal: anxiety, tremor, insomnia, seizures. Dangerous: taper.","Opioids: pinpoint pupils, sedation, low RR → withdrawal: dilated pupils, yawning, tearing, runny nose, gooseflesh, cramps, diarrhea. Miserable but rarely deadly.","Stimulants (cocaine, amphetamines): euphoria, ↑ HR and BP, dilated pupils, chest pain, paranoia, “cocaine bugs” → withdrawal (“crash”): fatigue, hypersomnia, hunger, depression and suicidal thoughts.","Cannabis: red eyes, hunger, dry mouth, tachycardia, slowed time → withdrawal (after about a week): irritability, insomnia, poor appetite.","Hallucinogens (LSD): perceptual changes, synesthesia, dilated pupils → “talk them down”; flashbacks (hallucinogen persisting perception disorder).","PCP: violence, nystagmus, high BP, numbness to pain, hyperthermia → can’t be talked down; low stimulation, benzodiazepine, restraint if needed.","Inhalants (glue, aerosols, gasoline): disinhibition, nystagmus → “sudden sniffing death” (arrhythmia); mostly teens.","Tobacco: withdrawal: irritability, anxiety, poor concentration, insomnia, hunger. Treat with nicotine replacement, bupropion, varenicline.","Caffeine: over 250 mg → restlessness, tachycardia, GI upset; overdose (energy drinks) → arrhythmia, seizures; withdrawal headache within 12–24 h."]},{"title":"Screening and assessment","lines":["SBIRT: screening, brief intervention, referral to treatment.","CAGE (2 or more = significant), CAGE-AID (adds drugs), AUDIT, T-ACE, CIWA-Ar for alcohol withdrawal, urine drug screens.","Assess co-occurring mental illness, trauma history, family history, strengths and readiness to change.","Look at your own attitudes: addiction is a disease, not a choice."]},{"title":"Alcohol facts","lines":["Standard drink = 14 g alcohol (12 oz beer, 5 oz wine, 1.5 oz liquor).","Binge drinking: 4+ drinks (women) or 5+ (men) in about 2 hours. Heavy: 8+ per week (women), 15+ per week (men).","Alcohol use disorder: 2 or more of 11 criteria in 12 months (mild 2–3, moderate 4–5, severe 6+).","40–60% genetic; flushing genes (aldehyde dehydrogenase) protect.","Blackouts: alcohol blocks new memories forming (hippocampus)."]},{"title":"Gambling disorder","lines":["Preoccupied, chases losses, lies, borrows, may commit crimes; it activates the reward pathway like a drug.","Gamblers Anonymous, CBT; SSRIs, mood stabilizers, naltrexone for severe cases.","Progress sounds like: “Gambling activates the reward pathways in my brain.”"]},{"title":"Teens and vaping","lines":["E-cigarettes: rapid spread among youth, flavored products, lung injury; the FDA banned most flavored cartridges in 2020.","Today’s cannabis is far more potent (THC 17–28% vs under 2% before the 1990s)."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-substances:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A nurse notices a colleague showing signs of a substance use disorder. What should the nurse do?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Accompany her during patient care","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Patient safety: report to the supervisor; peer assistance (alternative-to-discipline) programs follow."},{"id":"o1","text":"Offer to attend counseling with her","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Patient safety: report to the supervisor; peer assistance (alternative-to-discipline) programs follow."},{"id":"o2","text":"Refer her to peer assistance","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Patient safety: report to the supervisor; peer assistance (alternative-to-discipline) programs follow."},{"id":"o3","text":"Report the concerns to a supervisor immediately","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Patient safety: report to the supervisor; peer assistance (alternative-to-discipline) programs follow."}]},{"id":"t-substances:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Pinpoint pupils + coma + RR 6?","answer":"Opioid overdose: airway, naloxone, watch for re-sedation.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-substances:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Which two withdrawals can be fatal?","answer":"Alcohol and benzodiazepine/sedative withdrawal (seizures, DTs).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Neurocognitive disorders","cards":[{"title":"Compare","text":"Onset: delirium sudden (hours to days) · dementia slow (months to years) · depression gradual, worse with crisis. · Course: delirium fluctuates, worse at night · dementia progressive (sundowning) · depression worse in the morning. · Attention: impaired in delirium · intact early in dementia · poor concentration in depression."},{"title":"Types of dementia","text":"Alzheimer’s (60–80%): memory first. · Vascular (5–10%): stepwise after strokes; impaired judgment, slow gait. · Frontotemporal: onset 45–60; personality change and disinhibition first."},{"title":"Vocabulary","text":"Aphasia (language), apraxia (purposeful movement), agnosia (recognizing objects or people), agraphia (reading and writing). · Confabulation (filling gaps, not lying), perseveration (repeating), hyperorality (mouthing objects), sundowning (worse in the evening). · Executive function (planning, problem-solving), social cognition (recognizing people and social cues)."}],"caption":"Delirium vs dementia vs depression, the vocabulary of dementia, and caring for families."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"“She was fine last night, but this morning she’s confused.”","cue":"Delirium vs dementia vs depression, the vocabulary of dementia, and caring for families.","rule":"Suggests delirium (acute).","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-neurocog","ch":"23","chapters":["23"],"title":"Neurocognitive disorders","notes":["t-neurocog"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 23; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Delirium affects lower-level cognition (attention, orientation) and is acute and usually reversible. Mild and major neurocognitive disorders (major = dementia) affect higher cognition (memory, language, executive function, social cognition); dementia is progressive and irreversible. Alzheimer’s disease causes 60–80% of dementias.","Compare: Onset: delirium sudden (hours to days) · dementia slow (months to years) · depression gradual, worse with crisis.; Course: delirium fluctuates, worse at night · dementia progressive (sundowning) · depression worse in the morning.; Attention: impaired in delirium · intact early in dementia · poor concentration in depression.; Cause: delirium = medical illness, drugs, withdrawal · dementia = Alzheimer’s, vascular, Lewy body, etc. · depression = losses, illness.; Reversible? Delirium yes · dementia no · depression yes.; An older adult with new confusion → think delirium first; a grieving older adult with poor concentration → depression (pseudodementia).","Types of dementia: Alzheimer’s (60–80%): memory first.; Vascular (5–10%): stepwise after strokes; impaired judgment, slow gait.; Frontotemporal: onset 45–60; personality change and disinhibition first.; Lewy body: Alzheimer’s-like plus visual hallucinations, movement problems, sleep disorders (very sensitive to antipsychotics).; Parkinson’s disease dementia; mixed causes are common."],"terms":["donepezil"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-neurocog","title":"Neurocognitive disorders","blocks":[{"title":"Compare","lines":["Onset: delirium sudden (hours to days) · dementia slow (months to years) · depression gradual, worse with crisis.","Course: delirium fluctuates, worse at night · dementia progressive (sundowning) · depression worse in the morning.","Attention: impaired in delirium · intact early in dementia · poor concentration in depression.","Cause: delirium = medical illness, drugs, withdrawal · dementia = Alzheimer’s, vascular, Lewy body, etc. · depression = losses, illness.","Reversible? Delirium yes · dementia no · depression yes.","An older adult with new confusion → think delirium first; a grieving older adult with poor concentration → depression (pseudodementia)."]},{"title":"Types of dementia","lines":["Alzheimer’s (60–80%): memory first.","Vascular (5–10%): stepwise after strokes; impaired judgment, slow gait.","Frontotemporal: onset 45–60; personality change and disinhibition first.","Lewy body: Alzheimer’s-like plus visual hallucinations, movement problems, sleep disorders (very sensitive to antipsychotics).","Parkinson’s disease dementia; mixed causes are common."]},{"title":"Vocabulary","lines":["Aphasia (language), apraxia (purposeful movement), agnosia (recognizing objects or people), agraphia (reading and writing).","Confabulation (filling gaps, not lying), perseveration (repeating), hyperorality (mouthing objects), sundowning (worse in the evening).","Executive function (planning, problem-solving), social cognition (recognizing people and social cues)."]},{"title":"Risk and protection","lines":["Age is the biggest risk; APOE e4; early-onset genes (under 1%); Down syndrome.","Heart health = brain health: hypertension, diabetes, obesity, inactivity, high cholesterol; head injuries.","Protective: education and mental activity (cognitive reserve), exercise, social engagement, diet, sleep."]},{"title":"Care principles","lines":["Person-centered, unconditional positive regard; distraction, not confrontation; don’t quiz.","One simple direction at a time; introduce yourself; consistent routine and caregivers.","Finger foods, weekly weights, scheduled toileting, morning light, no restraints.","Families: support groups, respite, adult day care, legal and financial planning, grief.","Nurses: realistic expectations, journaling, exercise and relaxation to manage stress."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-neurocog:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"“She was fine last night, but this morning she’s confused.”","answer":"Suggests delirium (acute).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-neurocog:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"An 85-year-old suddenly disheveled, incoherent, smelling of urine?","answer":"Likely UTI → delirium.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Personality disorders","cards":[{"title":"Cluster A: odd or eccentric","text":"Paranoid: distrust, suspicion, holds grudges, projection → keep every promise, be clear and neutral (not overly friendly), explain procedures, set limits on threats. · Schizoid: detached loner, restricted emotion, doesn’t want relationships → respect privacy, don’t push socialization, protect from ridicule. · Schizotypal: magical thinking, odd beliefs and speech, ideas of reference, social anxiety; can be made aware of distortions (unlike schizophrenia) → respect isolation, assess for suicide, low-dose antipsychotics."},{"title":"Cluster B: dramatic, emotional, erratic","text":"Borderline: unstable relationships, identity and mood; splitting; self-harm; fear of abandonment → consistent limits, DBT. · Antisocial: violates others’ rights, deceit, no remorse; conduct disorder before 15 → safety, consistent consequences. · Histrionic: attention-seeking, dramatic, seductive, vague speech → keep it professional, encourage concrete language, assess suicide gestures."},{"title":"Cluster C: anxious or fearful","text":"Avoidant: intense fear of rejection, feels inadequate → friendly, accepting, reassuring; don’t push social situations; assertiveness training. · Dependent: submissive, clinging, can’t make decisions, fears being alone → address current stressors, teach assertiveness, watch for countertransference. · Obsessive-compulsive personality: perfectionism, rigidity, control, workaholic (ego-syntonic, unlike OCD) → avoid power struggles, provide structure, allow extra time."}],"caption":"All ten disorders in three clusters, with the nursing approach for each."},"strategy":{"title":"Match the response to the patient and the task","task":"Which personality disorders are “odd or eccentric”?","cue":"All ten disorders in three clusters, with the nursing approach for each.","rule":"Paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal (Cluster A).","trap":"Do not infer intent or a diagnosis from a single label. Use the stated findings and the exact question."},"id":"t-personality","ch":"24","chapters":["24"],"title":"Personality disorders","notes":["t-personality"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e · Chapter 24; existing site-authored topic notes and question bank.","why":["Personality is a person’s lasting pattern of thinking, feeling and behaving. A personality disorder is an inflexible, maladaptive pattern that impairs relationships and functioning; people often believe the problem lies outside themselves. They usually enter care for a comorbid problem (depression, anxiety, substance use, self-harm). Personality still changes slowly across life, so treatment can help. Judge personality within the person’s culture.","Cluster A: odd or eccentric: Paranoid: distrust, suspicion, holds grudges, projection → keep every promise, be clear and neutral (not overly friendly), explain procedures, set limits on threats.; Schizoid: detached loner, restricted emotion, doesn’t want relationships → respect privacy, don’t push socialization, protect from ridicule.; Schizotypal: magical thinking, odd beliefs and speech, ideas of reference, social anxiety; can be made aware of distortions (unlike schizophrenia) → respect isolation, assess for suicide, low-dose antipsychotics.","Cluster B: dramatic, emotional, erratic: Borderline: unstable relationships, identity and mood; splitting; self-harm; fear of abandonment → consistent limits, DBT.; Antisocial: violates others’ rights, deceit, no remorse; conduct disorder before 15 → safety, consistent consequences.; Histrionic: attention-seeking, dramatic, seductive, vague speech → keep it professional, encourage concrete language, assess suicide gestures.; Narcissistic: grandiose, entitled, no empathy, fragile self-esteem underneath → stay neutral, avoid power struggles, don’t challenge grandiosity directly, model empathy."],"terms":["bupropion","psychotherapy"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"t-personality","title":"Personality disorders","blocks":[{"title":"Cluster A: odd or eccentric","lines":["Paranoid: distrust, suspicion, holds grudges, projection → keep every promise, be clear and neutral (not overly friendly), explain procedures, set limits on threats.","Schizoid: detached loner, restricted emotion, doesn’t want relationships → respect privacy, don’t push socialization, protect from ridicule.","Schizotypal: magical thinking, odd beliefs and speech, ideas of reference, social anxiety; can be made aware of distortions (unlike schizophrenia) → respect isolation, assess for suicide, low-dose antipsychotics."]},{"title":"Cluster B: dramatic, emotional, erratic","lines":["Borderline: unstable relationships, identity and mood; splitting; self-harm; fear of abandonment → consistent limits, DBT.","Antisocial: violates others’ rights, deceit, no remorse; conduct disorder before 15 → safety, consistent consequences.","Histrionic: attention-seeking, dramatic, seductive, vague speech → keep it professional, encourage concrete language, assess suicide gestures.","Narcissistic: grandiose, entitled, no empathy, fragile self-esteem underneath → stay neutral, avoid power struggles, don’t challenge grandiosity directly, model empathy."]},{"title":"Cluster C: anxious or fearful","lines":["Avoidant: intense fear of rejection, feels inadequate → friendly, accepting, reassuring; don’t push social situations; assertiveness training.","Dependent: submissive, clinging, can’t make decisions, fears being alone → address current stressors, teach assertiveness, watch for countertransference.","Obsessive-compulsive personality: perfectionism, rigidity, control, workaholic (ego-syntonic, unlike OCD) → avoid power struggles, provide structure, allow extra time."]},{"title":"Primitive defenses","lines":["Splitting, projection, regression, undoing, acting out.","An ego-syntonic pattern = the person sees their behavior as acceptable."]},{"title":"Treatment overall","lines":["Psychotherapy first (CBT, DBT, schema-focused, mentalization-based, group).","No FDA-approved drugs: treat target symptoms (SSRIs, mood stabilizers, low-dose antipsychotics).","Clinical supervision for staff: these patients stir strong reactions."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"t-personality:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Schizotypal personality disorder differs from schizophrenia in that patients with schizotypal personality disorder:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Can be made aware of their odd beliefs","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Their beliefs are not fixed delusions."},{"id":"o1","text":"Are more delusional","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Their beliefs are not fixed delusions."},{"id":"o2","text":"Have a greater need for socialization","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Their beliefs are not fixed delusions."},{"id":"o3","text":"Don’t respond to antipsychotics","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Their beliefs are not fixed delusions."}]},{"id":"t-personality:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"Which personality disorders are “odd or eccentric”?","answer":"Paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal (Cluster A).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"t-personality:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"“I’d really like to hurt her for hurting me.” Next step?","answer":"Assess immediately for violence risk.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Look for inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity that is out of line with age, present before 12, in two or more settings (home and school), for 6 months, and that impairs school or social functioning. Gather data from parents, teachers and the child, and look for learning problems, anxiety and depression too."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priorities: safety (impulsive injury), then school performance and social relationships, then family coping and medication safety (appetite, growth, sleep, heart)."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Use teacher and parent rating scales to measure attention and behavior; track height, weight, BP, HR, sleep and appetite on medication; and look at friendships, grades and family stress."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"A 7-year-old who can’t stay seated, blurts out answers and loses everything, at home and at school.","rule":"Priorities: safety (impulsive injury), then school performance and social relationships, then family coping and medication safety (appetite, growth, sleep, heart).","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-adhd","ch":"11","chapters":["11"],"title":"ADHD","notes":["c-adhd"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 11; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: The symptoms are in both settings, began early and impair learning and friendships, so this fits ADHD, combined type. Note his low self-esteem and peer rejection: they drive later problems if not addressed.","Priority: Priorities: safety (impulsive injury), then school performance and social relationships, then family coping and medication safety (appetite, growth, sleep, heart).","Act: Combine behavior therapy (parent training first under age 6; parent training plus medication at 6 and older) with school supports (IEP or 504 plan) and stimulant medication. Use structure, consistent limits, rewards and the disruptive-behavior techniques (limit setting, redirection, planned ignoring, signals, time-out).","Reassess: Use teacher and parent rating scales to measure attention and behavior; track height, weight, BP, HR, sleep and appetite on medication; and look at friendships, grades and family stress."],"terms":["Inattention","Hyperactivity","Impulsivity","methylphenidate","atomoxetine"],"termDefinitions":{"Inattention":"Careless mistakes, loses things, doesn’t seem to listen, doesn’t finish work, easily distracted, forgetful. The brain’s attention and executive-function networks (frontal lobe, low dopamine and norepinephrine signaling) don’t sustain focus. Use short, clear instructions, one step at a time; cut down distractions; check that he understood.","Hyperactivity":"Fidgets, leaves his seat, runs and climbs, can’t play quietly, talks constantly, “driven by a motor.” Poor regulation of motor activity and arousal. Give safe physical outlets (sports, movement breaks) rather than demanding stillness.","Impulsivity":"Blurts out answers, can’t wait his turn, interrupts and grabs. Weak inhibition (“braking”) by the prefrontal cortex. Safety risk (darting into traffic, climbing) and peer rejection. Clear rules, consistent consequences, praise for waiting."},"patient":"7-year-old boy brought to the clinic by his mother. His teacher reports he leaves his seat, interrupts, can’t wait his turn, and starts work but never finishes it. At home he loses shoes and homework, and “runs like a motor.” Classmates no longer invite him to play. Methylphenidate is being started.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-adhd","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Look for inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity that is out of line with age, present before 12, in two or more settings (home and school), for 6 months, and that impairs school or social functioning. Gather data from parents, teachers and the child, and look for learning problems, anxiety and depression too."]},{"title":"Diagnosis","lines":["6 or more symptoms of inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity (5 if 17 or older) for 6 months.","Several symptoms before age 12.","In 2 or more settings.","Types: inattentive (formerly “ADD”), hyperactive-impulsive, combined.","About 10% of children; boys about 2 times as often as girls; median onset age 7; often continues into adulthood."]},{"title":"Often goes with","lines":["Oppositional defiant and conduct disorders, learning disorders, anxiety and depression, tics.","About 63% of children with Tourette disorder also have ADHD."]}]},{"id":"c-adhd","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["The symptoms are in both settings, began early and impair learning and friendships, so this fits ADHD, combined type. Note his low self-esteem and peer rejection: they drive later problems if not addressed."]}]},{"id":"c-adhd","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priorities: safety (impulsive injury), then school performance and social relationships, then family coping and medication safety (appetite, growth, sleep, heart)."]},{"title":"Common nursing diagnoses","lines":["Risk for injury; impaired impulse control.","Impaired socialization; situational low self-esteem.","Impaired family coping; readiness for enhanced parenting."]}]},{"id":"c-adhd","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: he finishes short tasks, waits his turn with reminders, has fewer incidents, makes a friend, keeps his growth on track while on medication, and his parents use consistent structure and rewards. Long term: he takes increasing responsibility for managing his symptoms."]}]},{"id":"c-adhd","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Combine behavior therapy (parent training first under age 6; parent training plus medication at 6 and older) with school supports (IEP or 504 plan) and stimulant medication. Use structure, consistent limits, rewards and the disruptive-behavior techniques (limit setting, redirection, planned ignoring, signals, time-out)."]},{"title":"Stimulants (first line)","lines":["Methylphenidate (Ritalin, Concerta) and amphetamines (Adderall, Vyvanse): raise dopamine and norepinephrine; work fast, often dramatically.","Side effects: insomnia (give the last dose by about 4 p.m., or use long-acting forms in the morning), appetite loss and weight loss (give with or after breakfast; healthy snacks), growth suppression (track height and weight), headache, stomachache, ↑ HR and BP; can worsen tics.","Controlled substances: risk of misuse or diversion; keep them locked."]},{"title":"Nonstimulants","lines":["Atomoxetine (Strattera): a norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor; takes up to 6 weeks; good with anxiety, tics or substance use. Watch liver function, BP and HR, and suicidal thinking.","Guanfacine and clonidine (alpha-2 agonists): sedation, low BP and HR; don’t stop suddenly (rebound hypertension)."]},{"title":"Techniques for disruptive behavior","lines":["Limit setting: firm, calm, specific: “Hitting is not okay.” (Not “behave yourself.”)","Redirection, planned ignoring (for safe attention-seeking behavior), signals (finger to lips), physical closeness, restructuring an activity.","Behavioral contract and point or reward systems; simple restitution (fix what you broke).","Time-out: short, used consistently; if overused (20 times a day) it loses its effect.","Least restrictive first: talk → offer medication → time-out → seclusion → restraint."]},{"title":"Activities","lines":["Choose physical, short, noncompetitive activities with quick feedback (tennis, running games); avoid long, slow board games or reading for long periods."]}]},{"id":"c-adhd","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Use teacher and parent rating scales to measure attention and behavior; track height, weight, BP, HR, sleep and appetite on medication; and look at friendships, grades and family stress."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-adhd:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"7-year-old boy brought to the clinic by his mother. His teacher reports he leaves his seat, interrupts, can’t wait his turn, and starts work but never finishes it. At home he loses shoes and homework, and “runs like a motor.” Classmates no longer invite him to play. Methylphenidate is being started.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priorities: safety (impulsive injury), then school performance and social relationships, then family coping and medication safety (appetite, growth, sleep, heart).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-adhd:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"7-year-old boy brought to the clinic by his mother. His teacher reports he leaves his seat, interrupts, can’t wait his turn, and starts work but never finishes it. At home he loses shoes and homework, and “runs like a motor.” Classmates no longer invite him to play. Methylphenidate is being started.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Combine behavior therapy (parent training first under age 6; parent training plus medication at 6 and older) with school supports (IEP or 504 plan) and stimulant medication. Use structure, consistent limits, rewards and the disruptive-behavior techniques (limit setting, redirection, planned ignoring, signals, time-out). Use teacher and parent rating scales to measure attention and behavior; track height, weight, BP, HR, sleep and appetite on medication; and look at friendships, grades and family stress.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Look for the two core areas: social communication deficits (reciprocity, nonverbal communication, relationships) and restricted, repetitive behavior (stereotyped movements or speech, insistence on sameness, fixated interests, over- or under-reaction to sensory input). Note the support level, any self-injury, the developmental history and family stress."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priorities: risk for injury (self-injury), then self-care deficit, impaired communication and socialization, and caregiver stress and family coping."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Count self-injury episodes, measure the time tolerated in new activities, track progress toward skills (communication, self-care), and ask about parental stress and use of resources."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"A nonverbal 12-year-old who needs the same routine and bites himself when it changes.","rule":"Priorities: risk for injury (self-injury), then self-care deficit, impaired communication and socialization, and caregiver stress and family coping.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-autism","ch":"11","chapters":["11"],"title":"Autism spectrum disorder","notes":["c-autism"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 11; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: A major change in routine (new school) is driving escalating self-injury in a nonverbal child who can’t say he is distressed: this is level 3 autism. The family is under heavy stress.","Priority: Priorities: risk for injury (self-injury), then self-care deficit, impaired communication and socialization, and caregiver stress and family coping.","Act: Keep him safe; keep routines predictable (visual schedules); introduce change gradually in small steps; reduce sensory triggers; use rewards for adaptive behavior; refer early to intensive behavioral programs (ABA / early intensive behavioral intervention), speech, occupational and physical therapy; support the parents.","Reassess: Count self-injury episodes, measure the time tolerated in new activities, track progress toward skills (communication, self-care), and ask about parental stress and use of resources."],"terms":["Insistence on sameness","Sensory hyper- or hyporeactivity","Stereotyped behavior and echolalia","Self-injury","aripiprazole","naltrexone","risperidone"],"termDefinitions":{"Insistence on sameness":"Extreme distress at small changes: a new route, an unripe banana, a new school. Predictability lowers overwhelming anxiety for a brain that processes change and sensory input differently. Keep routines and structure; prepare for changes with visual schedules and warnings; change one thing at a time.","Sensory hyper- or hyporeactivity":"Covers ears at a toilet flush; doesn’t react to pain; loves the sound of Velcro. Sensory input is processed atypically: too intense or too weak. Reduce triggers (don’t flush near him), use preferred sensations calmingly, and check carefully for injury or illness, since pain may not show.","Stereotyped behavior and echolalia":"Spinning objects, hand flapping, rocking, lining up toys, repeating words. Self-soothing and self-regulating behaviors. Allow safe ones; redirect only when unsafe or when they block learning.","Self-injury":"Hand biting, head banging, face slapping when frustrated. A way to communicate distress or regulate overwhelming input when he has no words. Risk for injury is the priority: protect (a helmet if needed), find the trigger, and teach other ways to signal distress."},"patient":"12-year-old boy with autism, nonverbal, needs help with toileting and dressing. He spins a ball for long periods, can’t stand the sound of a flushing toilet, and only eats bananas when they are perfectly ripe. Since starting a new school 3 weeks ago, he bites his hand and hits his head when upset. His parents say, “We’re overwhelmed.”","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-autism","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Look for the two core areas: social communication deficits (reciprocity, nonverbal communication, relationships) and restricted, repetitive behavior (stereotyped movements or speech, insistence on sameness, fixated interests, over- or under-reaction to sensory input). Note the support level, any self-injury, the developmental history and family stress."]},{"title":"Facts","lines":["Usually appears in the first 3 years; often first noticed as poor eye contact or social response in infancy.","4 times more common in boys; strongly genetic (identical twins 70–90%).","At least half have some intellectual disability; some have savant abilities.","Support levels: Level 1 requires support; Level 2 requires substantial support; Level 3 requires very substantial support (often nonverbal, needs help with ADLs)."]}]},{"id":"c-autism","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["A major change in routine (new school) is driving escalating self-injury in a nonverbal child who can’t say he is distressed: this is level 3 autism. The family is under heavy stress."]}]},{"id":"c-autism","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priorities: risk for injury (self-injury), then self-care deficit, impaired communication and socialization, and caregiver stress and family coping."]}]},{"id":"c-autism","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: fewer self-injury episodes, with the child using a signal or picture to show distress; he adapts to the new routine in small steps (sits in music class, touches an instrument); parents use the plan at home and report less stress with respite and support."]}]},{"id":"c-autism","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Keep him safe; keep routines predictable (visual schedules); introduce change gradually in small steps; reduce sensory triggers; use rewards for adaptive behavior; refer early to intensive behavioral programs (ABA / early intensive behavioral intervention), speech, occupational and physical therapy; support the parents."]},{"title":"What works (and what doesn’t)","lines":["Indicated: assess family stress and teach coping; behavior strategies for transitions; reward system (e.g., for sitting in music class); review the home plan with the parents.","Not helpful for a nonverbal child: written behavioral contracts and role-playing (these need strong language skills).","Redirect with simple gestures and words; use firm, calm limits."]},{"title":"Therapies","lines":["Applied behavior analysis (ABA) and early intensive behavioral intervention (up to 40 hours a week for years): the strongest evidence for language and thinking skills.","Early Start Denver Model: play-based, one-to-one routines.","Services are required by law: early intervention before age 3, then school services (IEP)."]},{"title":"Medications (target symptoms)","lines":["Risperidone and aripiprazole are FDA approved for autism-related irritability (tantrums, aggression, self-injury); watch for EPS, sleepiness and weight gain.","SSRIs for anxiety or repetitive behavior; stimulants for hyperactivity; naltrexone for self-injury."]}]},{"id":"c-autism","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Count self-injury episodes, measure the time tolerated in new activities, track progress toward skills (communication, self-care), and ask about parental stress and use of resources."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-autism:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"12-year-old boy with autism, nonverbal, needs help with toileting and dressing. He spins a ball for long periods, can’t stand the sound of a flushing toilet, and only eats bananas when they are perfectly ripe. Since starting a new school 3 weeks ago, he bites his hand and hits his head when upset. His parents say, “We’re overwhelmed.”","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priorities: risk for injury (self-injury), then self-care deficit, impaired communication and socialization, and caregiver stress and family coping.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-autism:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"12-year-old boy with autism, nonverbal, needs help with toileting and dressing. He spins a ball for long periods, can’t stand the sound of a flushing toilet, and only eats bananas when they are perfectly ripe. Since starting a new school 3 weeks ago, he bites his hand and hits his head when upset. His parents say, “We’re overwhelmed.”","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Keep him safe; keep routines predictable (visual schedules); introduce change gradually in small steps; reduce sensory triggers; use rewards for adaptive behavior; refer early to intensive behavioral programs (ABA / early intensive behavioral intervention), speech, occupational and physical therapy; support the parents. Count self-injury episodes, measure the time tolerated in new activities, track progress toward skills (communication, self-care), and ask about parental stress and use of resources.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Recognize severe underweight (BMI and % of ideal weight), an intense fear of weight gain, a distorted body image and the signs of starvation: bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, orthostasis, lanugo, cold extremities, amenorrhea, constipation, anemia, low bone density. Ask about purging and exercise. Most patients deny there is a problem."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"First: impaired nutrition, decreased cardiac output, electrolyte and fluid imbalance (life-threatening). Then body image, ineffective coping, low self-esteem and family processes."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Track weight on schedule, vital signs, orthostatics, electrolytes (especially phosphate in the first week), intake, hiding or compensating behaviors and statements about body image."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"A college runner at 50% of ideal weight, HR 54, fainting at practice, “afraid of getting fat.”","rule":"First: impaired nutrition, decreased cardiac output, electrolyte and fluid imbalance (life-threatening). Then body image, ineffective coping, low self-esteem and family processes.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-anorexia","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Anorexia nervosa","notes":["c-anorexia"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 18; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Severe malnutrition (about 50% of ideal weight) with cardiovascular compromise (bradycardia, hypotension, syncope) = medical admission. Once feeding starts, the big hidden danger is refeeding syndrome.","Priority: First: impaired nutrition, decreased cardiac output, electrolyte and fluid imbalance (life-threatening). Then body image, ineffective coping, low self-esteem and family processes.","Act: Medically stabilize, then a structured weight-restoration program: set meal times and duration, a dietitian’s plan, observe meals and 1 hour after, supervise bathroom use, limit exercise, weigh at set times in a gown or underwear after voiding (backward if needed), don’t negotiate or reweigh, praise progress, and use CBT and family-based therapy.","Reassess: Track weight on schedule, vital signs, orthostatics, electrolytes (especially phosphate in the first week), intake, hiding or compensating behaviors and statements about body image."],"terms":["Bradycardia and hypotension","Lanugo","Body image disturbance","Ego-syntonic illness","Refeeding syndrome"],"termDefinitions":{"Bradycardia and hypotension":"HR 54, BP 74/50, dizziness, fainting. The starving body lowers its metabolic rate to save energy, and heart muscle wastes too, so the heart pumps less. HR under 50, systolic under 90, temperature under 96 °F, arrhythmias or under 75% of ideal weight → hospitalize.","Lanugo":"Fine, soft, downy hair on the face, back and arms. The body grows insulating hair when fat stores are gone. A visible sign of significant starvation.","Body image disturbance":"“I’m fat” while emaciated; checks body parts every night. Altered processing of body signals (insula) plus a self-worth tied to shape and weight. Don’t argue about size; focus on health and feelings. Weigh him backward on the scale if seeing the number raises anxiety.","Ego-syntonic illness":"He values the restriction and resists treatment. Restriction briefly relieves anxiety (lower tryptophan, then less serotonin activity), which rewards it. Expect ambivalence; the nurse does not negotiate weight, but supports him through the fear.","Refeeding syndrome":"After feeding begins: weakness, swelling, confusion, arrhythmias, seizures, heart failure. Insulin rises with carbohydrate, pushing phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells (and using up thiamine), so blood levels crash. The wasted heart can’t handle the fluid. Start feeding low and slow; check phosphate, K⁺ and Mg²⁺ daily at first; give thiamine; watch for edema and heart failure. Gain no more than 2–3 lb a week (over 5 lb a week risks pulmonary edema)."},"patient":"21-year-old college track athlete brought in by his brothers after fainting at practice. 5 ft 2 in and 58 lb (about 50% of ideal body weight). BP 74/50, HR 54, temperature 96 °F, hemoglobin 9, fine downy hair on his back, cold mottled hands. He says, “I check my legs every night. I’m so afraid of getting fat or slowing down.”","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-anorexia","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Recognize severe underweight (BMI and % of ideal weight), an intense fear of weight gain, a distorted body image and the signs of starvation: bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, orthostasis, lanugo, cold extremities, amenorrhea, constipation, anemia, low bone density. Ask about purging and exercise. Most patients deny there is a problem."]},{"title":"Diagnosis and severity","lines":["Restricted intake → significantly low body weight; intense fear of gain or behavior that blocks gain; disturbed body image or denial of seriousness.","Subtypes: restricting and binge-eating/purging.","Severity by BMI: mild ≥17, moderate 16–17, severe 15–16, extreme <15.","Typically starts in adolescence; more common in females, but also in athletes in lean or aesthetic sports (running, wrestling, gymnastics) and in LGBTQ youth."]},{"title":"Labs to know","lines":["Electrolytes (low K⁺, especially with purging), glucose, thyroid (low T3/T4), CBC (anemia, low counts), ECG (bradycardia, arrhythmias), bone density (DEXA), ESR, CPK."]}]},{"id":"c-anorexia","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Severe malnutrition (about 50% of ideal weight) with cardiovascular compromise (bradycardia, hypotension, syncope) = medical admission. Once feeding starts, the big hidden danger is refeeding syndrome."]}]},{"id":"c-anorexia","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["First: impaired nutrition, decreased cardiac output, electrolyte and fluid imbalance (life-threatening). Then body image, ineffective coping, low self-esteem and family processes."]}]},{"id":"c-anorexia","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: vital signs and electrolytes stabilize; he gains 2–3 lb a week toward about 90% of ideal body weight (75% to leave the hospital, in the textbook case); eats planned meals without compensating; and later states feelings without restricting."]}]},{"id":"c-anorexia","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Medically stabilize, then a structured weight-restoration program: set meal times and duration, a dietitian’s plan, observe meals and 1 hour after, supervise bathroom use, limit exercise, weigh at set times in a gown or underwear after voiding (backward if needed), don’t negotiate or reweigh, praise progress, and use CBT and family-based therapy."]},{"title":"Unit interventions","lines":["Calm, pleasant meals with set times (about 30 minutes); no talk about food or weight at the table.","Liquid supplements if solid food can’t be finished.","Observe during and 1 hour after meals; bathroom checks; watch for hiding food, exercising in the room, water-loading before weigh-ins.","Weigh daily at first, then 3 times a week, same time and clothing, after voiding.","Increase choice and independence as weight goals are met.","Explore feelings; challenge distorted thoughts (CBT)."]},{"title":"Treatment","lines":["No FDA-approved drug for anorexia; SSRIs work poorly until weight is restored (they need dietary tryptophan) but help co-occurring depression, anxiety and OCD.","Family-based treatment is the best evidence for adolescents; CBT and adolescent-focused therapy.","Recovery is a process; relapse is common (about 50% in the first year)."]}]},{"id":"c-anorexia","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Track weight on schedule, vital signs, orthostatics, electrolytes (especially phosphate in the first week), intake, hiding or compensating behaviors and statements about body image."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-anorexia:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which finding meets a criterion for hospitalizing a patient with an eating disorder?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Daytime heart rate under 50","correct":true,"why":"Supported. HR under 50, systolic under 90, temperature under 96 °F, or under 75% of ideal weight → admit."},{"id":"o1","text":"Oral temperature of 100 °F or more","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. HR under 50, systolic under 90, temperature under 96 °F, or under 75% of ideal weight → admit."},{"id":"o2","text":"90% of ideal body weight","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. HR under 50, systolic under 90, temperature under 96 °F, or under 75% of ideal weight → admit."},{"id":"o3","text":"Systolic BP above 130","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. HR under 50, systolic under 90, temperature under 96 °F, or under 75% of ideal weight → admit."}]},{"id":"c-anorexia:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"21-year-old college track athlete brought in by his brothers after fainting at practice. 5 ft 2 in and 58 lb (about 50% of ideal body weight). BP 74/50, HR 54, temperature 96 °F, hemoglobin 9, fine downy hair on his back, cold mottled hands. He says, “I check my legs every night. I’m so afraid of getting fat or slowing down.”","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"First: impaired nutrition, decreased cardiac output, electrolyte and fluid imbalance (life-threatening). Then body image, ineffective coping, low self-esteem and family processes.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-anorexia:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"21-year-old college track athlete brought in by his brothers after fainting at practice. 5 ft 2 in and 58 lb (about 50% of ideal body weight). BP 74/50, HR 54, temperature 96 °F, hemoglobin 9, fine downy hair on his back, cold mottled hands. He says, “I check my legs every night. I’m so afraid of getting fat or slowing down.”","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Medically stabilize, then a structured weight-restoration program: set meal times and duration, a dietitian’s plan, observe meals and 1 hour after, supervise bathroom use, limit exercise, weigh at set times in a gown or underwear after voiding (backward if needed), don’t negotiate or reweigh, praise progress, and use CBT and family-based therapy. Track weight on schedule, vital signs, orthostatics, electrolytes (especially phosphate in the first week), intake, hiding or compensating behaviors and statements about body image.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Recognize recurrent binges (a large amount in about 2 hours with loss of control) followed by compensating (vomiting, laxatives, diuretics, fasting, excess exercise) at least weekly for 3 months, with self-worth tied to shape and weight. Weight is often normal, so look for the signs: Russell sign, parotid swelling, dental erosion, and electrolyte losses."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"First: electrolyte imbalance and decreased cardiac output (from purging). Then ineffective coping, chronic low self-esteem, body image and social isolation."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Track potassium and the ECG, the number of binge-purge episodes (journal), adherence to meal plans, use of coping skills and mood."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Normal weight, binges and vomits 15 times a night; potassium 2.7.","rule":"First: electrolyte imbalance and decreased cardiac output (from purging). Then ineffective coping, chronic low self-esteem, body image and social isolation.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-bulimia","ch":"18","chapters":["18"],"title":"Bulimia nervosa","notes":["c-bulimia"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 18; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Frequent purging has caused severe hypokalemia with ECG changes and orthostasis: a cardiac risk, even though her weight looks fine. Shame and a trauma history drive the binge-purge cycle.","Priority: First: electrolyte imbalance and decreased cardiac output (from purging). Then ineffective coping, chronic low self-esteem, body image and social isolation.","Act: Replace electrolytes and monitor; structured, regular meals; observe for 1–2 hours after meals and supervise the bathroom; teach the binge-purge cycle and planned eating; CBT (first line) to challenge distorted thoughts; journal triggers; treat comorbid depression; fluoxetine (FDA approved for bulimia, at higher doses).","Reassess: Track potassium and the ECG, the number of binge-purge episodes (journal), adherence to meal plans, use of coping skills and mood."],"terms":["Hypokalemia","Russell sign","Parotid swelling and dental erosion","bupropion","fluoxetine"],"termDefinitions":{"Hypokalemia":"K⁺ 2.7; weakness, palpitations, ECG changes (flat T, U waves). Vomiting loses stomach acid and potassium; the alkalosis that follows drives K⁺ into cells and the kidneys waste more. The most dangerous finding: arrhythmias and sudden death. Replace potassium and monitor the ECG. K⁺ below 3.2, chloride below 88, esophageal tears or arrhythmias → hospitalize.","Russell sign":"Calluses or scars on the knuckles and back of the hand. Teeth rub the hand during self-induced vomiting. A physical clue to purging in a normal-weight patient.","Parotid swelling and dental erosion":"“Chipmunk” cheeks, worn enamel, cavities. Repeated vomiting stimulates the salivary glands, and stomach acid dissolves enamel. Dental referral; teach rinsing with water (not brushing right after vomiting)."},"patient":"40-year-old teacher at 95% of ideal weight who binges every night and vomits up to 15 times. She has eroded tooth enamel, swollen parotid glands and calluses on the back of her hand. Potassium 2.7 mEq/L, and her ECG shows changes consistent with hypokalemia. She says, “I’m ashamed I can’t control it. I hate myself.”","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-bulimia","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Recognize recurrent binges (a large amount in about 2 hours with loss of control) followed by compensating (vomiting, laxatives, diuretics, fasting, excess exercise) at least weekly for 3 months, with self-worth tied to shape and weight. Weight is often normal, so look for the signs: Russell sign, parotid swelling, dental erosion, and electrolyte losses."]},{"title":"Facts","lines":["Starts in late adolescence or young adulthood; about 60% heritable.","Often with depression, anxiety, substance use, PTSD and borderline personality disorder; often a trauma history.","Patients feel shame (the illness is ego-dystonic), so they usually want help, unlike in anorexia."]}]},{"id":"c-bulimia","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Frequent purging has caused severe hypokalemia with ECG changes and orthostasis: a cardiac risk, even though her weight looks fine. Shame and a trauma history drive the binge-purge cycle."]},{"title":"The binge-purge cycle","lines":["Restrict (skip meals) → intense hunger → binge → guilt and fear of weight gain → purge → relief → restrict again.","Restriction sets up the next binge, which is why regular meals break the cycle.","Low serotonin signaling and reward-seeking dopamine changes may add to it."]}]},{"id":"c-bulimia","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["First: electrolyte imbalance and decreased cardiac output (from purging). Then ineffective coping, chronic low self-esteem, body image and social isolation."]}]},{"id":"c-bulimia","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: potassium and the ECG stay normal; no binge-purge episodes; she eats 3 planned meals plus snacks; she names her triggers and uses other coping skills; and she challenges “forbidden food” thoughts."]}]},{"id":"c-bulimia","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Replace electrolytes and monitor; structured, regular meals; observe for 1–2 hours after meals and supervise the bathroom; teach the binge-purge cycle and planned eating; CBT (first line) to challenge distorted thoughts; journal triggers; treat comorbid depression; fluoxetine (FDA approved for bulimia, at higher doses)."]},{"title":"Medications","lines":["Fluoxetine is FDA approved for bulimia in adults (dose higher than for depression); other SSRIs, TCAs and an MAOI have shown benefit.","Bupropion is contraindicated (seizure risk with purging and electrolyte loss)."]},{"title":"Teaching","lines":["Fasting sets up the next binge: eat regularly.","Signs of low potassium (weakness, palpitations) and when to seek care.","Laxatives and diuretics don’t prevent calorie absorption; stopping diuretics causes temporary rebound edema, which isn’t fat and passes.","Rinse your mouth with water after vomiting; dental care."]}]},{"id":"c-bulimia","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Track potassium and the ECG, the number of binge-purge episodes (journal), adherence to meal plans, use of coping skills and mood."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-bulimia:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which finding is the most dangerous in a patient with bulimia nervosa?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Swollen parotid glands","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Hypokalemia can cause fatal arrhythmias."},{"id":"o1","text":"Calluses on the knuckles","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Hypokalemia can cause fatal arrhythmias."},{"id":"o2","text":"Potassium of 2.7 mEq/L","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Hypokalemia can cause fatal arrhythmias."},{"id":"o3","text":"Dental caries","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Hypokalemia can cause fatal arrhythmias."}]},{"id":"c-bulimia:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"40-year-old teacher at 95% of ideal weight who binges every night and vomits up to 15 times. She has eroded tooth enamel, swollen parotid glands and calluses on the back of her hand. Potassium 2.7 mEq/L, and her ECG shows changes consistent with hypokalemia. She says, “I’m ashamed I can’t control it. I hate myself.”","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"First: electrolyte imbalance and decreased cardiac output (from purging). Then ineffective coping, chronic low self-esteem, body image and social isolation.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-bulimia:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"40-year-old teacher at 95% of ideal weight who binges every night and vomits up to 15 times. She has eroded tooth enamel, swollen parotid glands and calluses on the back of her hand. Potassium 2.7 mEq/L, and her ECG shows changes consistent with hypokalemia. She says, “I’m ashamed I can’t control it. I hate myself.”","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Replace electrolytes and monitor; structured, regular meals; observe for 1–2 hours after meals and supervise the bathroom; teach the binge-purge cycle and planned eating; CBT (first line) to challenge distorted thoughts; journal triggers; treat comorbid depression; fluoxetine (FDA approved for bulimia, at higher doses). Track potassium and the ECG, the number of binge-purge episodes (journal), adherence to meal plans, use of coping skills and mood.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Delirium = an acute (hours to days), fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness plus another cognitive change (disorientation, memory, perception), caused by a medical condition, substance or withdrawal. Look for inattention (can’t focus or follow), a sudden change from baseline (ask the family), visual hallucinations or illusions, a disrupted sleep–wake cycle, hyperactive (agitated) or hypoactive (quiet, easily missed) forms, and autonomic signs."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priorities: risk for injury (falls, pulling lines), acute confusion and its underlying cause, fluid balance, sleep, and fear or anxiety."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Recheck attention and orientation each shift (with a tool such as the CAM), vital signs, labs, intake, sleep and falls; she should return to baseline as the cause clears (delirium is usually reversible)."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Day 3 after hip surgery: fine yesterday, now pulling at her IV and seeing bugs on the blanket.","rule":"Priorities: risk for injury (falls, pulling lines), acute confusion and its underlying cause, fluid balance, sleep, and fear or anxiety.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-delirium","ch":"23","chapters":["23"],"title":"Delirium","notes":["c-delirium"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 23; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: An acute change from yesterday, fluctuating, with inattention, perceptual disturbance and fever with cloudy urine on opioids = delirium from likely UTI plus medication. It is a medical emergency: untreated, it raises mortality, length of stay and permanent cognitive decline.","Priority: Priorities: risk for injury (falls, pulling lines), acute confusion and its underlying cause, fluid balance, sleep, and fear or anxiety.","Act: Find and treat the cause (labs, cultures, medication review). Keep her safe without restraints if possible (they worsen delirium): close observation, family at the bedside, a low bed, a well-lit, quiet room, glasses and hearing aids on, clocks and calendars, consistent caregivers, short simple statements, introduce yourself every time, reorient gently (don’t quiz her). Fluids, nutrition, sleep and mobility. Antipsychotics or anxiolytics only cautiously for dangerous agitation.","Reassess: Recheck attention and orientation each shift (with a tool such as the CAM), vital signs, labs, intake, sleep and falls; she should return to baseline as the cause clears (delirium is usually reversible)."],"terms":["Inattention","Illusion vs hallucination","Hypoactive delirium"],"termDefinitions":{"Inattention":"Stares past you, loses track mid-sentence, can’t follow a two-step instruction. Brain metabolism is disturbed globally (infection, hypoxia, drugs, electrolytes), so arousal and attention fail first. The cardinal sign: any acute confusion in an older adult is delirium until proven otherwise, not “just dementia.”","Illusion vs hallucination":"Folds in the blanket look like snakes (illusion) vs seeing bugs that aren’t there (hallucination). Illusions misread a real stimulus; hallucinations have no stimulus. In delirium they are usually visual (and tactile). Illusions can be explained and corrected (turn on the light, show her the blanket). Respond to the fear.","Hypoactive delirium":"A quiet, drowsy, withdrawn patient who “seems calm.” Same brain dysfunction, without agitation. Often missed: assess attention in every older inpatient."},"patient":"82-year-old woman, day 3 after hip surgery, on opioids for pain, with a urinary catheter. Yesterday she chatted with her daughter; tonight she is picking at the sheets, says there are bugs on the blanket, tries to climb out of bed and pulls at her IV. She dozes, then is agitated again. Temperature 38.3 °C, cloudy urine.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-delirium","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Delirium = an acute (hours to days), fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness plus another cognitive change (disorientation, memory, perception), caused by a medical condition, substance or withdrawal. Look for inattention (can’t focus or follow), a sudden change from baseline (ask the family), visual hallucinations or illusions, a disrupted sleep–wake cycle, hyperactive (agitated) or hypoactive (quiet, easily missed) forms, and autonomic signs."]},{"title":"Common causes and risk factors","lines":["Infection (UTI, pneumonia), hypoxia, pain, dehydration, electrolyte or glucose problems.","Medications (opioids, benzodiazepines, anticholinergics, polypharmacy) and withdrawal (alcohol).","Immobility, restraints, catheters, sleep deprivation, noise, moving rooms.","Vision or hearing impairment, pre-existing dementia, older age, surgery, ICU stay."]}]},{"id":"c-delirium","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["An acute change from yesterday, fluctuating, with inattention, perceptual disturbance and fever with cloudy urine on opioids = delirium from likely UTI plus medication. It is a medical emergency: untreated, it raises mortality, length of stay and permanent cognitive decline."]}]},{"id":"c-delirium","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priorities: risk for injury (falls, pulling lines), acute confusion and its underlying cause, fluid balance, sleep, and fear or anxiety."]}]},{"id":"c-delirium","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: she stays safe (no falls, lines intact), the cause is treated (UTI, pain, medications), she becomes oriented in lucid periods and returns to her baseline, and she maintains fluids and nutrition."]}]},{"id":"c-delirium","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Find and treat the cause (labs, cultures, medication review). Keep her safe without restraints if possible (they worsen delirium): close observation, family at the bedside, a low bed, a well-lit, quiet room, glasses and hearing aids on, clocks and calendars, consistent caregivers, short simple statements, introduce yourself every time, reorient gently (don’t quiz her). Fluids, nutrition, sleep and mobility. Antipsychotics or anxiolytics only cautiously for dangerous agitation."]},{"title":"Communication in delirium","lines":["Approach from the front, say her name, introduce yourself.","Simple, direct statements; one thing at a time; explain what you’re doing.","Respond to the feeling (“You’re frightened. I’m here.”) and correct illusions.","Don’t argue with hallucinations; never leave an acutely delirious patient alone."]}]},{"id":"c-delirium","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Recheck attention and orientation each shift (with a tool such as the CAM), vital signs, labs, intake, sleep and falls; she should return to baseline as the cause clears (delirium is usually reversible)."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-delirium:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which statement by a family member suggests delirium rather than dementia?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“She was fine last night, but this morning she was confused.”","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Sudden onset = delirium."},{"id":"o1","text":"“Dad doesn’t recognize us anymore.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Sudden onset = delirium."},{"id":"o2","text":"“She’s convinced snakes come into her room at night.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Sudden onset = delirium."},{"id":"o3","text":"“He can’t remember to take his pills.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Sudden onset = delirium."}]},{"id":"c-delirium:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"82-year-old woman, day 3 after hip surgery, on opioids for pain, with a urinary catheter. Yesterday she chatted with her daughter; tonight she is picking at the sheets, says there are bugs on the blanket, tries to climb out of bed and pulls at her IV. She dozes, then is agitated again. Temperature 38.3 °C, cloudy urine.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priorities: risk for injury (falls, pulling lines), acute confusion and its underlying cause, fluid balance, sleep, and fear or anxiety.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-delirium:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"82-year-old woman, day 3 after hip surgery, on opioids for pain, with a urinary catheter. Yesterday she chatted with her daughter; tonight she is picking at the sheets, says there are bugs on the blanket, tries to climb out of bed and pulls at her IV. She dozes, then is agitated again. Temperature 38.3 °C, cloudy urine.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Find and treat the cause (labs, cultures, medication review). Keep her safe without restraints if possible (they worsen delirium): close observation, family at the bedside, a low bed, a well-lit, quiet room, glasses and hearing aids on, clocks and calendars, consistent caregivers, short simple statements, introduce yourself every time, reorient gently (don’t quiz her). Fluids, nutrition, sleep and mobility. Antipsychotics or anxiolytics only cautiously for dangerous agitation. Recheck attention and orientation each shift (with a tool such as the CAM), vital signs, labs, intake, sleep and falls; she should return to baseline as the cause clears (delirium is usually reversible).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Recognize a gradual, progressive, irreversible decline in memory plus other domains (language, executive function, perception, social cognition) that interferes with daily independence (major neurocognitive disorder). Know the signs: aphasia, apraxia, agnosia, agraphia, confabulation, perseveration, hyperorality, sundowning, wandering. Identify the stage and the safety risks."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: risk for injury (wandering, falls, burns, eating nonfood items). Then self-care deficits (eating, toileting, dressing), communication, sleep, and caregiver stress."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Count falls and wandering episodes, track weight and intake, continence, sleep, the level of assistance needed, and caregiver coping. Reset outcomes as the disease progresses."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Wanders four times a week, doesn’t recognize his wife, can’t find the bathroom.","rule":"Priority: risk for injury (wandering, falls, burns, eating nonfood items). Then self-care deficits (eating, toileting, dressing), communication, sleep, and caregiver stress.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-alz","ch":"23","chapters":["23"],"title":"Alzheimer’s disease (major neurocognitive disorder)","notes":["c-alz"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 23; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: He is in moderate to severe Alzheimer’s disease: wandering, night-time disorientation, agnosia and aphasia make injury the top risk, and his wife shows caregiver stress and grief.","Priority: Priority: risk for injury (wandering, falls, burns, eating nonfood items). Then self-care deficits (eating, toileting, dressing), communication, sleep, and caregiver stress.","Act: Person-centered care (know his life story; relationships before tasks), with unconditional positive regard. Safety: low bed or mattress on the floor, lighting, remove hazards, locks placed high, alarms, ID bracelet. Communication: name, introduce yourself, one simple direction at a time, distraction rather than confrontation, no quizzing. Routine and structure, finger foods, scheduled toileting, labeled rooms with pictures. Support the family.","Reassess: Count falls and wandering episodes, track weight and intake, continence, sleep, the level of assistance needed, and caregiver coping. Reset outcomes as the disease progresses."],"terms":["Agnosia","Apraxia","Aphasia and agraphia","Confabulation","Sundowning","Perseveration and hyperorality","donepezil","rivastigmine","memantine"],"termDefinitions":{"Agnosia":"Doesn’t recognize his wife, or picks up a spoon and asks what it is. Damage to the brain areas that match sensations to stored knowledge. Introduce yourself and others every time; label objects and rooms with pictures.","Apraxia":"Can’t dress himself (arms in his trouser legs) though his muscles work. Loss of planned, purposeful movement sequences. Give one step at a time (“Here’s your shirt. Put your arm in here.”); Velcro and elastic clothing.","Aphasia and agraphia":"Searches for words, then only a few words, then babbling or mutism; can’t read or write. The language areas degenerate. Short, simple sentences; yes/no questions; gestures and pictures; patience.","Confabulation":"“I went to lunch with my family Sunday” (he was in bed all weekend). The brain fills memory gaps to protect self-esteem; it is not lying. Don’t confront it; redirect gently.","Sundowning":"More agitated, confused and wandering in the late afternoon and evening. Fatigue, fading light and a disrupted body clock. Calm evenings, good lighting, daytime activity and morning sunlight; avoid naps and caffeine late in the day.","Perseveration and hyperorality":"Repeats “hello” again and again; puts everything in his mouth. Loss of frontal control. Redirect; remove nonfood items that look like food."},"patient":"67-year-old man with Alzheimer’s disease diagnosed 4 years ago. He wanders away from home about four times a week, has fallen getting out of bed at night (“I’m in a sleeping bag in the mountains”), is incontinent when he can’t find the bathroom, can’t find his words, and asked his wife, “Who is this woman?” She sobs: “I feel like I’ve betrayed him.”","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-alz","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Recognize a gradual, progressive, irreversible decline in memory plus other domains (language, executive function, perception, social cognition) that interferes with daily independence (major neurocognitive disorder). Know the signs: aphasia, apraxia, agnosia, agraphia, confabulation, perseveration, hyperorality, sundowning, wandering. Identify the stage and the safety risks."]},{"title":"Stages","lines":["Mild (early): forgets recent events and names, trouble planning, loses things, word-finding problems; may still work; may hide it.","Moderate (middle): forgets personal history, confused about time and place, needs help choosing clothes, sundowning, wandering, suspiciousness, incontinence, personality changes.","Severe (late): needs total care; loses speech, walking and finally swallowing; infections (pneumonia)."]},{"title":"Normal aging vs Alzheimer’s","lines":["Normal: occasionally forgets a name and remembers later; misplaces keys and retraces steps.","Alzheimer’s: memory loss disrupts daily life; can’t retrace steps; gets lost in familiar places; poor judgment."]},{"title":"Delirium vs dementia vs depression","lines":["Delirium: sudden, fluctuating, attention impaired, reversible; caused by a medical problem.","Dementia: slow (months to years), progressive, memory impaired, irreversible; alert.","Depression (“pseudodementia”): gradual with a crisis, sad and apathetic, “I don’t know” answers, poor concentration; reversible with treatment."]}]},{"id":"c-alz","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["He is in moderate to severe Alzheimer’s disease: wandering, night-time disorientation, agnosia and aphasia make injury the top risk, and his wife shows caregiver stress and grief."]}]},{"id":"c-alz","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: risk for injury (wandering, falls, burns, eating nonfood items). Then self-care deficits (eating, toileting, dressing), communication, sleep, and caregiver stress."]}]},{"id":"c-alz","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: free from injury; wanders only within safe areas; keeps the highest possible level of function (feeds himself finger foods, dresses with step-by-step help); maintains weight and sleep; his wife uses support and respite services."]}]},{"id":"c-alz","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Person-centered care (know his life story; relationships before tasks), with unconditional positive regard. Safety: low bed or mattress on the floor, lighting, remove hazards, locks placed high, alarms, ID bracelet. Communication: name, introduce yourself, one simple direction at a time, distraction rather than confrontation, no quizzing. Routine and structure, finger foods, scheduled toileting, labeled rooms with pictures. Support the family."]},{"title":"Medications","lines":["Cholinesterase inhibitors (donepezil for all stages; rivastigmine (patch for all stages); galantamine): raise acetylcholine; modest, temporary benefit. Side effects: nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, bradycardia and syncope, insomnia; take with food; don’t give donepezil at bedtime; caution with NSAIDs (GI bleeding).","Memantine: NMDA antagonist for moderate to severe disease; blocks excess glutamate; dizziness, headache, confusion, constipation. Namzaric = memantine + donepezil.","Antipsychotics for behavior: black box warning (higher death rates in older adults with dementia); last resort, start low and go slow."]},{"title":"Home safety teaching","lines":["Gradually stop driving; remove throw rugs; safety bars; low water-heater temperature; supervise smoking; lock up medications and cleaning products.","Wandering: an ID bracelet, alert the police and neighbors with a recent photo, locks placed high or complex, door sensors, daytime exercise.","Activities: picture books, music, walking, simple familiar group activities."]},{"title":"Caregiver support","lines":["Alzheimer’s Association (support groups, a 24/7 helpline), adult day care, respite, home health, hospice, Meals on Wheels.","Legal planning early: advance directives, durable power of attorney, guardianship.","Acknowledge the grief and guilt of placement: “This is a difficult decision.”"]}]},{"id":"c-alz","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Count falls and wandering episodes, track weight and intake, continence, sleep, the level of assistance needed, and caregiver coping. Reset outcomes as the disease progresses."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-alz:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which statement by a caregiver shows accurate understanding of a safe environment for a person with dementia?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“The police know he has wandered before.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Preventing wandering with locks (placed high) is a direct safety measure."},{"id":"o1","text":"“I keep the noise down in the house.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Preventing wandering with locks (placed high) is a direct safety measure."},{"id":"o2","text":"“We installed locks on all the outside doors.”","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Preventing wandering with locks (placed high) is a direct safety measure."},{"id":"o3","text":"“Our phone number is pinned inside his shirt pocket.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Preventing wandering with locks (placed high) is a direct safety measure."}]},{"id":"c-alz:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"67-year-old man with Alzheimer’s disease diagnosed 4 years ago. He wanders away from home about four times a week, has fallen getting out of bed at night (“I’m in a sleeping bag in the mountains”), is incontinent when he can’t find the bathroom, can’t find his words, and asked his wife, “Who is this woman?” She sobs: “I feel like I’ve betrayed him.”","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: risk for injury (wandering, falls, burns, eating nonfood items). Then self-care deficits (eating, toileting, dressing), communication, sleep, and caregiver stress.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-alz:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"67-year-old man with Alzheimer’s disease diagnosed 4 years ago. He wanders away from home about four times a week, has fallen getting out of bed at night (“I’m in a sleeping bag in the mountains”), is incontinent when he can’t find the bathroom, can’t find his words, and asked his wife, “Who is this woman?” She sobs: “I feel like I’ve betrayed him.”","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Person-centered care (know his life story; relationships before tasks), with unconditional positive regard. Safety: low bed or mattress on the floor, lighting, remove hazards, locks placed high, alarms, ID bracelet. Communication: name, introduce yourself, one simple direction at a time, distraction rather than confrontation, no quizzing. Routine and structure, finger foods, scheduled toileting, labeled rooms with pictures. Support the family. Count falls and wandering episodes, track weight and intake, continence, sleep, the level of assistance needed, and caregiver coping. Reset outcomes as the disease progresses.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Look for a long-standing pattern of unstable relationships (idealize → devalue), unstable self-image, intense, rapidly shifting moods (emotional lability), impulsivity (spending, sex, substances, binge eating, reckless driving), recurrent self-harm or suicidal behavior, chronic emptiness, intense anger, frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, and brief stress-related paranoia or dissociation. Hear splitting."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: risk for suicide and self-mutilation. Then risk for violence, ineffective coping, disturbed identity, and impaired social interaction."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Measure self-harm incidents, suicidal statements, use of skills and verbalizing instead of acting out, adherence to limits and attendance at DBT."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Cut her wrists and thighs after graduation; calls nurses “angels,” then demands a new one.","rule":"Priority: risk for suicide and self-mutilation. Then risk for violence, ineffective coping, disturbed identity, and impaired social interaction.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-bpd","ch":"24","chapters":["24"],"title":"Borderline personality disorder","notes":["c-bpd"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 24; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Repeated self-harm after a life transition (graduation = a separation), chronic emptiness, idealization then devaluation (splitting), impulsivity with alcohol and sex, and threats when limits are set = borderline personality disorder with high risk of self-mutilation and suicide.","Priority: Priority: risk for suicide and self-mutilation. Then risk for violence, ineffective coping, disturbed identity, and impaired social interaction.","Act: Safety first; a consistent team approach with clear, firm, consistent limits and consequences; straightforward communication; neutral wound care; encourage verbalizing feelings; teach coping and DBT skills; watch for manipulation (flattery, guilt); weekly staff meetings to manage splitting.","Reassess: Measure self-harm incidents, suicidal statements, use of skills and verbalizing instead of acting out, adherence to limits and attendance at DBT."],"terms":["Splitting","Self-mutilation","Fear of abandonment","lithium","naltrexone","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Splitting":"“You’re the best nurse ever. The night nurse is cruel.” Then, a day later, you’re the cruel one. An early defense: people are seen as all good or all bad, because good and bad can’t be held in one picture; linked to disrupted early attachment (Mahler’s separation-individuation). Don’t accept the praise or defend against the attack. Staff stay consistent and united; discuss it in team meetings.","Self-mutilation":"Cutting, burning, pouring drain cleaner on her skin. Relieves unbearable tension or numbness and can summon care. Treat wounds matter-of-factly and neutrally, then ask her to write down the events before and after before discussing it, which builds thinking over acting.","Fear of abandonment":"Rage or self-harm when a nurse goes on vacation or a partner leaves the room. Hypersensitivity to rejection, with a strong limbic reaction and weak frontal control. Prepare her for staff changes and endings ahead of time."},"patient":"24-year-old admitted after cutting her wrists, ankles and labia with glass after her college graduation. She says, “I feel empty inside.” On day 1 she calls the nurses “angels of mercy”; by day 7 she demands a new primary nurse because “she hates me,” sneaks alcohol onto the unit, and threatens to cut again when told no.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-bpd","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Look for a long-standing pattern of unstable relationships (idealize → devalue), unstable self-image, intense, rapidly shifting moods (emotional lability), impulsivity (spending, sex, substances, binge eating, reckless driving), recurrent self-harm or suicidal behavior, chronic emptiness, intense anger, frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, and brief stress-related paranoia or dissociation. Hear splitting."]},{"title":"Facts","lines":["About 70% attempt suicide; about 10% die by suicide.","Heritability is about 46%; low serotonin is linked to impulsive aggression; an overactive limbic system with weak prefrontal control.","Often with depression, bipolar disorder, anxiety, PTSD, substance use and eating disorders.","Symptoms often improve with age: high remission rates over 10 years."]}]},{"id":"c-bpd","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Repeated self-harm after a life transition (graduation = a separation), chronic emptiness, idealization then devaluation (splitting), impulsivity with alcohol and sex, and threats when limits are set = borderline personality disorder with high risk of self-mutilation and suicide."]}]},{"id":"c-bpd","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: risk for suicide and self-mutilation. Then risk for violence, ineffective coping, disturbed identity, and impaired social interaction."]}]},{"id":"c-bpd","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: no self-mutilation or suicide attempts; she talks about feelings with staff instead of acting them out; she follows unit rules within consistent limits; and she uses at least one DBT skill. Change comes one behavior at a time."]}]},{"id":"c-bpd","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Safety first; a consistent team approach with clear, firm, consistent limits and consequences; straightforward communication; neutral wound care; encourage verbalizing feelings; teach coping and DBT skills; watch for manipulation (flattery, guilt); weekly staff meetings to manage splitting."]},{"title":"Treatment","lines":["Dialectical behavior therapy (DBT): the best evidence; mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, interpersonal effectiveness; targets suicidal behavior first.","CBT and schema-focused therapy.","No FDA-approved drug: SSRIs, mood stabilizers (lithium, anticonvulsants) for mood swings, naltrexone for self-injury, second-generation antipsychotics for anger and brief psychosis."]},{"title":"The nurse’s own reactions","lines":["Being idealized feels good; being devalued stings. Expect both.","Use supervision; never take sides between staff."]}]},{"id":"c-bpd","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Measure self-harm incidents, suicidal statements, use of skills and verbalizing instead of acting out, adherence to limits and attendance at DBT."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-bpd:case","kind":"choice","scene":"When assessing a patient with borderline personality disorder, which statement needs immediate attention?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“My mother died 10 years ago.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. A statement of intent to harm someone → assess violence risk now."},{"id":"o1","text":"“I haven’t needed medication in weeks.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. A statement of intent to harm someone → assess violence risk now."},{"id":"o2","text":"“My dad never loved me.”","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. A statement of intent to harm someone → assess violence risk now."},{"id":"o3","text":"“I’d really like to hurt her for hurting me.”","correct":true,"why":"Supported. A statement of intent to harm someone → assess violence risk now."}]},{"id":"c-bpd:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"24-year-old admitted after cutting her wrists, ankles and labia with glass after her college graduation. She says, “I feel empty inside.” On day 1 she calls the nurses “angels of mercy”; by day 7 she demands a new primary nurse because “she hates me,” sneaks alcohol onto the unit, and threatens to cut again when told no.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: risk for suicide and self-mutilation. Then risk for violence, ineffective coping, disturbed identity, and impaired social interaction.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-bpd:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"24-year-old admitted after cutting her wrists, ankles and labia with glass after her college graduation. She says, “I feel empty inside.” On day 1 she calls the nurses “angels of mercy”; by day 7 she demands a new primary nurse because “she hates me,” sneaks alcohol onto the unit, and threatens to cut again when told no.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Safety first; a consistent team approach with clear, firm, consistent limits and consequences; straightforward communication; neutral wound care; encourage verbalizing feelings; teach coping and DBT skills; watch for manipulation (flattery, guilt); weekly staff meetings to manage splitting. Measure self-harm incidents, suicidal statements, use of skills and verbalizing instead of acting out, adherence to limits and attendance at DBT.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Look for a pervasive pattern since age 15 of disregard for and violation of others’ rights: illegal acts, deceit and conning, impulsivity, irritability and fights, recklessness, irresponsibility (work, debts), and lack of remorse. Must be 18 or older with a history of conduct disorder before 15. Notice superficial charm and manipulation."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: risk for violence (to others) and safety of patients and staff. Then impaired impulse control and impaired social interaction."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Count violent or threatening incidents and rule violations, his response to limits and consequences, and any signs of responsibility-taking."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Charming, flirtatious, repeated assaults, blames everyone, “the staff don’t understand me.”","rule":"Priority: risk for violence (to others) and safety of patients and staff. Then impaired impulse control and impaired social interaction.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-aspd","ch":"24","chapters":["24"],"title":"Antisocial personality disorder","notes":["c-aspd"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 24; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Repeated assaults and arrests, deceit, blaming others, charm used to manipulate and conduct problems since adolescence = antisocial personality disorder, with risk for violence and substance use.","Priority: Priority: risk for violence (to others) and safety of patients and staff. Then impaired impulse control and impaired social interaction.","Act: Keep everyone safe; give clear, consistent limits and consequences enforced by the whole team; address manipulation directly; document behavior; help him recognize anger early and offer physical outlets (walking); listen (being heard defuses tension); use behavioral rewards for positive behavior.","Reassess: Count violent or threatening incidents and rule violations, his response to limits and consequences, and any signs of responsibility-taking."],"terms":["Callousness","Manipulation","lithium","valproate","methylphenidate"],"termDefinitions":{"Callousness":"No guilt about hurting others; rationalizes (“he deserved it”). A profound lack of empathy; genetic risk (aggressive disregard, disinhibition), low serotonin with high dopamine, and childhood abuse or neglect. Don’t expect guilt to motivate change; use clear consequences.","Manipulation":"Flattery, flirting, playing staff against each other, making staff feel guilty. People are used as tools to get what he wants. Name the behavior directly and set limits: “Comments like that are not part of our professional relationship.” Document it; the whole team enforces the same limits."},"patient":"25-year-old divorced cab driver referred by the court after an assault charge. He has a history of multiple arrests, substance use and dropping out of three clinics because “the staff don’t understand me.” He is polite and flirtatious with the female nurse, says he isn’t responsible because he is “manic,” and says he can’t tolerate any medication.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-aspd","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Look for a pervasive pattern since age 15 of disregard for and violation of others’ rights: illegal acts, deceit and conning, impulsivity, irritability and fights, recklessness, irresponsibility (work, debts), and lack of remorse. Must be 18 or older with a history of conduct disorder before 15. Notice superficial charm and manipulation."]},{"title":"Facts","lines":["Prevalence 0.2–3.3%; much higher in prisons and among men with substance use.","Symptoms peak in the late teens to mid-20s and often ease after about 40.","Treatment is usually court-ordered; he rarely seeks it for the disorder itself."]}]},{"id":"c-aspd","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Repeated assaults and arrests, deceit, blaming others, charm used to manipulate and conduct problems since adolescence = antisocial personality disorder, with risk for violence and substance use."]}]},{"id":"c-aspd","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: risk for violence (to others) and safety of patients and staff. Then impaired impulse control and impaired social interaction."]}]},{"id":"c-aspd","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: no violence; he follows unit limits; he recognizes anger cues and uses an alternative (walking, talking to staff); takes some responsibility for his actions. Expect small, incremental change."]}]},{"id":"c-aspd","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Keep everyone safe; give clear, consistent limits and consequences enforced by the whole team; address manipulation directly; document behavior; help him recognize anger early and offer physical outlets (walking); listen (being heard defuses tension); use behavioral rewards for positive behavior."]},{"title":"Treatment","lines":["Behavioral therapy (rewards and penalties), CBT, mentalization-based therapy, DBT, anger-management groups, family therapy.","No FDA-approved drug: lithium or valproate for aggression and impulsivity, SSRIs for irritability, methylphenidate if ADHD coexists; use benzodiazepines cautiously (misuse)."]},{"title":"The other personality disorders (snapshot)","lines":["Cluster A (odd): paranoid (distrust; keep promises, be neutral, don’t be too friendly), schizoid (detached loner; respect privacy), schizotypal (magical thinking, odd beliefs; can be made aware, unlike schizophrenia).","Cluster B (dramatic): borderline, antisocial, histrionic (attention-seeking, seductive; keep it professional), narcissistic (grandiose, fragile self-esteem; stay neutral, avoid power struggles).","Cluster C (anxious): avoidant (fears rejection; gentle, accepting), dependent (needs to be cared for; build assertiveness), obsessive-compulsive personality (perfectionism and control; avoid power struggles, allow extra time)."]}]},{"id":"c-aspd","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Count violent or threatening incidents and rule violations, his response to limits and consequences, and any signs of responsibility-taking."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-aspd:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"25-year-old divorced cab driver referred by the court after an assault charge. He has a history of multiple arrests, substance use and dropping out of three clinics because “the staff don’t understand me.” He is polite and flirtatious with the female nurse, says he isn’t responsible because he is “manic,” and says he can’t tolerate any medication.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: risk for violence (to others) and safety of patients and staff. Then impaired impulse control and impaired social interaction.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-aspd:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"25-year-old divorced cab driver referred by the court after an assault charge. He has a history of multiple arrests, substance use and dropping out of three clinics because “the staff don’t understand me.” He is polite and flirtatious with the female nurse, says he isn’t responsible because he is “manic,” and says he can’t tolerate any medication.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Keep everyone safe; give clear, consistent limits and consequences enforced by the whole team; address manipulation directly; document behavior; help him recognize anger early and offer physical outlets (walking); listen (being heard defuses tension); use behavioral rewards for positive behavior. Count violent or threatening incidents and rule violations, his response to limits and consequences, and any signs of responsibility-taking.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Recognize positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions, disorganized speech and behavior), negative symptoms (the six A’s), cognitive problems and affective changes. Hear command hallucinations and paranoia: these are the safety cues. Then check the body: sleep, food, hygiene, water intake (polydipsia) and substance use. Rule out medical causes first."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority one: safety: risk of suicide (command hallucinations) and violence (paranoia). Then the physical: hyponatremia, sleep, nutrition, hygiene. Then disturbed thought and perception, nonadherence and family support."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Reassess safety every shift (commands, paranoia, suicide). Track sodium, intake and weight, sleep, hygiene and meals; whether she can say the voices are a symptom; whether she takes medication without cheeking; and side effects (AIMS for movements, weight, glucose)."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone. See the highlighted clinical update below."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Hearing voices that say she should die, sure she is being followed, stopped her medication.","rule":"Priority one: safety: risk of suicide (command hallucinations) and violence (paranoia). Then the physical: hyponatremia, sleep, nutrition, hygiene. Then disturbed thought and perception, nonadherence and family support.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-schizo","ch":"12","chapters":["12"],"title":"Schizophrenia — acute psychosis","notes":["c-schizo"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 12; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: This is an acute relapse of schizophrenia after stopping medication (because of weight gain, a side effect nobody addressed). Two safety risks stand out: command hallucinations telling her to die and paranoia. There is also a medical problem: hyponatremia from polydipsia may be adding confusion. Suicide risk in schizophrenia is high (5–10% die by suicide).","Priority: Priority one: safety: risk of suicide (command hallucinations) and violence (paranoia). Then the physical: hyponatremia, sleep, nutrition, hygiene. Then disturbed thought and perception, nonadherence and family support.","Act: Keep her safe and calm (low stimulation, a room near the station, closer observation). Build trust with short, frequent, honest contacts. With hallucinations: ask what she hears, don’t argue, state your reality, respond to the feeling and teach competing stimuli. With delusions: never debate; focus on the feeling and present reality. Manage fluids and sodium. Restart an antipsychotic she can tolerate, watch for cheeking, and teach the patient and family.","Reassess: Reassess safety every shift (commands, paranoia, suicide). Track sodium, intake and weight, sleep, hygiene and meals; whether she can say the voices are a symptom; whether she takes medication without cheeking; and side effects (AIMS for movements, weight, glucose).","Assess command content, intent, ability to resist, access to means and other actual risk factors. Most people with schizophrenia are not violent; respond to this patient’s findings and needs."],"terms":["Auditory hallucinations","Command hallucination","Persecutory delusion","Paranoia","Negative symptoms (the A’s)","Polydipsia and water intoxication","Anosognosia","clozapine","olanzapine","quetiapine","aripiprazole","haloperidol","risperidone","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Auditory hallucinations":"She turns her head toward the corner, whispers between answers and laughs at nothing. The brain’s hearing areas fire with no outside sound; dopamine overactivity in the mesolimbic pathway is the main theory. The voice feels as real as yours. Ask “What are you hearing?” (not “What are the voices saying?”). Ask whether they tell her to do anything (command hallucinations) and whether she believes them.","Command hallucination":"“The voice says I don’t deserve to live and should jump.” A voice directing an action. Risk is highest when the patient believes the voice is real and feels unable to resist. A psychiatric emergency cue: assess suicide and violence risk, increase observation, and tell the provider.","Persecutory delusion":"“They’re following me. They put a chip in my head.” A fixed false belief held despite evidence, kept in place by impaired reality testing. Never argue with the delusion and never agree. Respond to the feeling (fear) and help her feel safe.","Paranoia":"Suspicious, guarded, won’t eat food from the tray, watches staff. An irrational fear that others intend harm. The symptom most linked to violence toward others (she may act in “self-defense”). Keep a calm distance, explain everything, and offer sealed or packaged food.","Negative symptoms (the A’s)":"Flat face, says little, stays in bed, no interest in anything or anyone. Anhedonia, avolition, asociality, affective blunting, apathy, alogia: the loss of normal functions, linked to low frontal-lobe activity. Harder to treat than positive symptoms and more disabling. They are not laziness; break tasks into small steps.","Polydipsia and water intoxication":"Carrying water bottles everywhere, dilute urine, and new confusion or worse psychosis. Compulsive drinking (and antipsychotic dry mouth) dilutes the blood sodium (hyponatremia). A sudden worsening of psychosis or confusion → check sodium. Track weights and intake, and restrict fluids as ordered.","Anosognosia":"“I’m not sick. Why would I take medicine for nothing?” The illness itself damages the brain’s ability to recognize the illness; it isn’t denial. The leading cause of stopping medication. Don’t argue; tie treatment to her goals (“to quiet the voices,” “to keep your job”)."},"patient":"24-year-old brought in by her mother after 3 weeks without sleep, mumbling to herself, and covering her windows with foil “so they can’t read my thoughts.” She stopped her antipsychotic 2 months ago because “it made me gain weight.” She drinks large amounts of water and smokes 2 packs a day. She looks to the corner of the room and whispers between answers.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-schizo","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Recognize positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions, disorganized speech and behavior), negative symptoms (the six A’s), cognitive problems and affective changes. Hear command hallucinations and paranoia: these are the safety cues. Then check the body: sleep, food, hygiene, water intake (polydipsia) and substance use. Rule out medical causes first."]},{"title":"Diagnosis","lines":["2 or more for a significant part of 1 month: delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior, negative symptoms. At least one must be one of the first three.","Functioning is well below what it was before.","Continuous signs for at least 6 months (including 1 month of active symptoms).","Not caused by a substance or a medical condition; not a mood disorder with psychosis."]},{"title":"The other spectrum disorders","lines":["Delusional disorder: delusions for 1 month or more; otherwise functions fairly normally.","Brief psychotic disorder: symptoms for more than 1 day and less than 1 month, then a full return to normal.","Schizophreniform: like schizophrenia but less than 6 months.","Schizoaffective: schizophrenia plus a major mood episode.","Substance- or medically induced psychosis: rule these out first (drugs, alcohol withdrawal, delirium, anticholinergic toxicity, fever, brain injury)."]},{"title":"Speech and thought words","lines":["Associative looseness → word salad (extreme): ideas barely connected.","Clang association: words picked by sound (“track… Big Mac”).","Neologism: a made-up word (“his mannerologies are poor”).","Echolalia: repeats your words. Echopraxia: copies your movements.","Concrete thinking: “What brought you here?” “A cab.”","Thought blocking, thought insertion, thought deletion, magical thinking, circumstantiality, tangentiality."]},{"title":"Phases","lines":["Prodromal: months of withdrawal, falling grades, odd ideas, feeling “something is wrong.”","Acute: hallucinations, delusions, disorganization; often needs hospitalization.","Stabilization: symptoms ease; outpatient or partial hospitalization.","Maintenance (residual): positive symptoms fade; negative and cognitive ones often stay. Relapse prevention is the focus."]}]},{"id":"c-schizo","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["This is an acute relapse of schizophrenia after stopping medication (because of weight gain, a side effect nobody addressed). Two safety risks stand out: command hallucinations telling her to die and paranoia. There is also a medical problem: hyponatremia from polydipsia may be adding confusion. Suicide risk in schizophrenia is high (5–10% die by suicide)."]},{"title":"Why schizophrenia happens","lines":["About 80% genetic: an identical twin has a ~50% chance.","Dopamine excess in the mesolimbic pathway (positive symptoms), low dopamine in the frontal cortex (negative and cognitive); serotonin, glutamate and GABA are also involved.","Brain changes: enlarged ventricles, smaller hippocampus, less gray matter and less prefrontal activity.","Triggers in a vulnerable person: marijuana and stimulants, prenatal infection and malnutrition, older father, trauma, urban upbringing, stress."]},{"title":"Why patients stop medication","lines":["Side effects: weight gain, sedation, sexual dysfunction, EPS.","Anosognosia (doesn’t believe she is ill).","Stigma, cost, forgetting, mistrust.","Each relapse can leave more lasting damage, so adherence is worth fighting for."]}]},{"id":"c-schizo","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority one: safety: risk of suicide (command hallucinations) and violence (paranoia). Then the physical: hyponatremia, sleep, nutrition, hygiene. Then disturbed thought and perception, nonadherence and family support."]},{"title":"Priority problems","lines":["Risk for suicide and risk for violence (command hallucinations, paranoia).","Fluid and electrolyte imbalance (polydipsia), self-care deficit, impaired sleep.","Hallucinations, delusions, distorted thinking.","Nonadherence and lack of knowledge of the illness."]}]},{"id":"c-schizo","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Acute phase outcomes: she stays safe (no harm to self or others), sodium returns to normal, she sleeps and eats, and she begins to label the voices as “a symptom, not real” and doesn’t act on them. Later: she understands the illness, takes medication she can tolerate, knows her early relapse signs (less sleep, withdrawal, poor concentration), avoids drugs and connects with NAMI and a community mental health center."]}]},{"id":"c-schizo","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Keep her safe and calm (low stimulation, a room near the station, closer observation). Build trust with short, frequent, honest contacts. With hallucinations: ask what she hears, don’t argue, state your reality, respond to the feeling and teach competing stimuli. With delusions: never debate; focus on the feeling and present reality. Manage fluids and sodium. Restart an antipsychotic she can tolerate, watch for cheeking, and teach the patient and family."]},{"title":"Responding to hallucinations","lines":["Ask “What are you hearing?” Don’t talk about the voices as if they were real.","“I don’t hear the voice you hear, but it sounds frightening.” Don’t say “There are no voices” (dismissive) or “What else do they say?” (feeds them).","Focus on here-and-now, reality-based activities.","Teach competing stimuli: humming, music, talking with someone, reading aloud.","Teach reality testing: “Do the people around you seem to hear it?”","Call her by name, use simple words, and redirect her to your conversation."]},{"title":"Responding to delusions","lines":["Never debate the content and never agree with it.","Respond to the feeling or theme: “It must be frightening to feel followed. You’re safe here.”","Validate any part that is real: “Yes, a man was at the desk, but he didn’t mention you.”","Don’t dwell on it; refocus on reality-based topics.","As reality testing improves, gently voice doubt: “I wonder if there could be another explanation?”"]},{"title":"Disorganized speech","lines":["Don’t pretend to understand: “I’m having trouble following you” (put the difficulty on yourself).","Tell her what you did understand; summarize; look for themes.","Speak in short, concrete sentences."]},{"title":"Paranoia and violence risk","lines":["Keep a calm, nonthreatening stance and a safe distance; don’t touch without warning.","Explain everything before you do it; offer packaged food if she fears poisoning.","Watch for rising tension; de-escalate early; seclusion and restraint only as a last resort.","Separate her from any peer she has targeted."]},{"title":"Antipsychotic basics","lines":["First generation (haloperidol, fluphenazine, chlorpromazine): D2 blockers; treat positive symptoms; high EPS; cheap.","Second generation (risperidone, olanzapine, quetiapine, aripiprazole, clozapine…): D2 + 5-HT2A; positive and some negative symptoms; less EPS; metabolic syndrome risk.","Take 2–6 weeks to work; not addictive, but taper off; can impair swallowing (choking) and cause falls (orthostasis, sedation).","Cheeking or palming: mouth checks; liquid or dissolving forms; long-acting injectables (every 2–4 weeks, even 12) improve adherence.","After 2 failed drugs → clozapine (also lowers suicide risk)."]},{"title":"Teaching the patient and family","lines":["Schizophrenia is a brain illness, like diabetes: managed long term.","Early warning signs of relapse: less sleep, withdrawal, poor concentration, odd ideas.","Avoid marijuana, alcohol and stimulants; limit caffeine and tobacco (smoking also lowers clozapine and olanzapine levels).","Weight, glucose and lipid checks; healthy diet and activity.","Families: low criticism, simple calm communication; NAMI Family-to-Family; respite."]}]},{"id":"c-schizo","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Reassess safety every shift (commands, paranoia, suicide). Track sodium, intake and weight, sleep, hygiene and meals; whether she can say the voices are a symptom; whether she takes medication without cheeking; and side effects (AIMS for movements, weight, glucose)."]},{"title":"If it worsens","lines":["Psychosis suddenly worse plus confusion → check sodium and for anticholinergic toxicity, and get a medical evaluation.","Rigid, feverish, confused → think NMS (next condition).","Hopelessness or sudden calm → reassess suicide."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Assess command content, intent, ability to resist, access to means and other actual risk factors. Most people with schizophrenia are not violent; respond to this patient’s findings and needs.","sources":[{"title":"NIMH: schizophrenia, symptoms and individualized risk","url":"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/schizophrenia"},{"title":"DailyMed: haloperidol labeling, NMS recognition and management","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b5a6c54c-fb70-4132-90ae-7d75b5e9c9de"}],"steps":[{"id":"c-schizo:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient starting a first-generation antipsychotic asks what it will help. The nurse’s best response is that it should mainly:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Improve memory problems","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. First-generation drugs mainly treat positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions); they do little for negative or cognitive symptoms."},{"id":"o1","text":"Make depression less severe","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. First-generation drugs mainly treat positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions); they do little for negative or cognitive symptoms."},{"id":"o2","text":"Increase enjoyment of social contact","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. First-generation drugs mainly treat positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions); they do little for negative or cognitive symptoms."},{"id":"o3","text":"Reduce hallucinations","correct":true,"why":"Supported. First-generation drugs mainly treat positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions); they do little for negative or cognitive symptoms."}]},{"id":"c-schizo:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"24-year-old brought in by her mother after 3 weeks without sleep, mumbling to herself, and covering her windows with foil “so they can’t read my thoughts.” She stopped her antipsychotic 2 months ago because “it made me gain weight.” She drinks large amounts of water and smokes 2 packs a day. She looks to the corner of the room and whispers between answers.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority one: safety: risk of suicide (command hallucinations) and violence (paranoia). Then the physical: hyponatremia, sleep, nutrition, hygiene. Then disturbed thought and perception, nonadherence and family support.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-schizo:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"24-year-old brought in by her mother after 3 weeks without sleep, mumbling to herself, and covering her windows with foil “so they can’t read my thoughts.” She stopped her antipsychotic 2 months ago because “it made me gain weight.” She drinks large amounts of water and smokes 2 packs a day. She looks to the corner of the room and whispers between answers.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Keep her safe and calm (low stimulation, a room near the station, closer observation). Build trust with short, frequent, honest contacts. With hallucinations: ask what she hears, don’t argue, state your reality, respond to the feeling and teach competing stimuli. With delusions: never debate; focus on the feeling and present reality. Manage fluids and sodium. Restart an antipsychotic she can tolerate, watch for cheeking, and teach the patient and family. Reassess safety every shift (commands, paranoia, suicide). Track sodium, intake and weight, sleep, hygiene and meals; whether she can say the voices are a symptom; whether she takes medication without cheeking; and side effects (AIMS for movements, weight, glucose).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Know the side effects by timing and look. Acute dystonia (hours to days: sudden muscle spasm of the neck, eyes or tongue). Akathisia (days to weeks: can’t sit still, inner restlessness). Pseudoparkinsonism (weeks: tremor, shuffling, mask face). Tardive dyskinesia (months to years: lip smacking, tongue writhing). The emergencies: NMS (rigidity + high fever + altered mental status + unstable vitals), anticholinergic toxicity and severe neutropenia."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"NMS is the emergency: hyperthermia, risk of airway or respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and kidney injury. Everything else waits."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Monitor temperature, vital signs, mental status, rigidity, CK, kidney function and urine output until normal. Document the reaction as an allergy or adverse event, and make sure any future antipsychotic is restarted cautiously, after full recovery and at a low dose."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Day 5 of haloperidol: rigid, temperature 40.1 °C, confused, BP swinging.","rule":"NMS is the emergency: hyperthermia, risk of airway or respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and kidney injury. Everything else waits.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-nms","ch":"12","chapters":["12"],"title":"Antipsychotic side-effect emergencies","notes":["c-nms"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 12; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Rigidity + fever + altered consciousness + unstable vital signs on a first-generation antipsychotic early in treatment = NMS until proven otherwise. The earlier dystonia shows he is sensitive to D2 blockade. Check CK (very high), WBC, kidney function and electrolytes.","Priority: NMS is the emergency: hyperthermia, risk of airway or respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and kidney injury. Everything else waits.","Act: Hold all antipsychotics and call the prescriber stat; transfer to critical care. Cool (cooling blanket, ice packs or bath as ordered), give IV fluids and correct electrolytes, monitor the airway, rhythm and urine output, and give dantrolene (muscle relaxant) or bromocriptine (dopamine agonist) as ordered. For the milder EPS, treat as below.","Reassess: Monitor temperature, vital signs, mental status, rigidity, CK, kidney function and urine output until normal. Document the reaction as an allergy or adverse event, and make sure any future antipsychotic is restarted cautiously, after full recovery and at a low dose."],"terms":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)","Acute dystonia","Akathisia","Pseudoparkinsonism","Tardive dyskinesia","Anticholinergic toxicity","clozapine","olanzapine","quetiapine","benztropine","dantrolene","diazepam","lorazepam","haloperidol","risperidone","paliperidone","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)":"“Lead-pipe” rigidity, temperature above 103 °F (39.4 °C), confusion or stupor, sweating, labile BP, tachycardia, incontinence. Sudden, excessive dopamine blockade in the hypothalamus (temperature control) and the muscles; the rigid muscles generate heat and break down (rhabdomyolysis, high CK) → kidney failure, respiratory failure. Life-threatening emergency (about 6% die). Hold all antipsychotics, call the prescriber stat, cool, hydrate, transfer to critical care; dantrolene or bromocriptine.","Acute dystonia":"Sudden painful spasm: neck twisted (torticollis), eyes rolled up (oculogyric crisis), tongue protruding, back arched. D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway upsets the dopamine–acetylcholine balance that controls movement. Frightening, rarely dangerous (unless laryngeal: watch the airway). Give benztropine or diphenhydramine IM; relief in 5–15 minutes. Stay with him.","Akathisia":"Paces, shifts from foot to foot, rocks, says “I can’t sit still.” Motor restlessness from D2 blockade. Often mistaken for anxiety or agitation, which leads to giving more of the same drug. Report it; propranolol, a benzodiazepine or a medication change. Severe akathisia raises suicide risk.","Pseudoparkinsonism":"Mask-like face, shuffling gait, pill-rolling tremor, stiffness, drooling, slow movement. Too little dopamine effect in the movement circuits, as in Parkinson disease. Antiparkinsonian drug (benztropine, trihexyphenidyl), dose reduction; fall and choking precautions.","Tardive dyskinesia":"Lip smacking, tongue writhing or protruding, chewing, grimacing, finger and trunk movements. Long-term D2 blockade causes the receptors to become oversensitive; it develops after months to years. Often permanent. Screen with the AIMS at least every 3 months; valbenazine or deutetrabenazine; consider a second-generation drug.","Anticholinergic toxicity":"Hot, dry, flushed skin with fever, dilated pupils, urinary retention, no bowel sounds, tachycardia, delirium, worsening “psychosis.” Too much muscarinic blockade from several anticholinergic drugs (antipsychotic + benztropine + diphenhydramine). Emergency: hold medications, cool, catheterize as needed; specialist-directed physostigmine (after contraindication review, with cardiac monitoring). Suspect it whenever psychosis suddenly worsens."},"patient":"30-year-old man on day 5 of haloperidol for acute psychosis. This morning he is stiff “like a board,” sweating, barely responds, and is incontinent. BP 172/104, then 98/60 an hour later; HR 124; temperature 40.1 °C (104.2 °F). Two days ago his neck twisted to one side and his eyes rolled up; benztropine fixed it within 20 minutes.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-nms","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Know the side effects by timing and look. Acute dystonia (hours to days: sudden muscle spasm of the neck, eyes or tongue). Akathisia (days to weeks: can’t sit still, inner restlessness). Pseudoparkinsonism (weeks: tremor, shuffling, mask face). Tardive dyskinesia (months to years: lip smacking, tongue writhing). The emergencies: NMS (rigidity + high fever + altered mental status + unstable vitals), anticholinergic toxicity and severe neutropenia."]},{"title":"NMS vs serotonin syndrome vs anticholinergic toxicity","lines":["NMS: antipsychotic; rigid “lead pipe”; slow onset (days); sweating.","Serotonin syndrome: serotonergic drugs; clonus and hyperreflexia, tremor; fast onset (hours); diarrhea.","Anticholinergic toxicity: hot and dry, dilated pupils, urinary retention, delirium.","All three cause fever, tachycardia and confusion: the muscle findings and the drug history tell them apart."]}]},{"id":"c-nms","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Rigidity + fever + altered consciousness + unstable vital signs on a first-generation antipsychotic early in treatment = NMS until proven otherwise. The earlier dystonia shows he is sensitive to D2 blockade. Check CK (very high), WBC, kidney function and electrolytes."]},{"title":"Who is at risk of which effect","lines":["High-potency first generation (haloperidol, fluphenazine): highest EPS and NMS risk.","Low-potency first generation (chlorpromazine): more sedation, anticholinergic effects and orthostasis.","Clozapine and olanzapine: highest metabolic risk; clozapine → neutropenia, seizures, myocarditis.","Risperidone and paliperidone: high prolactin. Ziprasidone, quetiapine and haloperidol → QT prolongation (get an ECG before starting)."]}]},{"id":"c-nms","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["NMS is the emergency: hyperthermia, risk of airway or respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and kidney injury. Everything else waits."]}]},{"id":"c-nms","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: temperature, BP and HR return to normal; rigidity resolves; CK falls; urine output stays at least 30 mL/hr with no kidney injury; he survives without complications. Later, a safer antipsychotic is chosen and the patient and family know the warning signs."]}]},{"id":"c-nms","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Hold all antipsychotics and call the prescriber stat; transfer to critical care. Cool (cooling blanket, ice packs or bath as ordered), give IV fluids and correct electrolytes, monitor the airway, rhythm and urine output, and give dantrolene (muscle relaxant) or bromocriptine (dopamine agonist) as ordered. For the milder EPS, treat as below."]},{"title":"Side-effect care, at a glance","lines":["Acute dystonia: benztropine or diphenhydramine IM; check the airway; reassure; consider a preventive oral antiparkinsonian.","Akathisia: propranolol, lorazepam, diazepam or benztropine; consider changing the drug.","Pseudoparkinsonism: benztropine or trihexyphenidyl; fall precautions; towel for drooling.","Tardive dyskinesia: AIMS every 3 months; valbenazine or deutetrabenazine (watch the QT and sedation).","Orthostatic hypotension: lying and standing BP; hold and call if standing systolic is below 80; rise slowly.","Anticholinergic effects: sips of water, sugarless gum, fiber and fluids, check for a full bladder.","Metabolic syndrome: weight, waist, glucose and lipids; diet and activity; metformin may be added.","Clozapine neutropenia: ANC weekly for 6 months, every 2 weeks for 6 months, then monthly; ANC below 1000 → hold and notify; teach to report fever and sore throat."]}]},{"id":"c-nms","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Monitor temperature, vital signs, mental status, rigidity, CK, kidney function and urine output until normal. Document the reaction as an allergy or adverse event, and make sure any future antipsychotic is restarted cautiously, after full recovery and at a low dose."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[{"title":"Utah Poison Control: physostigmine precautions and monitoring","url":"https://poisoncontrol.utah.edu/news/2024/02/physostigmine-or-rivastigmine-anticholinergic-toxicity"},{"title":"DailyMed: haloperidol labeling, NMS recognition and management","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b5a6c54c-fb70-4132-90ae-7d75b5e9c9de"}],"steps":[{"id":"c-nms:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"30-year-old man on day 5 of haloperidol for acute psychosis. This morning he is stiff “like a board,” sweating, barely responds, and is incontinent. BP 172/104, then 98/60 an hour later; HR 124; temperature 40.1 °C (104.2 °F). Two days ago his neck twisted to one side and his eyes rolled up; benztropine fixed it within 20 minutes.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"NMS is the emergency: hyperthermia, risk of airway or respiratory failure, rhabdomyolysis and kidney injury. Everything else waits.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-nms:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"30-year-old man on day 5 of haloperidol for acute psychosis. This morning he is stiff “like a board,” sweating, barely responds, and is incontinent. BP 172/104, then 98/60 an hour later; HR 124; temperature 40.1 °C (104.2 °F). Two days ago his neck twisted to one side and his eyes rolled up; benztropine fixed it within 20 minutes.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Hold all antipsychotics and call the prescriber stat; transfer to critical care. Cool (cooling blanket, ice packs or bath as ordered), give IV fluids and correct electrolytes, monitor the airway, rhythm and urine output, and give dantrolene (muscle relaxant) or bromocriptine (dopamine agonist) as ordered. For the milder EPS, treat as below. Monitor temperature, vital signs, mental status, rigidity, CK, kidney function and urine output until normal. Document the reaction as an allergy or adverse event, and make sure any future antipsychotic is restarted cautiously, after full recovery and at a low dose.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Recognize real, distressing physical symptoms (pain, fatigue, palpitations, GI symptoms) with excessive thoughts, worry or time spent on them for more than 6 months (somatic symptom disorder); worry about having an illness with few or no symptoms (illness anxiety); or a neurological loss (paralysis, blindness, seizures) with no neurological cause (conversion), often with la belle indifférence. Always rule out a medical cause first."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priorities: ineffective coping, anxiety, pain, social isolation (loneliness) and low self-esteem; for factitious disorder imposed on another, the child’s safety comes first."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Evaluate the number of ED visits, use of coping skills, social contacts and her statements about her symptoms. Symptoms may persist, but concern and impairment should drop."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Recurrent chest pain and palpitations with normal tests; another patient suddenly can’t move her arm.","rule":"Priorities: ineffective coping, anxiety, pain, social isolation (loneliness) and low self-esteem; for factitious disorder imposed on another, the child’s safety comes first.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-somatic","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Somatic symptom and conversion disorders","notes":["c-somatic"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 17; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Normal work-ups, repeated visits, excessive health worry, a recent loss (divorce, children leaving) and isolation and loneliness point to somatic symptom disorder, with unresolved grief and few coping resources.","Priority: Priorities: ineffective coping, anxiety, pain, social isolation (loneliness) and low self-esteem; for factitious disorder imposed on another, the child’s safety comes first.","Act: Build a trusting relationship; acknowledge the symptoms as real; after a medical work-up, avoid more tests and don’t reinforce complaints; shift the focus to feelings and coping; teach relaxation and assertiveness; keep one consistent provider with brief, regular visits; refer for CBT.","Reassess: Evaluate the number of ED visits, use of coping skills, social contacts and her statements about her symptoms. Symptoms may persist, but concern and impairment should drop."],"terms":["Somatization","La belle indifférence","Secondary gain"],"termDefinitions":{"Somatization":"Distress shows up as back pain, palpitations, headaches or stomachaches, not as sadness or worry. Stress activates the body (HPA axis, sympathetic system), and some people and cultures experience distress physically. The symptoms are real, not faked or under conscious control. Never say “it’s all in your head.”","La belle indifférence":"Calmly discusses sudden blindness or paralysis. In conversion, the symptom may “solve” an unbearable conflict, so the anxiety is relieved. A clue to conversion, but assume an organic cause until it is ruled out.","Secondary gain":"Extra attention, being excused from duties, others taking over care. The benefits of the sick role reinforce the symptoms (not consciously). Reduce it: attend to the person at times other than complaints; encourage self-care."},"patient":"49-year-old recently divorced mother in the ED for the fourth time this year with palpitations, shortness of breath and fatigue. Cardiac work-ups are normal. She says, “My mood is fine, but my body is a major problem,” leaves home only for work and groceries, and says, “I don’t have any friends.” (A second patient: a woman who suddenly can’t see after witnessing her husband’s affair, and calmly discusses it.)","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-somatic","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Recognize real, distressing physical symptoms (pain, fatigue, palpitations, GI symptoms) with excessive thoughts, worry or time spent on them for more than 6 months (somatic symptom disorder); worry about having an illness with few or no symptoms (illness anxiety); or a neurological loss (paralysis, blindness, seizures) with no neurological cause (conversion), often with la belle indifférence. Always rule out a medical cause first."]},{"title":"Tell them apart","lines":["Somatic symptom disorder: real symptoms + excessive worry and time spent, more than 6 months; high help-seeking.","Illness anxiety disorder: fear of having or getting a serious illness; symptoms mild or absent; care-seeking (constant checks) or care-avoidant types.","Conversion (functional neurological) disorder: motor or sensory loss with no neurological cause; often after a stressor.","Psychological factors affecting a medical condition: stress or depression worsening a real disease (heart disease, ulcers, hypertension).","Factitious disorder: consciously fakes or induces illness to be a patient (no outside reward); imposed on another = a caregiver harms a dependent (Munchausen by proxy).","Malingering: consciously fakes for an external reward (money, drugs, avoiding work or jail)."]}]},{"id":"c-somatic","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Normal work-ups, repeated visits, excessive health worry, a recent loss (divorce, children leaving) and isolation and loneliness point to somatic symptom disorder, with unresolved grief and few coping resources."]}]},{"id":"c-somatic","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priorities: ineffective coping, anxiety, pain, social isolation (loneliness) and low self-esteem; for factitious disorder imposed on another, the child’s safety comes first."]}]},{"id":"c-somatic","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: she identifies feelings and stressors linked to her symptoms, uses coping and relaxation skills, keeps scheduled visits instead of ED visits, reconnects with people, and reports less concern even if symptoms continue."]}]},{"id":"c-somatic","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Build a trusting relationship; acknowledge the symptoms as real; after a medical work-up, avoid more tests and don’t reinforce complaints; shift the focus to feelings and coping; teach relaxation and assertiveness; keep one consistent provider with brief, regular visits; refer for CBT."]},{"title":"Nursing interventions","lines":["Once complaints have been checked, don’t recheck vitals at every complaint.","Spend time with her when she isn’t complaining.","Don’t imply symptoms aren’t real; don’t confront the conversion symptom directly.","Matter-of-fact support for self-care (e.g., eat with the working arm).","Teach stress reduction, assertive communication and exercise; build self-compassion and social connection.","Case management to prevent “doctor shopping.”"]},{"title":"Factitious disorder and malingering","lines":["Factitious: monitor safety, report suspicious findings to the team, avoid angry confrontation; management rather than cure.","Imposed on another: suspected child abuse → mandatory report and protect the child.","Notice your own anger at faked illness, and get supervision."]}]},{"id":"c-somatic","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Evaluate the number of ED visits, use of coping skills, social contacts and her statements about her symptoms. Symptoms may persist, but concern and impairment should drop."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-somatic:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which outcome shows success for a patient with somatic symptom disorder and a diagnosis of impaired coping?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Showers and dresses in clean clothes daily","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Reaching out to supports instead of focusing on symptoms shows improved coping."},{"id":"o1","text":"Calls a friend to talk when feeling lonely","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Reaching out to supports instead of focusing on symptoms shows improved coping."},{"id":"o2","text":"Spends more time describing her abdominal pain","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Reaching out to supports instead of focusing on symptoms shows improved coping."},{"id":"o3","text":"Maintains focus and concentration","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Reaching out to supports instead of focusing on symptoms shows improved coping."}]},{"id":"c-somatic:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"49-year-old recently divorced mother in the ED for the fourth time this year with palpitations, shortness of breath and fatigue. Cardiac work-ups are normal. She says, “My mood is fine, but my body is a major problem,” leaves home only for work and groceries, and says, “I don’t have any friends.” (A second patient: a woman who suddenly can’t see after witnessing her husband’s affair, and calmly discusses it.)","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priorities: ineffective coping, anxiety, pain, social isolation (loneliness) and low self-esteem; for factitious disorder imposed on another, the child’s safety comes first.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-somatic:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"49-year-old recently divorced mother in the ED for the fourth time this year with palpitations, shortness of breath and fatigue. Cardiac work-ups are normal. She says, “My mood is fine, but my body is a major problem,” leaves home only for work and groceries, and says, “I don’t have any friends.” (A second patient: a woman who suddenly can’t see after witnessing her husband’s affair, and calmly discusses it.)","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Build a trusting relationship; acknowledge the symptoms as real; after a medical work-up, avoid more tests and don’t reinforce complaints; shift the focus to feelings and coping; teach relaxation and assertiveness; keep one consistent provider with brief, regular visits; refer for CBT. Evaluate the number of ED visits, use of coping skills, social contacts and her statements about her symptoms. Symptoms may persist, but concern and impairment should drop.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Recognize dissociation: gaps in memory (“lost time”), depersonalization (detached from self) or derealization (world unreal), amnesia for personal information (sometimes with fugue: travel with a new identity), or two or more distinct identities (alters) that take control. Screen for self-harm and suicide (very high in DID), trauma history, PTSD, depression and substance use. Rule out seizures, head injury and substances."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: risk for self-harm and suicide, then disturbed identity, anxiety and role performance."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Evaluate for no self-harm, fewer episodes of lost time, grounding used effectively, less anxiety and engagement in therapy."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Deep cuts on her arms she can’t remember making; strangers greet her by another name.","rule":"Priority: risk for self-harm and suicide, then disturbed identity, anxiety and role performance.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-did","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Dissociative identity disorder and amnesia","notes":["c-did"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 16; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Self-injury that she can’t remember, plus alter behavior, means safety can’t be reliably judged from one conversation. The underlying cause is severe early trauma.","Priority: Priority: risk for self-harm and suicide, then disturbed identity, anxiety and role performance.","Act: Ensure safety (close observation, 1:1 if needed), give a simple, predictable routine, orient her to time, place and identity, teach grounding (feet on the floor, holding ice, 5-4-3-2-1, a shower), let memories come at her own pace (don’t flood her with details), support her through painful disclosures, and refer for phased trauma therapy (safety → processing → integration).","Reassess: Evaluate for no self-harm, fewer episodes of lost time, grounding used effectively, less anxiety and engagement in therapy."],"terms":["Alters","Depersonalization and derealization","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Alters":"A change in voice, posture, name or handedness; using “we”; others report behavior she can’t recall. Severe, repeated childhood trauma before a unified identity forms; parts of experience are split off to survive it. Speak to whoever is present respectfully, but keep focusing on the whole person; safety covers all alters.","Depersonalization and derealization":"“I’m watching myself in a movie.” “Everything looks foggy and unreal.” The brain dampens emotion and sensation to escape overwhelming stress. Grounding brings her back to the present. Reality testing stays intact (unlike psychosis)."},"patient":"29-year-old admitted after arriving at the ED with deep cuts on her arms and no memory of how they happened. She describes “losing time,” finding clothes she doesn’t remember buying, and people calling her by another name. History of severe childhood abuse. She refers to herself as “we” at times.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-did","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Recognize dissociation: gaps in memory (“lost time”), depersonalization (detached from self) or derealization (world unreal), amnesia for personal information (sometimes with fugue: travel with a new identity), or two or more distinct identities (alters) that take control. Screen for self-harm and suicide (very high in DID), trauma history, PTSD, depression and substance use. Rule out seizures, head injury and substances."]},{"title":"Facts","lines":["About 90% of people with DID experienced childhood abuse or neglect; most also have PTSD.","Up to 70% of outpatients with DID have attempted suicide.","Often misdiagnosed as schizophrenia, depression or borderline personality disorder.","Dissociative amnesia usually resolves on its own once the stress is gone."]}]},{"id":"c-did","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Self-injury that she can’t remember, plus alter behavior, means safety can’t be reliably judged from one conversation. The underlying cause is severe early trauma."]}]},{"id":"c-did","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: risk for self-harm and suicide, then disturbed identity, anxiety and role performance."]}]},{"id":"c-did","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: no self-harm; she uses grounding when she starts to dissociate; keeps a journal of lost time and triggers; and engages in long-term trauma therapy aimed at integration."]}]},{"id":"c-did","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Ensure safety (close observation, 1:1 if needed), give a simple, predictable routine, orient her to time, place and identity, teach grounding (feet on the floor, holding ice, 5-4-3-2-1, a shower), let memories come at her own pace (don’t flood her with details), support her through painful disclosures, and refer for phased trauma therapy (safety → processing → integration)."]},{"title":"Self-awareness for the nurse","lines":["Skepticism or fascination are common reactions; get supervision.","Trust builds slowly: these patients have had trust betrayed.","Crises can be sudden; plan for them."]}]},{"id":"c-did","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Evaluate for no self-harm, fewer episodes of lost time, grounding used effectively, less anxiety and engagement in therapy."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-did:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with dissociative identity disorder was admitted with deep arm cuts she doesn’t remember making. The priority intervention is to:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Help her recover memories of abuse","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Safety first: self-harm risk can’t be reliably judged across identities."},{"id":"o1","text":"Maintain 1:1 observation","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Safety first: self-harm risk can’t be reliably judged across identities."},{"id":"o2","text":"Teach stress-management strategies","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Safety first: self-harm risk can’t be reliably judged across identities."},{"id":"o3","text":"Refer for integrative therapy","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Safety first: self-harm risk can’t be reliably judged across identities."}]},{"id":"c-did:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"29-year-old admitted after arriving at the ED with deep cuts on her arms and no memory of how they happened. She describes “losing time,” finding clothes she doesn’t remember buying, and people calling her by another name. History of severe childhood abuse. She refers to herself as “we” at times.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: risk for self-harm and suicide, then disturbed identity, anxiety and role performance.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-did:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"29-year-old admitted after arriving at the ED with deep cuts on her arms and no memory of how they happened. She describes “losing time,” finding clothes she doesn’t remember buying, and people calling her by another name. History of severe childhood abuse. She refers to herself as “we” at times.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Ensure safety (close observation, 1:1 if needed), give a simple, predictable routine, orient her to time, place and identity, teach grounding (feet on the floor, holding ice, 5-4-3-2-1, a shower), let memories come at her own pace (don’t flood her with details), support her through painful disclosures, and refer for phased trauma therapy (safety → processing → integration). Evaluate for no self-harm, fewer episodes of lost time, grounding used effectively, less anxiety and engagement in therapy.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Know the timeline: tremor (\"the shakes\") 6–8 hours after the last drink; hallucinations or psychosis from 8–10 hours; withdrawal seizures at 12–24 hours; delirium tremens (DTs) any time in the first 72 hours. Watch autonomic signs (↑ HR, BP and temperature, sweating), agitation, vomiting, confusion and visual or tactile hallucinations. Use the CIWA-Ar scale. Screen with CAGE or AUDIT."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: withdrawal and its dangers (seizures, DTs, injury). Then fluids and nutrition, then suicide risk (depression plus alcohol), then denial, coping and the family."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Trend the CIWA-Ar score and vital signs, look for seizures and confusion, check intake and electrolytes, and later assess readiness for change, AA attendance and relapse-prevention skills."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"8 hours after her last drink: tremor, sweating, BP up, then confusion and vomiting.","rule":"Priority: withdrawal and its dangers (seizures, DTs, injury). Then fluids and nutrition, then suicide risk (depression plus alcohol), then denial, coping and the family.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-alcohol","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Alcohol withdrawal and delirium tremens","notes":["c-alcohol"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 22; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Rising vital signs, tremor, vomiting and new confusion about 8 hours after stopping mean moderate to severe withdrawal progressing toward DTs, made riskier by older age, neuropathy (malnutrition) and depression. She is also at risk of seizures and Wernicke.","Priority: Priority: withdrawal and its dangers (seizures, DTs, injury). Then fluids and nutrition, then suicide risk (depression plus alcohol), then denial, coping and the family.","Act: Benzodiazepines per the CIWA-Ar protocol (chlordiazepoxide, lorazepam, diazepam), thiamine before glucose, fluids and nutrition, seizure precautions, a calm low-stimulation and well-lit room, frequent vital signs, reorientation, and remove alcohol-containing products (cough syrup, mouthwash). Then rehab, relapse prevention and AA / Al-Anon.","Reassess: Trend the CIWA-Ar score and vital signs, look for seizures and confusion, check intake and electrolytes, and later assess readiness for change, AA attendance and relapse-prevention skills."],"terms":["Withdrawal tremor and autonomic hyperactivity","Delirium tremens (DTs)","Wernicke encephalopathy","Tolerance","naltrexone","disulfiram","acamprosate","diazepam","lorazepam","psychosis"],"termDefinitions":{"Withdrawal tremor and autonomic hyperactivity":"Shaky hands, sweating, racing pulse, high BP, fever, anxiety, insomnia. Long-term alcohol boosts GABA (the brake) and suppresses glutamate; the brain adapts. When alcohol stops, the brake is gone and the system rebounds into overexcitation. Treat early with a benzodiazepine, which replaces the missing GABA effect. Rising vital signs mean withdrawal is getting worse.","Delirium tremens (DTs)":"Confusion, disorientation, agitation, visual and tactile hallucinations (bugs on the skin), fever, sweating, tachycardia, fluctuating consciousness. The most severe withdrawal state; more likely with medical illness (pneumonia, pancreatitis, liver disease). Medical emergency: up to 20% die untreated. IV lorazepam, fluids, a safe low-stimulation room, close observation, seizure precautions.","Wernicke encephalopathy":"Confusion, ataxic gait, nystagmus and eye-movement palsies. Thiamine (B1) deficiency from poor diet and poor absorption. Give thiamine before glucose (glucose uses up the last thiamine). Untreated, it becomes Korsakoff syndrome: permanent memory loss with confabulation.","Tolerance":"Blood alcohol 0.31 but alert and talking normally. The brain and liver adapt, so it takes more alcohol for the same effect. A sign of physical dependence, and a warning that withdrawal will be significant."},"patient":"61-year-old admitted for detox after a DUI (court’s choice: detox or jail). Heavy daily drinker for years. About 8 hours since her last drink: hand tremor, foot bouncing, sweating, BP 168/98, HR 112, temperature 38.1 °C, “pins and needles” in her legs. She vomits, becomes confused and grabs for her alcohol-containing cough syrup.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-alcohol","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Know the timeline: tremor (\"the shakes\") 6–8 hours after the last drink; hallucinations or psychosis from 8–10 hours; withdrawal seizures at 12–24 hours; delirium tremens (DTs) any time in the first 72 hours. Watch autonomic signs (↑ HR, BP and temperature, sweating), agitation, vomiting, confusion and visual or tactile hallucinations. Use the CIWA-Ar scale. Screen with CAGE or AUDIT."]},{"title":"Blood alcohol levels","lines":["0.08: impaired coordination and judgment (the legal limit in most states; 0.08–0.10).","0.15: vomiting, major loss of balance. 0.20: blackouts.","0.30: low temperature, BP and respirations; amnesia. 0.40: possible death.","Tolerant drinkers may look sober at high levels."]},{"title":"Screening","lines":["CAGE: Cut down? Annoyed by criticism? Guilty? Eye-opener in the morning? A score of 2 or more is significant.","AUDIT: 10 questions; 8 or more (men) or 7 or more (women) needs more assessment.","SBIRT: Screening, Brief Intervention, Referral to Treatment, in any setting."]},{"title":"Damage from long-term drinking","lines":["Liver: fatty liver → alcoholic hepatitis → cirrhosis (jaundice, ascites, bleeding).","GI: gastritis, esophagitis, esophageal varices, pancreatitis.","Heart: cardiomyopathy. Nerves: peripheral neuropathy (pins and needles). Muscles: myopathy.","Blood: low WBC and platelets (bleeding). Cancers of the mouth, throat, esophagus, liver, breast and colon.","Pregnancy: fetal alcohol syndrome (small head, facial features, intellectual disability). No safe amount."]}]},{"id":"c-alcohol","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Rising vital signs, tremor, vomiting and new confusion about 8 hours after stopping mean moderate to severe withdrawal progressing toward DTs, made riskier by older age, neuropathy (malnutrition) and depression. She is also at risk of seizures and Wernicke."]}]},{"id":"c-alcohol","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: withdrawal and its dangers (seizures, DTs, injury). Then fluids and nutrition, then suicide risk (depression plus alcohol), then denial, coping and the family."]}]},{"id":"c-alcohol","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: vital signs return to baseline, no seizures or DTs, no injury, she is hydrated and nourished, and then she engages in treatment (rehab, AA, medication-assisted treatment) and names her triggers and a relapse-prevention plan."]}]},{"id":"c-alcohol","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Benzodiazepines per the CIWA-Ar protocol (chlordiazepoxide, lorazepam, diazepam), thiamine before glucose, fluids and nutrition, seizure precautions, a calm low-stimulation and well-lit room, frequent vital signs, reorientation, and remove alcohol-containing products (cough syrup, mouthwash). Then rehab, relapse prevention and AA / Al-Anon."]},{"title":"Medications for alcohol use disorder","lines":["Disulfiram (Antabuse): drinking causes severe nausea and vomiting, flushing, sweating, headache, dyspnea and confusion. Avoid all alcohol, including cough syrup, mouthwash, sauces and some lotions.","Naltrexone (oral or monthly IM Vivitrol): reduces craving and the pleasure of drinking; must be opioid-free (about 7–10 days) first.","Acamprosate: reduces craving; start on day 5 of abstinence; avoid in kidney impairment.","Benzodiazepines for withdrawal; clonidine for mild to moderate withdrawal symptoms."]},{"title":"The continuum of care","lines":["Detox (medically managed) → rehab (residential) → halfway house → partial hospitalization (about 6 h/day, 5 days a week) → intensive outpatient (about 3 h/day, 3 days a week) → outpatient.","Mutual-help throughout: AA; Al-Anon (family), Alateen (teens).","Relapse is expected in a chronic disease: plan for triggers and coping.","Motivational interviewing (stages of change: precontemplation → contemplation → preparation → action → maintenance) and CBT."]},{"title":"Family and the nurse","lines":["Codependence: overly responsible behavior, self-worth from caring for the drinker; enabling.","Nurses are at higher risk themselves: a peer assistance (alternative to discipline) program; report a colleague’s suspected impairment to a supervisor.","Addiction is a chronic brain disease, not a lack of character."]}]},{"id":"c-alcohol","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Trend the CIWA-Ar score and vital signs, look for seizures and confusion, check intake and electrolytes, and later assess readiness for change, AA attendance and relapse-prevention skills."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"c-alcohol:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A patient is admitted for inpatient alcohol detoxification because of:","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Heavy use of a substance known to cause withdrawal","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Detox is medically needed when stopping a substance with dangerous withdrawal; withdrawal is CNS hyperactivity."},{"id":"o1","text":"A need for rehabilitation","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Detox is medically needed when stopping a substance with dangerous withdrawal; withdrawal is CNS hyperactivity."},{"id":"o2","text":"The potential for relapse","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Detox is medically needed when stopping a substance with dangerous withdrawal; withdrawal is CNS hyperactivity."},{"id":"o3","text":"CNS hypoactivity after stopping","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Detox is medically needed when stopping a substance with dangerous withdrawal; withdrawal is CNS hyperactivity."}]},{"id":"c-alcohol:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"61-year-old admitted for detox after a DUI (court’s choice: detox or jail). Heavy daily drinker for years. About 8 hours since her last drink: hand tremor, foot bouncing, sweating, BP 168/98, HR 112, temperature 38.1 °C, “pins and needles” in her legs. She vomits, becomes confused and grabs for her alcohol-containing cough syrup.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: withdrawal and its dangers (seizures, DTs, injury). Then fluids and nutrition, then suicide risk (depression plus alcohol), then denial, coping and the family.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-alcohol:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"61-year-old admitted for detox after a DUI (court’s choice: detox or jail). Heavy daily drinker for years. About 8 hours since her last drink: hand tremor, foot bouncing, sweating, BP 168/98, HR 112, temperature 38.1 °C, “pins and needles” in her legs. She vomits, becomes confused and grabs for her alcohol-containing cough syrup.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Benzodiazepines per the CIWA-Ar protocol (chlordiazepoxide, lorazepam, diazepam), thiamine before glucose, fluids and nutrition, seizure precautions, a calm low-stimulation and well-lit room, frequent vital signs, reorientation, and remove alcohol-containing products (cough syrup, mouthwash). Then rehab, relapse prevention and AA / Al-Anon. Trend the CIWA-Ar score and vital signs, look for seizures and confusion, check intake and electrolytes, and later assess readiness for change, AA attendance and relapse-prevention skills.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"From cues to a safer next action","cards":[{"title":"Recognize","text":"Intoxication or overdose: pinpoint pupils (miosis), sedation and head-nodding, slurred speech, respiratory depression, low BP, HR and temperature, decreased bowel sounds, track marks. The overdose triad: coma + pinpoint pupils + respiratory depression. Withdrawal is the opposite picture: dilated pupils, yawning, tearing, runny nose, gooseflesh (piloerection), sweating, ↑ HR and BP, cramps, vomiting, diarrhea, aches, anxiety, insomnia."},{"title":"Explain the priority","text":"Priority: airway and breathing (overdose), then re-sedation risk, then withdrawal comfort, then engagement in treatment and harm reduction."},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Continuous RR and SpO₂ until naloxone’s effect has clearly outlasted the opioid; a withdrawal score (e.g., COWS); and engagement in treatment (accepted buprenorphine or methadone, follow-up booked, naloxone kit given)."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s actual findings rather than the diagnosis label alone. See the highlighted clinical update below."},"strategy":{"title":"Recognize → prioritize → act → reassess","task":"Choose the next response for this patient.","cue":"Found unresponsive: RR 6, pinpoint pupils. After naloxone: sweating, yawning, cramps.","rule":"Priority: airway and breathing (overdose), then re-sedation risk, then withdrawal comfort, then engagement in treatment and harm reduction.","trap":"Do not delay an urgent protective action while choosing a routine teaching or comfort intervention."},"id":"c-opioid","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Opioid overdose and withdrawal","notes":["c-opioid"],"book":"Varcarolis 9e chapter 22; existing site-authored clinical reasoning case and question bank.","why":["Mechanism: Classic opioid overdose, reversed by naloxone, which pushed him into precipitated withdrawal. Naloxone is short-acting, so the opioid (especially fentanyl or methadone) can outlast it, and he can stop breathing again.","Priority: Priority: airway and breathing (overdose), then re-sedation risk, then withdrawal comfort, then engagement in treatment and harm reduction.","Act: Airway and ventilation, naloxone (IN, IM, SubQ or IV; repeat as needed, since it is short-acting), continuous monitoring. For withdrawal: clonidine or lofexidine, then buprenorphine (only once in early withdrawal, 12–24 hours after the last opioid) or methadone; naltrexone after detox. Manage withdrawal well early on (it keeps people in treatment) and refer to NA.","Reassess: Continuous RR and SpO₂ until naloxone’s effect has clearly outlasted the opioid; a withdrawal score (e.g., COWS); and engagement in treatment (accepted buprenorphine or methadone, follow-up booked, naloxone kit given).","For suspected opioid overdose, activate emergency help, support breathing and give naloxone under the product directions/protocol. Continue observation and repeat dosing as indicated: respiratory depression can return after naloxone wears off."],"terms":["Respiratory depression","Withdrawal (cold turkey)","naloxone","naltrexone","buprenorphine","methadone","methylphenidate","lorazepam","alprazolam"],"termDefinitions":{"Respiratory depression":"RR 6, shallow, SpO₂ dropping, cyanosis. Opioids act on mu receptors in the brainstem’s breathing center, making it less responsive to CO₂. The cause of death in overdose: airway, breathing (ventilate), naloxone.","Withdrawal (cold turkey)":"Gooseflesh, yawning, tears, runny nose, dilated pupils, cramps, diarrhea, sweating. Rebound of the systems that opioids suppressed (sympathetic overactivity). Very uncomfortable but rarely life-threatening (unlike alcohol or benzodiazepine withdrawal). Starts 6–8 hours after heroin; peaks on days 2–3."},"patient":"23-year-old found unresponsive in a bathroom. RR 6 and shallow, SpO₂ 82%, pinpoint pupils, BP 88/50, HR 54, fresh injection marks. After intranasal naloxone he wakes, angry, sweating, yawning, with runny nose and eyes, gooseflesh, cramps and vomiting.","sourceSections":[{"id":"c-opioid","title":"Recognize cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Intoxication or overdose: pinpoint pupils (miosis), sedation and head-nodding, slurred speech, respiratory depression, low BP, HR and temperature, decreased bowel sounds, track marks. The overdose triad: coma + pinpoint pupils + respiratory depression. Withdrawal is the opposite picture: dilated pupils, yawning, tearing, runny nose, gooseflesh (piloerection), sweating, ↑ HR and BP, cramps, vomiting, diarrhea, aches, anxiety, insomnia."]},{"title":"DEA schedules","lines":["I: no accepted medical use, high abuse potential (heroin, LSD; marijuana federally).","II: high abuse potential, prescription only (methadone, oxycodone, methylphenidate).","III: moderate (buprenorphine, acetaminophen-codeine, testosterone).","IV: low (alprazolam, lorazepam).","V: lowest (cough syrups with codeine, Lomotil, pregabalin).","The lower the number, the higher the misuse potential."]}]},{"id":"c-opioid","title":"Analyze cues","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Classic opioid overdose, reversed by naloxone, which pushed him into precipitated withdrawal. Naloxone is short-acting, so the opioid (especially fentanyl or methadone) can outlast it, and he can stop breathing again."]}]},{"id":"c-opioid","title":"Prioritize","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Priority: airway and breathing (overdose), then re-sedation risk, then withdrawal comfort, then engagement in treatment and harm reduction."]}]},{"id":"c-opioid","title":"Goals","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Outcomes: a stable respiratory rate and SpO₂ through the observation period; withdrawal symptoms managed; he accepts a referral for medication-assisted treatment; and he leaves with naloxone and an overdose plan."]}]},{"id":"c-opioid","title":"Take action","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Airway and ventilation, naloxone (IN, IM, SubQ or IV; repeat as needed, since it is short-acting), continuous monitoring. For withdrawal: clonidine or lofexidine, then buprenorphine (only once in early withdrawal, 12–24 hours after the last opioid) or methadone; naltrexone after detox. Manage withdrawal well early on (it keeps people in treatment) and refer to NA."]},{"title":"Opioid treatment medications","lines":["Naloxone (Narcan): antagonist; reverses overdose in minutes; short duration: re-dose and watch. Too much → withdrawal.","Methadone: long-acting agonist; once daily, only through certified programs; watch for respiratory depression, QT, chest pain; safest choice in pregnancy.","Buprenorphine (Subutex) / buprenorphine-naloxone (Suboxone): partial agonist, ceiling on respiratory depression; give only in early withdrawal, or it precipitates withdrawal; monthly injection and 6-month implant forms exist.","Naltrexone (oral, or monthly Vivitrol IM): antagonist, blocks euphoria; must be opioid-free first; injection-site reactions.","Clonidine and lofexidine (alpha-2 agonists): ease sweating, tearing and restlessness during withdrawal."]}]},{"id":"c-opioid","title":"Evaluate","blocks":[{"title":"Reasoning","lines":["Continuous RR and SpO₂ until naloxone’s effect has clearly outlasted the opioid; a withdrawal score (e.g., COWS); and engagement in treatment (accepted buprenorphine or methadone, follow-up booked, naloxone kit given)."]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"For suspected opioid overdose, activate emergency help, support breathing and give naloxone under the product directions/protocol. Continue observation and repeat dosing as indicated: respiratory depression can return after naloxone wears off.","sources":[{"title":"FDA: naloxone, emergency help and continued observation","url":"https://www.fda.gov/consumers/consumer-updates/access-naloxone-can-save-life-during-opioid-overdose"}],"steps":[{"id":"c-opioid:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Early in rehab for opioid use disorder, which nursing intervention best supports the patient’s success?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Identify the exact task, relevant findings and the safety constraint before comparing the choices.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Restrict visitors to family","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Untreated withdrawal drives people back to using; good withdrawal management keeps them in treatment."},{"id":"o1","text":"Manage withdrawal symptoms well","correct":true,"why":"Supported. Untreated withdrawal drives people back to using; good withdrawal management keeps them in treatment."},{"id":"o2","text":"Provide a low-stimulus environment","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Untreated withdrawal drives people back to using; good withdrawal management keeps them in treatment."},{"id":"o3","text":"Advocate for 3 months of treatment","correct":false,"why":"Not supported for this question. Untreated withdrawal drives people back to using; good withdrawal management keeps them in treatment."}]},{"id":"c-opioid:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"23-year-old found unresponsive in a bathroom. RR 6 and shallow, SpO₂ 82%, pinpoint pupils, BP 88/50, HR 54, fresh injection marks. After intranasal naloxone he wakes, angry, sweating, yawning, with runny nose and eyes, gooseflesh, cramps and vomiting.","task":"Which findings change the priority, and why?","answer":"Priority: airway and breathing (overdose), then re-sedation risk, then withdrawal comfort, then engagement in treatment and harm reduction.","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"c-opioid:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"23-year-old found unresponsive in a bathroom. RR 6 and shallow, SpO₂ 82%, pinpoint pupils, BP 88/50, HR 54, fresh injection marks. After intranasal naloxone he wakes, angry, sweating, yawning, with runny nose and eyes, gooseflesh, cramps and vomiting.","task":"What will you do, and how will you know it worked?","answer":"Airway and ventilation, naloxone (IN, IM, SubQ or IV; repeat as needed, since it is short-acting), continuous monitoring. For withdrawal: clonidine or lofexidine, then buprenorphine (only once in early withdrawal, 12–24 hours after the last opioid) or methadone; naltrexone after detox. Manage withdrawal well early on (it keeps people in treatment) and refer to NA. Continuous RR and SpO₂ until naloxone’s effect has clearly outlasted the opioid; a withdrawal score (e.g., COWS); and engagement in treatment (accepted buprenorphine or methadone, follow-up booked, naloxone kit given).","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""}]}};
